nemsha Inn and Cottages but also other establishments such as the Beach Plum Inn and Restaurant, and the Home Port Restaurant.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The Menemsha Inn is part of a collection of properties owned by Robert Nixon and Sarah Thorsby Guinan in Martha's Vineyard. The property includes the Menemsha Inn and Cottages, as well as additional establishments like the Beach Plum Inn and Restaurant, and the Home Port Restaurant."}, {"heading": "", "body": "While specific details about the history of the Menemsha Inn are not provided in the sources, it is known that Robert Nixon and his wife Sarah Thorsby Guinan own these properties. Nixon is a renowned filmmaker and conservationist who has been involved in various environmental projects."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Menemsha Inn is associated with Robert Nixon's personal life and his family. It serves as a part of their residence and business interests on Martha's Vineyard, alongside other establishments they own."}], "facts": [{"label": "Owner", "value": "Robert Nixon and Sarah Thorsby Guinan"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "Martha's Vineyard"}, {"label": "Properties Included", "value": "Menemsha Inn and Cottages, Beach Plum Inn and Restaurant, Home Port Restaurant"}, {"label": "Robert Nixon's Profession", "value": "Film director, writer, conservationist"}, {"label": "Nixon's Notable Films", "value": "Gorillas in the Mist, Amazon Diary, Mission Blue"}, {"label": "Conservation Work", "value": "Earth Conservation Corps, Wings Over America"}, {"label": "Martha's Vineyard Significance", "value": "Tourism and residential area with various establishments"}, {"label": "Nixon's Personal Life", "value": "Involvement in projects like American Heroes Fishing Challenge"}], "secs": 55.0}}}, {"subject": "toyota 4runner", "slug": "toyota-4runner", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "The Toyota 4Runner is an SUV manufactured by the Japanese automaker Toyota and marketed globally since 1984 across six generations. Originally a compact SUV based on the Toyota Hilux pickup truck with a fiberglass shell over the bed, it has evolved into a cross between a compact and mid-size SUV. All 4Runners have been built in Japan at Hino Motors' Hamura plant or Toyota's Tahara plant in Aichi.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The name \"4Runner\" was created by copywriter Robert Nathan of the Saatchi & Saatchi advertising company as a play on the word \"forerunner\". According to Toyota, the \"4\" represents the vehicle's 4-wheel drive system and \"Runner\" refers to its all-terrain capabilities. As of 2021, the 4Runner is sold primarily in North and South America. In markets such as Asia, Europe, and the Middle East where it is not offered, the Land Cruiser Prado is often provided instead. For some markets, the 4Runner was branded as the Hilux Surf, which was later replaced by the Fortuner."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Before the official launch of the 4Runner, Toyota partnered with third-party manufacturers for recreational vehicle conversions. Between 1981 and 1983, Winnebago Industries produced the Trekker, a \"walk-through\" conversion of the short-bed Toyota Hilux truck. The Trekker involved removing the rear wall of the cab and replacing the bed with a fiberglass tub and canopy. These third-party conversions served as marketing test vehicles for the 4Runner. Once the 4Runner was began production in 1984, the Trekker was discontinued."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The first generation N60 series 4Runner (and Hilux Surf) introduced in 1983 was a modification of the existing short-bed pickup body Hilux. Toyota borrowed concepts from the Chevrolet K5 Blazer and second generation Ford Bronco, borrowing the removal of the rear wall behind the front seats to add rear seats and a removable fiberglass canopy. Mechanically, the first generation was nearly identical to the Toyota Hilux 4x4. All first generation models had two doors."}, {"heading": "", "body": "A 2019 study by iSeeCars.com ranking the longest-lasting vehicles in the US found that the 4Runner placed number five. The study indicated that 3.94% of 4Runners had a mileage of over 200,000 miles (320,000 km)."}], "facts": [{"label": "Manufacturer", "value": "Toyota"}, {"label": "Production Start Date", "value": "1984"}, {"label": "Nombre in Japan", "value": "Toyota Hilux Surf"}, {"label": "label", "value": "Robert Nathan"}, {"label": "Production Plants", "value": "Tahara, Aichi and Hamura (Hino Motors)"}, {"label": "Primary Markets", "value": "North America and South America"}, {"label": "label", "value": "Robert Nathan"}], "secs": 77.6}, "ornith": {"lede": "The Toyota 4Runner is a four-wheel-drive SUV manufactured by the Japanese automaker Toyota and marketed globally since 1984. Originally derived from the Toyota Hilux pickup truck with an added fiberglass shell, it has evolved into a standalone vehicle positioned between compact and mid-size SUVs. The name \"4Runner\" was created as a play on \"forerunner,\" referencing its four-wheel drive system and all-terrain capabilities.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The 4Runner evolved from third-party recreational vehicle conversions of the Toyota Hilux. In 1981, Winnebago Industries produced a conversion called the Trekker, which removed the rear wall of the short-bed Hilux to add a bench seat and fiberglass shell. Approximately 1,200 to 1,500 Trekkers were built before being discontinued in 1984 when Toyota began producing the 4Runner. The first-generation 4Runner (N60), introduced in 1983, was mechanically nearly identical to the Hilux 4x4, featuring a removable fiberglass canopy and rear seats added behind the B-pillar."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The name \"4Runner\" was coined by copywriter Robert Nathan for Saatchi & Saatchi advertising. Toyota states that the \"4\" refers to the four-wheel drive system, while \"Runner\" highlights its all-terrain capabilities. As of 2021, the 4Runner is sold primarily in North and South America. In markets where it is not offered, such as Europe and Asia, consumers receive the Land Cruiser Prado instead."}, {"heading": "", "body": "All 4Runners are built in Japan at Toyota's Tahara plant or the Hino Motors plant in Hamura. In a 2019 study by iSeeCars.com ranking the longest-lasting vehicles in the US, the 4Runner placed fifth, with 3.94% of units reaching over 200,000 miles."}], "facts": [{"label": "First Generation", "value": "1983 (N60 series)"}, {"label": "Manufacturing Plants", "value": "Tahara, Aichi and Hamura, Japan"}, {"label": "Primary Markets", "value": "North America and South America"}, {"label": "Name Origin", "value": "Created by Robert Nathan for Saatchi & Saatchi"}, {"label": "Reliability Ranking", "value": "Fifth in 2019 iSeeCars.com study"}], "secs": 25.6}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "The Toyota 4Runner is an SUV manufactured by the Japanese automaker Toyota and has been marketed globally since 1984 across six generations. Originally, it was a compact SUV that resembled a Toyota Hilux pickup truck with a fiberglass shell over the bed. The name '4Runner' was created as a play on the term 'forerunner,' where the '4' indicated the vehicle's four-wheel drive system and 'Runner' referenced its all-terrain capabilities.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The 4Runner is sold primarily in North America and South America as of 2021. In markets that do not offer the 4Runner, such as Europe, Asia, and the Middle East, Toyota provides the Land Cruiser Prado instead. For some markets, the 4Runner was branded the Hilux Surf, which was later replaced by the Fortuner, a lower-cost alternative based on the Hilux platform."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The original 4Runner concept drew inspiration from vehicles like the second generation Ford Bronco and the Chevrolet K5 Blazer. The first generation (N60; 1983) was developed by modifying an existing short-bed Toyota Hilux, adding removable fiberglass shells over the rear sections and bench seats. This modification process allowed the vehicle to be more than simply a conventional pickup truck with a shell added."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The concept of third-party recreational vehicle conversions predates the 4Runner; for example, in 1981\u20131983, Winnebago Industries built the Trekker conversion on the short-bed Toyota Hilux. The discontinuation of the Trekker occurred when Toyota began producing and selling the 4Runner SUV, suggesting that these third-party conversions served as marketing test vehicles."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The 4Runner has demonstrated longevity in use; a 2019 study by iSeeCars.com found that 3.94% of 4Runners had accumulated mileage exceeding 200,000 miles (320,000 km). The vehicle's name was created by copywriter Robert Nathan for the Saatchi & Saatchi advertising company."}], "facts": [{"label": "Global Market Availability", "value": "Sold primarily in North America and South America as of 2021."}, {"label": "Alternative Markets", "value": "Land Cruiser Prado is offered instead in markets like Europe, Asia, and the Middle East."}, {"label": "Original Name Origin", "value": "The '4' described the vehicle's four-wheel drive system; 'Runner' referenced its all-terrain capabilities."}, {"label": "First Generation Model Year", "value": "N60 series introduced in 1983."}, {"label": "Predecessor Conversion Example", "value": "The Trekker (1981\u20131983) was a third-party conversion built on the short-bed Toyota Hilux."}], "secs": 21.4}, "mistral": {"lede": "The Toyota 4Runner is a compact SUV manufactured by Toyota since 1984, marketed globally across six generations. It was initially based on the Hilux pickup truck but has since undergone significant independent development.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The 4Runner's predecessor, the Trekker, was a third-party conversion of the Toyota Hilux built by Winnebago Industries from 1981 to 1983. The first generation 4Runner (N60) was introduced in 1983, based on the Hilux pickup truck with a removable fiberglass canopy and rear seats added."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The 4Runner has evolved from being nearly mechanically identical to the Toyota Hilux to becoming a standalone SUV. The second generation (N10) introduced in 1968 was based on the Hilux pickup truck, while the sixth generation (N250) is a purpose-built SUV."}], "facts": [{"label": "First introduced", "value": "1984"}, {"label": "Based on", "value": "Toyota Hilux pickup truck (initially)"}, {"label": "Generations", "value": "Six"}, {"label": "Sold primarily in", "value": "North America and South America"}], "secs": 18.6}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['The Toyota 4Runner is an SUV manufactured by Toyota since 1984, available in six generations.', 'Initially based on the Hilux pickup truck, it has evolved into a versatile vehicle with significant independent development.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The Toyota 4Runner is an SUV that was initially developed as a modified version of the short-bed Toyota Hilux pickup truck. It has undergone significant development over six generations, evolving from a compact SUV to a vehicle that blends features of both compact and mid-size SUVs."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The 4Runner's history traces back to the Toyota Trekker, a third-party conversion of the Hilux truck in the early 1980s. The first generation of the 4Runner was introduced in 1983 as a modified version of the Hilux, and it has since been developed independently by Toyota."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The 4Runner is primarily sold in North America and South America. In other markets like Europe, Asia, and the Middle East, similar vehicles such as the Land Cruiser Prado or Fortuner are offered instead. The vehicle has been marketed under different names, including Hilux Surf in Japan."}], "facts": [{"label": "Introduction Year", "value": "1984"}, {"label": "Generations", "value": "Six"}, {"label": "Original Base Vehicle", "value": "Toyota Hilux"}, {"label": "Primary Markets", "value": "North America and South America"}, {"label": "Alternative Models in Other Regions", "value": "Land Cruiser Prado, Fortuner"}, {"label": "Japanese Market Name", "value": "Hilux Surf (1984-2009)"}, {"label": "Production Location", "value": "Toyota's plant in Tahara, Aichi, or Hino Motors' plant in Hamura"}, {"label": "Longevity Ranking", "value": "Number five in a 2019 study by iSeeCars.com for longest-lasting vehicles in the US"}], "secs": 64.5}}}, {"subject": "madlib", "slug": "madlib", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Mad Libs is a phrasal template word game created by Leonard Stern and Roger Price. Its signature as a producer of comedic content is the use of blanks in stories that are filled with user-provided words from specific categories to create comedic sentences.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The core technique of Mad Libs is the phrasal template. This involves creating short stories where key words are- replaced with blanks, and each blank is specified by a category such as \"noun\", \"verb\", \"number\", \"celebrity\", \"exclamation\", or \"part of the body\". A player prompts others for words fitting these categories without revealing the context of the story. Once completed, the story is read aloud, typically resulting in comedic sentences."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The first book of Mad Libs was released in 1958 by Leonard Stern and Roger Price. This record is canonical because it resembled earlier games like 'consequences' and 'exquisite corpse'. The series eventually grew to include more than 70 editions of the latter, and the publishing firm Price Stern Sloan founded by the creators, publishing over 150 softcover books including titles such as How to Be a Jewish Mother (1964)."}], "facts": [], "secs": 46.4}, "ornith": {"lede": "", "sections": [], "facts": [], "secs": 19.5}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "The provided sources do not profile a music producer or musician. The subject, Mad Libs, is a word game and publishing concept.", "sections": [], "facts": [], "secs": 9.5}, "mistral": {"lede": "Mad Libs is a classic word game created by Leonard Stern and Roger Price in the United States, selling over 110 million copies since its first publication in 1958. It's renowned for its comedic phrasal template format.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Mad Libs is a game where one player prompts others for words to substitute for blanks in a story before reading aloud, resulting in comedic sentences. The game's unique phrasal template format is its signature technique."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The first book of Mad Libs, published in 1958, is considered the game's landmark record. It popularized the game and led to over 70 editions being released under Price Stern Sloan."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Mad Libs was created by Leonard Stern and Roger Price. They partnered with Larry Sloan in the early 1960s to found Price Stern Sloan, which published over 70 editions of Mad Libs."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Mad Libs is considered canonical due to its immense popularity and influence on phrasal template games. It has sold over 110 million copies worldwide since its inception in 1958."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Created in the United States in 1953, first published in 1958"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Word game, phrasal template game"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Over 70 editions of Mad Libs books published since 1958"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Price Stern Sloan (founded by Leonard Stern, Roger Price, and Larry Sloan)"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Phrasal template format with blanks for words to be substituted"}], "secs": 21.4}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Mad Libs is a word game created by Leonard Stern and Roger Price, consisting of players substituting words into stories based on categories like noun or verb.', 'The game was first published in 1958 and has since sold over 110 million copies.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Mad Libs employs a unique format where stories contain blanks categorized by parts of speech, which players fill in to create humorous and often nonsensical narratives. The core technique relies on the juxtaposition of random words into structured sentences, leading to comedic outcomes."}, {"heading": "", "body": "While Mad Libs is not a musical production, its cultural impact has been significant enough to spawn various media adaptations including a Disney Channel game show in 1998-1999 and an off-Broadway stage adaptation titled 'Mad Libs Live!' in 2015."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Mad Libs was co-created by Leonard Stern and Roger Price, with Larry Sloan joining as a partner to publish the series. The game's lineage includes influences from earlier games like 'Consequences' and books such as 'Revelations About My Friends'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Mad Libs is considered canonical in its genre due to its innovative format, widespread popularity, and enduring cultural impact. It has influenced numerous media adaptations and remains a staple of wordplay games."}], "facts": [{"label": "Invention Year", "value": "1953"}, {"label": "First Publication Year", "value": "1958"}, {"label": "Creators", "value": "Leonard Stern, Roger Price"}, {"label": "Notable Collaborator", "value": "Larry Sloan"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Word substitution into categorized blanks in stories"}, {"label": "Cultural Impact", "value": "Over 110 million copies sold; numerous media adaptations"}, {"label": "Influences", "value": "'Consequences', 'Revelations About My Friends'"}, {"label": "Adaptations", "value": "Disney Channel game show (1998-1999), stage adaptation 'Mad Libs Live!'"}], "secs": 59.1}}}, {"subject": "younger brother", "slug": "younger-brother", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Younger Brother is an electronic duo formed in 2003 by Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan. The term also refers to a Japanese manga series titled Please Excuse My Younger Brothers and a 1949 American Western film called The Younger Brothers.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Younger Brother is an electronic duo consisting of Simon Posford (Shpongle, Hallucinogen) and Benji Vaughan (Prometheus). Formed in 2003 after making a remix for a charity record, the duo's name was taken from a Kogi prophecy regarding Westerners who would destroy the Earth and environment. Their debut album A Flock of Bleeps was released in 2003 on Twisted Records and became a cult classic in the underground electronica scene. Following this, they released The Last Days of Gravity in 2007, which featured vocals by Ruu Campbell and artwork by Storm Thorgerson. Their third album Vaccine was released on April 4, 2011, recorded with a U.S. band lineup including Marc Brownstein, Joe Russo, and Tom Hamilton. In 2014, they released an alternate album of remixes titled Vaccine Electronic as a free stream via a mobile app. They also produced 'Phoenix in Dynamite Sky' for the film Heartless in 2009."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Please Excuse My Younger Brothers (Uchi no Ot\u014dto-domo ga Sumimasen) is a sh\u014djo manga series written and illustrated by Akira Ozaki, which began serialization in January 2020 in Bessatsu Margaret. The plot follows Ito Narita, who moves due to her mother's remarriage and discovers she has four younger brothers: Gen, Raku, Sh\u016b, and Rui. A live-action film adaptation premiered in December 2024, directed by Koichiro Miki and produced by Shochiku. An anime television series produced by Lay-duce premiered on July 4, 2026, running for 24 episodes across two cours."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Younger Brothers is a 1949 American Western film directed by Edwin L. Marin. The story focuses on Cole, Jim, and Bob Younger, who travel to Cedar Creek, Minnesota, for a parole hearing in an attempt to reform from their outlaw ways. They are targeted by a detective named Ryckman, who goads the youngest brother, John, into a situation where a man is killed. After being forced into a bank robbery by Katie Shepherd, the Youngers are eventually cleared of wrongdoing and ride away free."}], "facts": [{"label": "Electronic Duo Members", "value": "Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan"}, {"label": "Younger Brother Albums", "value": "A Flock of Bleeps, The Last Days of Gravity, The Last Days of Gravity, Vaccine, Vaccine Electronic, Mutually Assured Distraction"}, {"label": "Manga Author", "value": "Akira Ozaki"}, {"label": "Manga Characters", "value": "Ito Narita and her four younger brothers: Gen, Raku, Sh\u016b, and Rui"}, {"label": "Western Film Director", "value": "value own's the 1949 film The Younger Brothers was directed by Edwin L. Marin"}, {"label": "Film Cast", "value": "Wayne Morris, Bruce Bennett, and Janis Paige"}], "secs": 48.5}, "ornith": {"lede": "\"Younger brother\" is a familial term referring to a male sibling who was born after another in the same family. The concept appears across various cultural domains, including music, literature, and film, where it often serves as a title or thematic element.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Younger Brother is an electronic duo formed in 2003 by Simon Posford (of Shpongle and Hallucinogen) and Benji Vaughan (of Prometheus). The band's name was taken from a Kogi prophecy, where the Kogi regarded themselves as the Older Brother and identified Westerners as Younger Brother, whom they believed would cause the destruction of the Earth. Their debut album, A Flock of Bleeps (2003), became a cult classic in the underground electronica scene. They followed this with The Last Days of Gravity (2007) and Vaccine (2011). In 2014, they released an alternate remix album titled Vaccine Electronic via a mobile app."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Please Excuse My Younger Brothers is a Japanese manga series written and illustrated by Akira Ozaki. It began serialization in Shueisha's sh\u014djo manga magazine Bessatsu Margaret in January 2020. The plot follows Ito Narita, who moves after her mother remarries and discovers she has four younger brothers: Gen, Raku, Sh\u016b, and Rui. A live-action film adaptation premiered in Japanese theaters in December 2024, followed by an anime television series produced by Lay-duce that premiered in July 2026."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Younger Brothers is a 1949 American Western film directed by Edwin L. Marin and starring Wayne Morris, Bruce Bennett, and Janis Paige. The plot centers on Cole, Jim, and Bob Younger, who ride to Cedar Creek, Minnesota, for a parole hearing in an attempt to reform from their outlaw ways. They face threats from a vengeful detective and a lawless band led by Katie Shepherd before ultimately being cleared of wrongdoing."}], "facts": [{"label": "Electronic Duo Formation", "value": "2003"}, {"label": "Manga Serialization Start", "value": "January 2020"}, {"label": "Anime Premiere Date", "value": "July 4, 2026"}, {"label": "Western Film Release Year", "value": "1949"}, {"label": "Manga Author", "value": "Akira Ozaki"}, {"label": "Electronic Duo Members", "value": "Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan"}], "secs": 10.7}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Younger Brother is an electronic duo formed in 2003 by Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan. Their debut album, A Flock of Bleeps, was released that same year. The group's name has origins in a Kogi prophecy.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Simon Posford (Shpongle, Hallucinogen) and Benji Vaughan (Prometheus) formed the band after making a remix for a charity record. The name 'Younger Brother' is derived from a Kogi prophecy; according to the Kogi, they regard themselves as the Older Brother, and prophesy that Westerners will be called Younger Brother when Earth and environment are destroyed. Fragments of this prophecy appear in the song \"Evil and Harm.\""}, {"heading": "", "body": "The duo's first album, A Flock of Bleeps (2003), became a cult classic in the underground electronica scene, featuring tracks like \"Crumblenaut\" and \"Finger.\" Following this success, they released Last Days of Gravity in 2007, an album that moved toward a more organic sound. This album was inspired by their live shows and featured artwork by Storm Thorgerson. Their third album, Vaccine, followed in 2011, recorded with a U.S. band lineup. In 2014, they released the alternate remix album Vaccine Electronic as a free stream via an app."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The success of A Flock of Bleeps led to live shows for Younger Brother in locations ranging from London and Tokyo to New York and Moscow. For Last Days of Gravity, they played with a 'Younger Brother Live' band that included Marc Brownstein on bass, Tom Hamilton on guitar, and Joe Russo on drums in Japan, the US, and the UK. The group also produced \"Phoenix in Dynamite Sky\" for the British cult film Heartless in 2009."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The name 'Younger Brother' has been used in other media. In the realm of manga, Please Excuse My Younger Brothers is a series written and illustrated by Akira Ozaki, which began serialization in January 2020. Additionally, The Younger Brothers is the title of a 1949 American Western film directed by Edwin L. Marin."}], "facts": [{"label": "Formation", "value": "Formed in 2003 by Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan."}, {"label": "Debut Album", "value": "A Flock of Bleeps (2003)."}, {"label": "Name Origin", "value": "Taken from a Kogi prophecy regarding Westerners' role in environmental destruction."}, {"label": "Key Albums", "value": "The Last Days of Gravity (2007), Vaccine (2011), and Vaccine Electronic (2014)."}], "secs": 12.1}, "mistral": {"lede": "Younger Brother is an electronic duo formed in 2003 by Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan. They are known for their albums 'A Flock of Bleeps', 'Last Days of Gravity', and 'Vaccine'.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Younger Brother was formed in 2003 by Simon Posford (Shpongle, Hallucinogen) and Benji Vaughan (Prometheus). The band's name is taken from a Kogi prophecy that regards Westerners as Younger Brother."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Their debut album 'A Flock of Bleeps' was released in 2003, followed by 'The Last Days of Gravity' in 2007, which featured vocals by Ruu Campbell. Their third album 'Vaccine', recorded with a U.S. band lineup, became available on 4 April 2011."}, {"heading": "", "body": "'A Flock of Bleeps' led to demand for live shows, which they played from London to Tokyo. They also produced 'Phoenix in Dynamite Sky' for the British cult film 'Heartless'. In 2014, they released an alternate album of remixes titled 'Vaccine Electronic' as a free stream for users who installed their app."}], "facts": [{"label": "Formation Year", "value": "2003"}, {"label": "Founding Members", "value": "Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan"}, {"label": "First Album", "value": "'A Flock of Bleeps' (2003)"}, {"label": "Second Album", "value": "'The Last Days of Gravity' (2007)"}, {"label": "Third Album", "value": "'Vaccine' (2011)"}], "secs": 11.5}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Younger Brother is an electronic duo formed in 2003 by Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan, known for their albums like A Flock of Bleeps, The Last Days of Gravity, and Vaccine.', \"The band's name draws from a Kogi prophecy about Westerners called Younger Brother being responsible for the Earth's destruction.\"]", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Younger Brother is an electronic duo formed in 2003 by Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan. Their debut album, A Flock of Bleeps, became a cult classic in the underground electronica scene. They have since released several albums and EPs, evolving their sound to include more organic elements."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Simon Posford and Benji Vaughan formed Younger Brother after making a remix for a charity record. Their first album, A Flock of Bleeps, was released in 2003, leading to live performances around the world. They then moved towards a more organic sound with their second album, The Last Days of Gravity, which included vocals by Ruu Campbell and featured artwork by Storm Thorgerson."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Younger Brother's third studio album, Vaccine, was released in 2011 with a lineup that included Marc Brownstein, Joe Russo, and Tom Hamilton. In 2014, they developed an app for mobile devices featuring an alternate album of remixes titled Vaccine Electronic. Benji Vaughan announced he would take a break from the band in 2014."}], "facts": [{"label": "Formation Year", "value": "2003"}, {"label": "Members", "value": "Simon Posford, Benji Vaughan"}, {"label": "First Album", "value": "A Flock of Bleeps (2003)"}, {"label": "Second Album", "value": "The Last Days of Gravity (2007)"}, {"label": "Third Album", "value": "Vaccine (2011)"}, {"label": "App Release", "value": "Vaccine Electronic (2014)"}, {"label": "Artwork Artist", "value": "Storm Thorgerson"}, {"label": "Band Break", "value": "Benji Vaughan announced in 2014"}], "secs": 34.6}}}, {"subject": "shpongle", "slug": "shpongle", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Shpongle is an English psychedelic electronic music project formed in 1996. The group consists of Simon Posford and Raja Ram, who are considered progenitors of the psybient genre.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's music combines traditional world music and vocals with contemporary western synthesizer-based psychedelic music. The project is heavily influenced by psychedelic experiences, using sonic textures that approximate psychedelic states and vocal samples relating to altered states of awareness, hallucinations, and consciousness expansion. For example, the track \"A New Way to Say 'Hooray'\" features a vocal sample of Terence McKenna describing DMT effects. Raja Ram has described the term \"Shpongle\" as an umbrella term for feeling euphoric and positive emotions. Simon Posford is generally responsible for live instrumentation, studio work, and synthesizers, while Raja Ram contributes flute arrangements and broad musical concepts."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The duo's first track, \"Vapour Rumours\", was released in 1996 on the Infinite Excursions compilation by TIP Records. Their debut album, Are You Shpongled?, was released in 1998 via Twisted Records. Subsequent studio albums include Tales of the Inexpressible (2001), Nothing Lasts... But Nothing Is Lost (2005), Ineffable Mysteries from Shpongleland (2009), Museum of Consciousness (2013), and Codex VI (2017). They also released Improvisations for Piano & Flute as Simon Posford and Raja Ram in 2024. Additionally, the group has produced several EP albums such as Divine Moments of Truth (2000), Dorset Perception (2004), The God Particle (2010), and Carnival of Peculiarities (2021), along with remix albums including Remixed (2003) and Unreleased Remixes (2008)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's first live performance took place in July 2001 at the Solstice Music Festival in Japan. The group has performed various formats, including DJ sets by Simon Posford frequently accompanied by Raja Ram on flute. Full ensemble performances have occurred, as well as a tour featuring the 'Shpongletron', a custom-made projection surface and DJ booth designed by visual artist Zebbler. In 2011, they debuted the track \"Brain in a Fish Tank\" during their US tour. They performed with a full 12 piece band at Red Rocks Amphitheater in May 2014, and their last full public concerts were two consecutive nights at Red Rocks Amphitheatre on May 3 and 4, 2019."}], "facts": [{"label": "Formed", "value": "1996"}, {"label": " ", "value": ""}, {"label": "Genre", "value": "Psybient"}, {"label": "Members", "value": "Simon Posford and Raja Ram"}, {"label": "First Track", "value": "Vapour Rumours"}, {"label": "First Live Performance", "value": "July 2001, Solstice Music Festival, Japan"}, {"label": "Last Full Public Concerts", "value": "May 3-4, 2019, Red Rocks Amphitheatre"}], "secs": 51.5}, "ornith": {"lede": "Shpongle is a psychedelic electronic music project from England formed in 1996 by Simon Posford and Raja Ram. The duo are considered progenitors of the psybient genre, which combines world music with psychedelic trance and ambient sounds. Their musical style integrates traditional music and vocals from around the world with contemporary western synthesizer-based psychedelic music.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle formed in 1996, consisting of Simon Posford and Raja Ram, who is one of three members of The Infinity Project. Posford is generally responsible for synthesizers, studio work, and live instrumentation, while Raja Ram contributes broad musical concepts and flute arrangements. Raja Ram has stated that \"Shpongle\" is an umbrella term for feeling positive and euphoric emotions. Their music is heavily influenced by psychedelic experiences and frequently uses sonic textures that approximate psychedelic states, along with vocal samples relating to consciousness expansion and altered awareness. For example, the track \"A New Way to Say 'Hooray'\" contains a vocal sample of Terence McKenna describing the effects of DMT. Posford has described Shpongle's music as \"like nothing you've ever heard before.\""}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's first track, \"Vapour Rumours,\" was released on TIP Records' Infinite Excursions compilation in 1996. Their debut album, Are You Shpongled?, followed in 1998 on Twisted Records. The group has released six studio albums: Tales of the Inexpressible (2001), Nothing Lasts... But Nothing Is Lost (2005), Ineffable Mysteries from Shpongleland (2009), Museum of Consciousness (2013), Codex VI (2017), and Improvisations for Piano & Flute (2024). On 9 September 2014, Posford confirmed work on a sixth album, which was released as Codex VI on 23 October 2017. Even before this release, the duo announced they had begun working on their seventh studio album. Since June 2017, they have been releasing remasters of previous albums, focusing on special edition vinyl records."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's first live performance was at the Solstice Music Festival in Japan in July 2001. The band has toured extensively, including a sold-out concert at London's Roundhouse venue on 31 October 2008 with a full lineup featuring Andy Gangadeen (drums), Nogera (percussion), Chris Taylor (bass), Pete Callard (guitar), Harry Escott (cello), and Dick Trevor (keyboards). In January 2011, Shpongle toured Australia and played the Electric Forest Festival with the \"Shpongletron,\" a visual setup designed by artist Zebbler. On 10 May 2014, they performed at Red Rocks Amphitheater in Colorado with a full 12-piece band; this concert was filmed and released on DVD in October 2015. In September 2018, Shpongle announced their last full public concerts would be two consecutive nights at Red Rocks Amphitheatre on 3\u20134 May 2019, though they stated they would continue releasing studio recordings."}], "facts": [{"label": "Formed", "value": "1996"}, {"label": "Members", "value": "Simon Posford, Raja Ram"}, {"label": "Genre", "value": "Psybient (psychedelic electronic music combining world music, psychedelic trance, and ambient)"}, {"label": "Debut Album", "value": "Are You Shpongled? (1998)"}, {"label": "Record Labels", "value": "Twisted Records, TIP Records"}, {"label": "Notable Collaborators", "value": "Terence McKenna (vocal samples), Zebbler (visual artist)"}], "secs": 15.0}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Shpongle is a psychedelic electronic music project from England that formed in 1996. The group includes Simon Posford and Raja Ram, who are considered progenitors of the psybient genre. Their musical style combines traditional world music with contemporary western synthesizer-based psychedelic music.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's sound is heavily influenced by psychedelic experiences, frequently utilizing sonic textures that approximate altered states of awareness. The duo incorporates traditional music from around the world and vocals, with Posford describing the music as 'like nothing you've ever heard before.' Raja Ram stated that \"Shpongle\" itself is an umbrella term for feeling positive and euphoric emotions. For example, the track \"A New Way to Say 'Hooray'\" features a vocal sample from Terence McKenna discussing the effects of DMT."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The duo released their first track, \"Vapour Rumours,\" on TIP Records' Infinite Excursions compilation in 1996. Their debut album was *Are You Shpongled?* (1998), followed by *Tales of the Inexpressible* (2001). They continued to release albums such as *Nothing Lasts... But Nothing Is Lost* (2005) and *Museum of Consciousness* (2013). Posford is generally responsible for the synthesizers, studio work, and live instrumentation, while Raja Ram contributes broad musical concepts and flute arrangements."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle has performed numerous live concerts, with Simon Posford frequently touring performing DJ sets. Raja Ram occasionally accompanies these sets with live flute performances. Notable appearances include a sold-out concert at The Roundhouse venue in North London on 31 October 2008, and a full ensemble performance at Camp Bisco X in New York on 8 July 2011. In 2014, the group played with a full 12 piece band at Red Rocks Amphitheater in Colorado."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's discography includes studio albums such as *Are You Shpongled?* (1998), *Tales of the Inexpressible* (2001), and *Codex VI* (2017). They have also released EP albums, including *Divine Moments of Truth* (2000) and *Dorset Perception* (2004). The group's material is available across various formats, including remix albums and DVD releases."}], "facts": [{"label": "Formation", "value": "1996"}, {"label": "Members", "value": "Simon Posford and Raja Ram"}, {"label": "Genre Progenitors", "value": "Psybient genre (combining world music with psychedelic trance and ambient)"}, {"label": "Debut Album", "value": "*Are You Shpongled?* (1998)"}, {"label": "Key Influences", "value": "Psychedelic experiences, traditional global music, and consciousness expansion themes"}], "secs": 12.6}, "mistral": {"lede": "Shpongle is a psychedelic electronic music project from England, formed in 1996 by Simon Posford and Raja Ram. Known for their unique blend of world music, psychedelic trance, ambient, and synthesizer-based music, Shpongle has released numerous albums and performed live worldwide.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle was formed in 1996 by Simon Posford and Raja Ram. Their debut album 'Are You Shpongled?' was released in 1998 on Twisted Records. Since then, they have released six studio albums, including their latest 'Improvisations for Piano & Flute' in 2024."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's music is a unique blend of world music, psychedelic trance, ambient, and synthesizer-based music. Their style combines traditional music from various cultures with contemporary western electronic music elements. The duo often incorporates vocal samples relating to consciousness expansion, hallucinations, and altered states of awareness."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle has performed live concerts worldwide since 2001. Their first live performance was at the Solstice Music Festival in Japan. They have played with various lineups, including a full 12-piece band at Red Rocks Amphitheater in Morrison, Colorado."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founding Year", "value": "1996"}, {"label": "Members", "value": "Simon Posford and Raja Ram"}, {"label": "First Album", "value": "'Are You Shpongled?' (1998)"}, {"label": "Latest Album", "value": "'Improvisations for Piano & Flute' (2024)"}], "secs": 12.3}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Shpongle is a psychedelic electronic music project from England that formed in 1996.', 'Their music combines world music with psychedelic trance and ambient, often incorporating vocal samples related to consciousness expansion and altered states of awareness.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle is a psychedelic electronic music project from England that formed in 1996. The group includes Simon Posford and Raja Ram, who are considered pioneers of the psybient genre. Their music blends traditional world music with contemporary western synthesizer-based psychedelic sounds."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle's first track was released in 1996 on TIP Records' Infinite Excursions compilation, and their debut album, 'Are You Shpongled?', followed in 1998. Since then, they have released several studio albums, EPs, and remixes, with their most recent full-length album being 'Codex VI' in 2017."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shpongle has played numerous live concerts worldwide since their first performance at the Solstice Music Festival in Japan in 2001. Notable performances include headlining dates at festivals like Ozora and Rainbow Serpent, as well as sold-out shows in major cities such as New York City and Melbourne."}], "facts": [{"label": "Formation Year", "value": "1996"}, {"label": "Genre", "value": "Psybient"}, {"label": "First Album", "value": "Are You Shpongled? (1998)"}, {"label": "Latest Studio Album", "value": "Codex VI (2017)"}, {"label": "Members", "value": "Simon Posford, Raja Ram"}, {"label": "Notable Collaborations", "value": "Younger Brother, Hallucinogen, Joe Russo's Almost Dead"}, {"label": "Influences", "value": "Psychedelic experiences, world music, ambient sounds"}, {"label": "Record Label", "value": "Twisted Records (for most albums), TIP Records (for some releases)"}], "secs": 36.1}}}, {"subject": "me at the zoo", "slug": "me-at-the-zoo", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "A zoo, short for zoological garden, is a facility where animals are kept in enclosures for exhibition and often bred for conservation purposes. The term is derived from the Ancient Greek 'z\u014dion' meaning animal and '-logia' meaning study of. In the United States, zoos are visited by over 181 million people annually.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The abbreviation 'zoo' first appeared in print in the United Kingdom around 1847 for the Clifton Zoo, though it was not until approximately 20 years later that the the term became popular through Alfred Vance's rhyming song 'Walking in the Zoo'. The London Zoological Gardens, opened for scientific study in 1828 and to the public in 1847, was initially known as the 'Gardens and Menagerie of the Zoological Society of London'. Other terms such as 'zoological park' were used for facilities in Washington, D.C., Halifax, Nova Scotia, and the Bronx in New York. In the late 20th century, some professionals adopted names like 'bio park'\u2014a term coined by the National Zoo in Washington D.C. in the late 1980s\u2014or 'conservation park' to distance themselves from 19th-century stereotypes. The New York Zoological Society changed its name to the Wildlife Conservation Society in 1993 and rebranded its zoos as 'wildlife conservation parks'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The predecessor of the zoological garden is the menagerie. The oldest known zoological collection was discovered in 2009 during excavations at Hierakonpolis, Egypt, dating to approximately 3500 BCE and containing animals such as elephants, baboons, wildcats, hartebeest, and hippos. Other early collections were kept by King Ashur-bel-kala of the Middle Assyrian Empire in the 11th century BC, King Wen of Zhou in China, and various rulers including King Solomon, Queen Semiramis, and King Nebuchadnezzar. By the 4th century BC, zoos existed in most Greek city states; Roman emperors kept private collections for study or use in arenas where animals often fared poorly. In medieval England, King John I created a collection in the Tower of London as early as 1204, which was opened to the public in the 16th century and later moved to the London Zoo. Aztec emperor Moctezuma II also maintained a 'house of animals' in Tenochtitlan, which was destroyed by Hern\u00e1n Cort\u00e9s."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Sch\u00f6nbrunn Zoo in Vienna, Austria, constructed in 1752, is the oldest zoo still in existence. Other early facilities like those in Madrid (1775) and Paris (1795) were founded for scientific research and education. The transition from royal menageries designed to entertain high society into public zoological gardens occurred in the 19th century, with London and Paris serving as models. These modern zoos aimed to educate the general population using modern scientific lines and were often supported by local scientific or commercial societies. In the United Kingdom, the focus was on providing educational exhibits for entertainment and inspiration alongside scientific study."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Animal welfare organizations have criticized zoos and the conditions in which animals are kept. Concerns have been raised regarding visitor behavior and housing conditions and their impact on animal welfare. In recent decades, efforts have been made to improve these conditions."}], "facts": [{"label": "Oldest existing zoo", "value": "Sch\u00f6nbrunn Zoo in Vienna, Austria (1752)"}, {"label": " ", "value": ""}, {"label": "First modern zoo", "value": "Tierpark Hagenbeck by Carl Hagenbeck in Germany"}, {"label": "Annual US visitors", "value": "Over 181 million people"}, {"label": "Oldest known zoological collection", "value": "Hierakonpolis, Egypt (c. 3500 BCE)"}, {"label": "First zoo in Russia", "value": "Kazan Zoo (1806)"}, {"label": "Tower of London collection", "value": "Opened to the public in the 16th century"}], "secs": 66.5}, "ornith": {"lede": "A zoo, short for zoological garden, is a facility where animals are kept within enclosures for exhibition and often bred for conservation purposes. The term derives from the Ancient Greek words for animal and study of. The abbreviation 'zoo' was first used in reference to the London Zoological Gardens, which opened for scientific study in 1828.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The term zoological garden refers to zoology, the study of animals. The word is derived from the Ancient Greek \u03b6\u1ff7\u03bf\u03bd (z\u014dion), meaning 'animal', and the suffix -\u03bb\u03bf\u03b3\u03af\u03b1 (-logia), meaning 'study of'. The abbreviation 'zoo' first appeared in print in the United Kingdom around 1847 when it was used for the Clifton Zoo. It became popular approximately twenty years later through the rhyming song 'Walking in the Zoo' by music-hall artist Alfred Vance. Relatively new terms that emerged in the late 20th century include 'conservation park' and 'bio park', with the latter being first coined by the National Zoo in Washington D.C. in the late 1980s."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The predecessor of the zoological garden is the menagerie, which has a long history from ancient times to the modern era. The oldest known zoological collection was revealed during excavations at Hierakonpolis, Egypt in 2009, dating to approximately 3500 BCE. By the 19th century, major European cities established public zoological gardens, transitioning from princely menageries designed to entertain high society into facilities focused on educating the general population along modern scientific lines. The oldest zoo still in existence is the Sch\u00f6nbrunn Zoo in Vienna, Austria, constructed in 1752."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The first modern zoo was the Tierpark Hagenbeck by Carl Hagenbeck in Germany. In the United States alone, zoos are visited by over 181 million people annually. However, animal welfare organizations have expressed criticism of zoos and the conditions in which animals are kept, raising concerns about housing conditions and the effects of visitor behavior on animal welfare. In recent decades, efforts have been made to improve conditions in zoos, with some institutions adopting new names like 'wildlife conservation parks' to distance themselves from the criticized concept of 19th-century zoos."}], "facts": [{"label": "First use of abbreviation", "value": "The abbreviation 'zoo' was first used for the London Zoological Gardens, which opened for scientific study in 1828 and to the public in 1847."}, {"label": "Oldest existing zoo", "value": "The Sch\u00f6nbrunn Zoo in Vienna, Austria, constructed in 1752, is the oldest zoo still in existence."}, {"label": "First modern zoo", "value": "The Tierpark Hagenbeck by Carl Hagenbeck in Germany was the first modern zoo."}, {"label": "US zoo visits", "value": "In the United States alone, zoos are visited by over 181 million people annually."}, {"label": "Ancient origins", "value": "The oldest known zoological collection was revealed during excavations at Hierakonpolis, Egypt in 2009, dating to approximately 3500 BCE."}], "secs": 13.8}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "A zoo is a facility where animals are kept within enclosures for exhibition and often bred for conservation purposes. The term 'zoological garden' refers to zoology, the study of animals, and the abbreviation 'zoo' was first used in relation to the London Zoological Gardens. Historically, zoos have evolved from ancient menageries into modern scientific institutions.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The term for a zoo is derived from the Ancient Greek \u03b6\u1ff7\u03bf\u03bd, z\u014dion, meaning 'animal,' combined with the suffix -\u03bb\u03bf\u03b3\u03af\u03b1, '-logia,' meaning 'study of.' The abbreviation 'zoo' first appeared in print in the United Kingdom around 1847. While initially known as menageries or 'zoological forests,' newer terms such as 'conservation park' or 'bio park' have been adopted by some professionals to distance institutions from older, criticized concepts."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The concept of keeping exotic animals is ancient; the oldest known zoological collection dates back to c. 3500 BCE in Hierakonpolis, Egypt. Throughout history, zoos served various purposes\u2014from symbolizing royal power (such as King Louis XIV's menagerie at Versailles) to providing educational exhibits. The modern zoo emerged in the 19th century when major European cities began establishing public zoological gardens aimed at educating the general population using scientific methods."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Today, zoos are facilities that exhibit animals and often breed them for conservation purposes. Despite their role in conservation efforts, animal welfare organizations have expressed criticism regarding housing conditions and the impact of visitor behavior. In response to these concerns, efforts have been made over recent decades to improve conditions within zoological institutions."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Separately, 'morning zoo' is a format for English-language radio shows characterized by its spontaneous, playful, and irreverent interactions among hosts. This concept was first conceived in 1981 by Scott Shannon and Cleveland Wheeler at WRBQ-FM in Tampa, Florida, and quickly spread across the United States."}], "facts": [{"label": "Definition", "value": "A facility where animals are kept within enclosures for exhibition and often bred for conservation purposes."}, {"label": "Etymological Origin", "value": "Derived from Ancient Greek \u03b6\u1ff7\u03bf\u03bd ('animal') and -\u03bb\u03bf\u03b3\u03af\u03b1 ('study of')."}, {"label": "First Modern Zoo", "value": "The Tierpark Hagenbeck by Carl Hagenbeck in Germany."}, {"label": "Ancient Predecessor", "value": "The oldest known zoological collection dates to c. 3500 BCE at Hierakonpolis, Egypt."}], "secs": 12.0}, "mistral": {"lede": "A zoo, short for zoological garden, is a facility where animals are kept within enclosures for exhibition and often bred for conservation purposes. The term 'zoo' was first used of the London Zoological Gardens in 1847.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The predecessor of the zoological garden is the menagerie, which has a long history from the ancient world to modern times. The oldest known zoological collection was revealed during excavations at Hierakonpolis, Egypt in 2009... The modern zoo that emerged in the 19th century in the United Kingdom was focused on providing scientific study and later educational exhibits to the public for entertainment and inspiration."}], "facts": [{"label": "First Modern Zoo", "value": "The first modern zoo was the Tierpark Hagenbeck by Carl Hagenbeck in Germany."}, {"label": "Annual Visitors (US)", "value": "Over 181 million people annually visit zoos in the United States alone."}, {"label": "Oldest Zoo", "value": "The oldest zoo still in existence is the Sch\u00f6nbrunn Zoo in Vienna, Austria."}, {"label": "Zoo Name Origin", "value": "The term 'zoo' was first used of the London Zoological Gardens in 1847."}], "secs": 12.7}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['A zoo is a facility where animals are kept within enclosures for exhibition and often bred for conservation purposes.', \"The term 'zoo' originates from the Greek words meaning 'study of animals', and it has evolved significantly since its inception in ancient times.\"]", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Zoos are institutions that house a variety of animals for public display and conservation. They serve educational purposes, allowing visitors to learn about different species and their habitats. However, they have faced criticism regarding animal welfare concerns."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The concept of zoos dates back to ancient times with royal menageries in Egypt, Assyria, China, and Rome. The modern zoo emerged in the 19th century, evolving from private collections into public institutions focused on scientific study and education."}, {"heading": "", "body": "In recent decades, there has been increased scrutiny of zoos due to concerns about animal welfare. Efforts have been made to improve living conditions and promote conservation efforts within these facilities."}], "facts": [{"label": "First Modern Zoo", "value": "Tierpark Hagenbeck in Germany"}, {"label": "Annual Visitors (US)", "value": "Over 181 million people annually"}, {"label": "Oldest Existing Zoo", "value": "Sch\u00f6nbrunn Zoo in Vienna, Austria"}, {"label": "First Morning Zoo Show", "value": "Q Morning Zoo on WRBQ-FM in Tampa, Florida (1981)"}, {"label": "Zoo Etymology", "value": "Derived from Ancient Greek \u03b6\u1ff7\u03bf\u03bd (z\u014dion) 'animal' and -\u03bb\u03bf\u03b3\u03af\u03b1 (-logia) 'study of'"}, {"label": "First Use of 'Zoo'", "value": "London Zoological Gardens, 1847"}, {"label": "Morning Zoo Popularity", "value": "Adopted by numerous stations across the US and UK in the 1980s"}, {"label": "Zoo Rebranding", "value": "Some zoos rebranded as 'conservation parks' or 'bio parks'"}], "secs": 42.6}}}, {"subject": "30 for 30", "slug": "30-for-30", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "30 for 30 is the name of several distinct subjects, including an ESPN documentary series, a song by SZA and Kendrick Lamar, and a .30-30 Winchester cartridge. The ESPN series focuses on sports history, while the song is a collaboration between two American artists, and the cartridge is a sporting rifle ammunition type.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The 30 for 30 series was conceived in 2007 by Connor Schell and Bill Simmons to celebrate ESPN's 30th anniversary in 2009. It began with Volume I, which premiered in October 2009 and chronicled stories from the 'ESPN era' starting in 1979. The series has since expanded to include four volumes of 30 episodes each, a 13-episode series under ESPN Films Presents, 30 for 30 Shorts, audio podcasts, and Soccer Stories. The entire archive is available on ESPN+ as of April 2018. Executive producers in 2010 included John Dahl, Connor Schell, and Bill Simmons."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Released on January 7, 2025, as a single from SZA's reissue album Lana, \"30 for 30\" is a collaboration between singer-songwriter SZA and rapper Kendrick Lamar. The song features soaring strings and booming 808s, starting with a spoken-word sample from Bobby DeBarge of the band Switch. Critics have noted that SZA delivers a more confident rap-singing performance in this track. The song debuted at number one on the US Hot R&B Songs chart, breaking the record for most number-ones by a woman on that chart."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The .30-30 Winchester (also known as .30 WCF) is a small-bore sporting rifle cartridge designed for smokeless powder, first marketed in 1895 for the Winchester Model 1894 lever-action rifle. It is widely used for hunting big game in North America, including deer, elk, and moose, though it is not recommended for moose hunting by provincial authorities in Newfoundland, Canada. The cartridge is known for its reliable effectiveness in civilian applications and is commonly loaded with round-nose or flat-nose bullets to ensure safety in tubular magazines."}], "facts": [{"label": "ESPN Series Founders", "value": "Bill Simmons and Connor Schell"}, {"label": "SZA Song Chart Performance", "value": "Debuted atop the US Hot R&B Songs chart"}, {"label": "Cartridge Introduction Year", "value": "1895"}, {"label": "ESPN Series Accolades", "value": "2016 Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature (O.J.: Made in America)"}, {"label": "label la SZA and Lamar collaboration date", "value": "January 7, 2025"}, {"label": "Cartridge Primary Rifle", "value": "Winchester Model 1894 lever-action rifle"}], "secs": 53.9}, "ornith": {"lede": "30 for 30 is a series of documentary films airing on ESPN, its sister networks, and online that highlights interesting people and events in sports history. The concept originated in 2007 from Bill Simmons and Connor Schell as 30 films to celebrate ESPN's 30th anniversary in 2009. The franchise has expanded to include multiple volumes of episodes, shorts, a companion series on women's sports, and audio podcasts.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The idea for the series began in 2007 from ESPN.com columnist Bill Simmons and ESPN's Connor Schell. The title derived from the genesis of 30 films in celebration of ESPN's 30th anniversary in 2009, exploring the biggest stories from ESPN's first 30 years on-air through a series of one-hour films by 30 filmmakers. Volume I premiered in October 2009 and ran to December 2010, chronicling 30 stories from the \"ESPN era\" beginning with the network's founding in 1979."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The series includes four volumes of 30 episodes each, a 13-episode series under the ESPN Films Presents title in 2011\u20132012, and a series of shorts shown through the ESPN.com website. The franchise has expanded to include Soccer Stories, which aired ahead of the 2014 FIFA World Cup, as well as audio podcasts. In April 2018, it was announced that the entire archive would be available on ESPN+ upon its launch."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The series received a slow start with poor ratings for its first film but grew as awareness increased. It has won numerous awards, including a 2016 Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature for O.J.: Made in America, a 2014 Primetime Emmy Award, and multiple Peabody Awards. The series is sponsored by Cadillac and Levi's."}], "facts": [{"label": "Creator", "value": "Bill Simmons and Connor Schell"}, {"label": "Premiere Date", "value": "October 2009"}, {"label": "Network", "value": "ESPN"}, {"label": "Original Concept", "value": "30 films for ESPN's 30th anniversary"}, {"label": "Notable Award", "value": "2016 Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature (O.J.: Made in America)"}, {"label": "Presenting Sponsors", "value": "Cadillac and Levi's"}], "secs": 11.8}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "30 for 30 is the title of three distinct subjects covered in these sources: a documentary film series about sports, a popular song by SZA and Kendrick Lamar, and an early sporting cartridge designation. The documentary series highlights significant moments and figures in sports history, while the song was released as part of SZA's album *Lana*.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The 30 for 30 film series is a collection of documentaries airing on ESPN and its sister networks that highlight interesting people and events in sports history. The concept originated in 2007 from Bill Simmons, an ESPN.com columnist, and Connor Schell. Initially titled to celebrate ESPN's 30th anniversary in 2009, Volume I premiered in October 2009, chronicling 30 stories from the network\u2019s first three decades of existence. Subsequent volumes expanded the series beyond the 'ESPN era,' and it has also included formats like Soccer Stories and audio podcasts."}, {"heading": "", "body": "\"30 for 30\" is a song by SZA and Kendrick Lamar, released on January 7, 2025, as a single from SZA's first reissue album, *Lana*. The track was written by both artists, along with Bobby DeBarge, Greg Williams, Anthony Jermaine White, and Michael Uzowuru. It arrived shortly after the release of Lamar\u2019s sixth studio album, GNX, and features SZA delivering a confident rap-singing performance."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The .30-30 Winchester cartridge (also known as 7.62\u00d752mmR or .30 WCF) was first marketed in 1895 for the Winchester Model 1894 lever-action rifle. It was one of the United States' first small-bore sporting rifle cartridges designed for smokeless powder. The cartridge remains widely used due to its reliable effectiveness in civilian hunting applications, and it is noted for leaving lower volumes of spoiled venison after a kill."}], "facts": [{"label": "Documentary Series Scope", "value": "Highlights interesting people and events in sports history."}, {"label": "Song Release Date", "value": "January 7, 2025"}, {"label": "Cartridge Introduction Year", "value": "1895"}, {"label": "Sporting Cartridge Use", "value": "Commonly used on whitetail, mule deer, pronghorn, caribou, elk, moose, and black bear."}], "secs": 12.0}, "mistral": {"lede": "30 for 30 is a series of documentary films airing on ESPN, highlighting interesting people and events in sports history. It began as a celebration of ESPN's 30th anniversary but has since expanded beyond the 'ESPN era'.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The series consists of four volumes, each containing 30 episodes, along with additional specials and shorts. It was created by ESPN.com columnist Bill Simmons and Connor Schell in celebration of ESPN's 30th anniversary."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Volume I premiered in October 2009 and ran to December 2010, chronicling 30 stories from the 'ESPN era'. Subsequent films expanded the series beyond this timeframe. In September 2014, Schell stated that there was no shortage of incredible moments from the world of sports, enabling them to continue making quality films."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The series has received positive reviews and critical acclaim, winning awards such as the Peabody Award (2010), International Documentary Association's Distinguished Continuing Series (2010), Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Short-Format Nonfiction Program (2014), and Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature (2016) for O.J.: Made in America."}], "facts": [{"label": "Creator", "value": "Bill Simmons and Connor Schell"}, {"label": "Premiere Date", "value": "October 6, 2009"}, {"label": "Number of Episodes (Volumes I-IV)", "value": "120"}, {"label": "Awards Won", "value": "Peabody Award, International Documentary Association's Distinguished Continuing Series, Primetime Emmy Award, Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature"}], "secs": 14.7}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['30 for 30 is a series of documentary films airing on ESPN and its platforms, highlighting interesting people and events in sports history.', \"The series includes four volumes of 30 episodes each, an additional 13-episode series, and online shorts, expanding beyond the 'ESPN era' to cover broader sports history.\"]", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The idea for 30 for 30 originated in 2007 from ESPN.com columnist Bill Simmons and ESPN's Connor Schell. The series was initially conceived as a celebration of ESPN's 30th anniversary with 30 one-hour films by 30 filmmakers, exploring significant sports stories from the network's first three decades."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Volume I premiered in October 2009 and covered events from ESPN's founding in 1979. Subsequent volumes and online shorts expanded the series to include a wide range of sports stories beyond ESPN's history, including Soccer Stories ahead of the 2014 FIFA World Cup."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The series received mixed critical responses, with some episodes receiving high praise while others were less favorably reviewed. Despite a slow start in terms of ratings, awareness and critical acclaim led to increased viewership over time. The series has won several awards, including the 2016 Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature for 'O.J.: Made in America'."}], "facts": [{"label": "Series Origin", "value": "Conceived in 2007 by Bill Simmons and Connor Schell to celebrate ESPN's 30th anniversary."}, {"label": "Initial Volume Release", "value": "Volume I premiered in October 2009, covering events from ESPN's founding in 1979."}, {"label": "Number of Episodes", "value": "Includes four volumes of 30 episodes each, a 13-episode series, and online shorts."}, {"label": "Notable Awards", "value": "Won the 2016 Academy Award for Best Documentary Feature for 'O.J.: Made in America'."}, {"label": "Sponsors", "value": "Cadillac and Levi's are presenting sponsors of the series."}, {"label": "Platform Availability", "value": "Available on ESPN, its sister networks, and online platforms including ESPN+."}, {"label": "Series Expansion", "value": "Expanded to include Soccer Stories ahead of the 2014 FIFA World Cup and audio podcasts."}, {"label": "Executive Producers", "value": "John Dahl, Connor Schell, and Bill Simmons served as executive producers in 2010."}], "secs": 46.2}}}, {"subject": "music in film on screen", "slug": "music-in-film-on-screen", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Music in film is utilized to enhance dramatic narrative and emotional impact through original music specifically written to accompany a film or television program, known as a film score. While often orchestral, scores can be influenced by various genres such as jazz, rock, pop, and electronic music. Modern films may also utilize digital samples and music composition software to create these scores.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "A film score consists of a number of orchestral, instrumental, or choral pieces called cues that are timed to begin and end at specific moments. These are written by composers under the guidance of a director or producer and performed by an ensemble of musicians, such as a symphony orchestra, band, or choir. While most scores are rooted in Western classical music, they can incorporate elements of jazz, rock, pop, blues, new-age, ambient, and ethnic world music styles. Since the 1950s, electronic elements have been integrated into scores, often resulting in a hybrid of orchestral and electronic instruments. Pop and rock songs are generally not considered part of the score, though they are part of the soundtrack. Scores usually lack lyrics unless sung by choirs or soloists as part of a cue."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The creation of a film score is typically initiated during the editing phase of filming, where the composer and director watch a 'rough cut' of the film to identify scenes requiring music. This process, known as spotting, is used to determine the precise timing and timing notes for each cue. In some cases, filmmakers may edit the film to fit the flow of music rather than the composer editing the score to the score to the final cut; examples include Baby Driver, Koyaanisqatsi, and Powaqqatsi. Alternatively, some composers write music based on script impressions or storyboards without seeing the film itself, as seen with Hans Zimmer's work for Inception and Gustavo Santaolalla's Brokeback Mountain."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Syncing is the process of aligning dramatic events on screen with musical events in the score. Composers use SMPTE timecode for syncing purposes, and a leeway of 3-4 frames is generally considered sufficiently accurate. Methods for syncing include sequencing software, mathematical formulas, and free timing. In free timing, a conductor uses a stopwatch or watches the film while conducting musicians to predetermined timings represented by streamers (vertical lines) and punches (bursts of light). A written click track is another method used to establish constant tempo in lieu of a metronome value in beats per minute (BPM)."}], "facts": [{"label": "Common Terminology", "value": "Background score, background music, film soundtrack, original soundtrack (OST), film music, screen composition, screen music, or incidental music."}, {"label": "Performance Ensemble", "value": "Usually includes an orchestra (most likely a symphony orchestra) or band, instrumental soloists, and choir or vocalists known as playback singers."}, {"label": "Digital Technology Influence", "value": "The invention of digital technology and audio sampling has allowed modern films to rely on digital samples to imitate acoustic instruments."}, {"label": "Spotting Process", "value": "The process where the director and composer watch a rough cut of the film to note which scenes require original music and take precise timing notes."}, {"label": "Syncing Accuracy", "value": "value: \"suf\ufb01ciently accurate\" if it is 3-4 frames late or early"}, {"label": "Score vs Soundtrack", "value": "Pop and rock songs are usually not considered part of the film's score, though they do form part of the film's soundtrack."}], "secs": 60.3}, "ornith": {"lede": "Music in film encompasses original compositions, known as scores, alongside pre-existing songs that accompany motion pictures. A film score consists of orchestral, instrumental, or choral pieces called cues, timed to enhance the dramatic narrative and emotional impact of scenes. This music is typically written by composers under the guidance of a film's director or producer.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "A film score is original music specifically written to accompany a film or television program. It constitutes a number of orchestral, instrumental, or choral pieces called cues that are timed to begin and end at certain moments during the film. The term is less frequently applied to music for live theatre, radio programs, or video games, which are typically referred to as soundtracks or incidental music. Songs such as pop or rock songs are usually not considered part of a film's score, though they form part of the soundtrack; however, scores occasionally feature lyrics when sung by choirs or soloists as part of a cue."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Film scores encompass an enormous variety of styles depending on the nature of the films they accompany. While the majority are orchestral works rooted in Western classical music, many are influenced by jazz, rock, pop, blues, new-age, ambient music, and a wide range of ethnic and world music styles. Since the 1950s, scores have increasingly included electronic elements, with many modern scores featuring a hybrid of orchestral and electronic instruments. Digital technology has allowed some scores to rely on digital samples to imitate acoustic instruments or be created wholly by composers using composition software and synthesizers."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The composer usually enters the creative process towards the end of filming, watching an unpolished 'rough cut' with the director to identify scenes requiring original music. This process, known as spotting, involves taking precise timing notes for each cue. While scores are generally edited to fit the final film cut, some filmmakers edit their films to match pre-existing music; examples include Edgar Wright's Baby Driver and Steven Spielberg editing the finale of E.T. to John Williams' score. Composers sometimes write music based on scripts or storyboards without seeing the film, granting them more freedom regarding cue lengths."}, {"heading": "", "body": "A primary goal when writing for film is to sync dramatic events on screen with musical events in the score. Composers work using SMPTE timecode and may use sequencing software, mathematic formulas, or free timing techniques involving streamers and punches on the film. When conducting live performers, a written click track may be used to establish constant tempo without the stiffness of a metronome. Generally, a leeway of 3\u20134 frames late or early is considered sufficiently accurate for syncing music to picture."}], "facts": [{"label": "Alternative Names", "value": "Background score, background music, film soundtrack, original soundtrack (OST), film music, screen composition, screen music, or incidental music"}, {"label": "Primary Performers", "value": "An ensemble of musicians including an orchestra (usually symphony), band, instrumental soloists, and choir or vocalists known as playback singers"}, {"label": "Historical Shift", "value": "Since the 1950s, a growing number of scores have included electronic elements"}, {"label": "Syncing Accuracy", "value": "A leeway of 3\u20134 frames late or early is generally considered sufficiently accurate when syncing music to picture"}], "secs": 14.1}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "A film score is original music specifically composed to accompany a film or television program, enhancing the dramatic narrative and emotional impact of scenes. This music consists of cues\u2014orchestral, instrumental, or choral pieces\u2014that are timed to specific moments during the film's runtime. Scores can encompass diverse styles, ranging from Western classical music to jazz, rock, pop, blues, ambient, and various world music genres.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Film scores are written by composers under the guidance of or in collaboration with the film's director or producer. While many scores are orchestral works rooted in Western classical music, they can also incorporate influences from jazz, rock, pop, blues, new-age, ambient, and various ethnic styles. Since the 1950s, electronic elements have become more common, leading to modern scores often featuring a hybrid of orchestral and electronic instruments. Although songs like pop or rock tracks are usually considered part of the film's soundtrack rather than the score, some musicals may feature songs based on thematic ideas from the score."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The creation process involves several stages, beginning with 'spotting,' where the composer watches an unpolished 'rough cut' and discusses required styles and tones with the director. During spotting, precise timing notes are taken to determine how long each cue must be and when it should coincide with specific moments in a scene. Alternatively, composers may write music based only on scripts or storyboards without seeing the film itself. Another key process is 'syncing,' which involves aligning dramatic events on screen with musical events using methods like SMPTE timecode or free timing."}, {"heading": "", "body": "A film score is known by several names, including background score, background music, film soundtrack, original soundtrack (OST), film music, screen composition, and incidental music. The term 'soundtrack' is generally reserved for media like live theatre, television, radio programs, or video games; for these, the music is typically called the soundtrack or incidental music."}], "facts": [{"label": "Definition", "value": "A film score is original music written to accompany a film or TV program, consisting of timed cues that enhance narrative and emotional impact."}, {"label": "Styles", "value": "Scores can be influenced by jazz, rock, pop, blues, ambient, Western classical, and various world music styles."}, {"label": "Modern Scores", "value": "Many modern scores feature a hybrid of orchestral and electronic instruments due to advancements in digital technology and audio sampling."}, {"label": "Spotting Process", "value": "Composers watch 'rough cuts' during spotting, taking precise timing notes for when cues must begin and end."}], "secs": 13.7}, "mistral": {"lede": "Music plays a significant role in film, enhancing storytelling, evoking emotions, and creating atmosphere. This page explores music's impact on cinema, focusing on Charli XCX's album 'Music, Fashion, Film' and the classic musical drama 'The Sound of Music'.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Released in 2026, Charli XCX's album 'Music, Fashion, Film' is a pop album featuring guitars influenced by Daft Punk-inspired electronic rock. The album explores themes of nightlife, friendship, and self-aware humor, with prominent distorted guitar instrumentation and Auto-Tune sounds. It was supported by four singles: 'Rock Music', 'SS26', 'Wink Wink', and 'Camera'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "'The Sound of Music' is a 1965 American musical drama film directed by Robert Wise, based on the stage musical of the same name. The film stars Julie Andrews as Maria von Trapp and follows her experiences as governess to seven children, her eventual marriage to their father Captain Georg von Trapp, and their escape during the Anschluss in 1938. It won five Academy Awards, including Best Picture."}], "facts": [{"label": "Album Release Date", "value": "24 July 2026"}, {"label": "Film Release Date", "value": "March 2, 1965"}, {"label": "Academy Awards Won by 'The Sound of Music'", "value": "Five"}, {"label": "Singles from 'Music, Fashion, Film'", "value": "'Rock Music', 'SS26', 'Wink Wink', and 'Camera'"}], "secs": 15.1}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Music in film encompasses original compositions specifically created to accompany films, enhancing their dramatic narrative and emotional impact.', 'Film scores vary widely in style, incorporating elements from classical music to jazz, rock, pop, and electronic sounds.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "A film score is original music written to accompany a film or television program. It consists of orchestral, instrumental, or choral pieces called cues that are timed to enhance the film's narrative and emotional impact."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Film scores have evolved since the invention of digital technology, allowing composers to use electronic elements and software to create music. The process involves spotting scenes requiring original music, syncing dramatic events with musical cues, and using various methods for timing and conducting."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The creation of a film score begins during the editing phase when composers work closely with directors to identify which scenes need music. Composers then write and sync their music to the film's timeline, using techniques like spotting, syncing, and written click tracks."}], "facts": [{"label": "Definition", "value": "Original music specifically written to accompany a film or television program."}, {"label": "Styles", "value": "Encompasses orchestral works rooted in Western classical music, jazz, rock, pop, blues, new-age and ambient music, and ethnic/world music styles."}, {"label": "Syncing Method", "value": "Involves using sequencing software, mathematical formulas, or free timing with reference timings to sync dramatic events with musical cues."}, {"label": "Timing Technique", "value": "A leeway of 3\u20134 frames late or early is considered sufficiently accurate for syncing music to picture."}], "secs": 38.1}}}, {"subject": "german history", "slug": "german-history", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Germany is a region whose concept as a distinct entity can be traced back to Julius Caesar's reference to 'Germania'. Its history spans from prehistoric human presence and early Germanic tribes to its development into a modern economic powerhouse within the European Union.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Human presence in Germany dates back millions of years, with pre-human apes like Danuvius guggenmosi present over 11 million years ago. Archaic humans were present at least 600,000 years ago, and stone tools date to 1.3 million years. Early modern humans appeared around 47,500 years ago in the Ilsenh\u00f6hle cave. Prehistoric cultures such as the Linear Pottery culture, R\u00f6ssen culture, and Funnelbeaker culture occurred, followed by the Indo-European ancestry of the Corded Ware culture. In antiquity, Julius Caesar referred to the area east of the Rhine as Germania. Germanic tribes' victory at the Battle of the Teutoburg Forest prevented Roman annexation, though Roman provinces of Germania Superior and Germania Inferior were established along the Rhine."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Following the fall of the Western Roman Empire, the Franks conquered West Germanic tribes. The Frankish Empire was divided in 843, leading to the eastern part becoming East Francia and later the Kingdom of Germany. Otto I became the first Holy Roman Emperor in 962. During the High Middle Ages, the Hanseatic League dominated Baltic and North Sea port cities, while the Northern Crusades led to the establishment of the State of the Teutonic Order. The Protestant Reformation, led by Martin Luther after 1517, divided the region into Protestant northern and eastern states and Catholic southern and western states. This religious divide contributed to the Thirty Years' War (1618-48), which caused tremendous destruction to the Holy Roman Empire."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Holy Roman Empire dissolved in 1806 during the Napoleonic Wars. Following a period of urban growth and industrialization, unification was achieved under Otto von Bismarck in 1871 with the formation of the German Empire. By 1900, Germany became the dominant power on the European continent. However, this led to World War I, where Germany led the Central Powers but was defeated. The resulting Weimar Republic was replaced in 1933 by Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party, who established a totalitarian regime. This regime implemented antisemitic policies and orchestrated the Holocaust, a genocide that killed 11 million people, including 6 million Jews. After defeat in 1945, Germany was occupied by Allies and its territories were split."}, {"heading": "", "body": "During the Cold War, Germany was divided into NATO-aligned West Germany and Warsaw Pact-aligned East Germany. The two were reunited in 1990 after the Berlin Wall opened in 1989. Today, Germany is a founding member of the eurozone and an economic powerhouse contributing about one quarter of the eurozone's annual GDP. Parallel to this, the history of Jews in Germany dates back to at least 321 AD in Cologne. While they experienced periods of prosperity under bishops in Mainz, Speyer, and Worms, they faced severe persecution during the Crusades, the Black Death, and most notably under the Nazi regime."}], "facts": [{"label": "First Holy Roman Emperor", "value": "Otto I (962)"}, {"label": "German Empire Formation", "value": "1871"}], "secs": 60.4}, "ornith": {"lede": "Germany is a federal republic in central-western Europe with a history spanning from prehistoric settlements to its current role as an economic powerhouse within the European Union. Its name derives from 'Germania,' a term used by Julius Caesar for the lands east of the Rhine, where Germanic tribes resisted Roman expansion. The region's political landscape evolved through the Holy Roman Empire, the Protestant Reformation, and 19th-century unification under Prussia.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The concept of Germany as a distinct region traces back to Julius Caesar, who distinguished Germania from Gaul. The Germanic tribes' victory at the Battle of the Teutoburg Forest prevented Roman annexation, though provinces like Germania Superior and Inferior were established along the Rhine. Following the Fall of the Western Roman Empire, the Franks conquered other West Germanic tribes; upon the Frankish Empire's division in 843, the eastern part became East Francia, later the Kingdom of Germany. In 962, Otto I became the first Holy Roman Emperor. During the High Middle Ages, the Hanseatic League dominated trade along the Baltic and North Seas, while the Investiture Controversy saw German Emperors resist Church authority."}, {"heading": "", "body": "In 1517, Martin Luther led the Protestant Reformation, splitting religious allegiance between northern/eastern Protestant states and southern/western Catholic ones. The subsequent Thirty Years' War (1618\u201348) devastated the Holy Roman Empire, leading to the Peace of Westphalia which granted autonomy to estates like Austria, Prussia, Bavaria, and Saxony. Feudalism fell during the Napoleonic Wars, dissolving the Empire in 1806. Although the German revolutions of 1848\u20131849 failed, industrialization modernized the economy and elevated Prussia's power. Under Otto von Bismarck, Germany was unified as the German Empire in 1871."}, {"heading": "", "body": "By 1900, Germany had become Europe's dominant industrial power, leading the Central Powers in World War I before its defeat forced it to pay reparations and cede territory. The German Revolution of 1918\u20131919 established the Weimar Republic. In 1933, Adolf Hitler used economic hardship and resentment over WWI terms to establish a totalitarian Nazi regime that initiated World War II and the Holocaust, which killed six million Jews and eleven million people total. After Germany's defeat in 1945, territories were annexed by Poland and the Soviet Union, and the country was divided during the Cold War into NATO-aligned West Germany and Warsaw Pact-aligned East Germany."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West Germany experienced significant economic expansion, becoming the dominant economy in Western Europe. In 1989, the Berlin Wall was opened following the collapse of the Eastern Bloc, leading to the reunification of East and West Germany in 1990. Franco-German friendship became the foundation for European political integration within the European Union. Germany joined the eurozone as a founding member in 1999 and remains an economic powerhouse, contributing approximately one-quarter of the eurozone's annual gross domestic product."}], "facts": [{"label": "First Holy Roman Emperor", "value": "Otto I (crowned 962)"}, {"label": "Year of Unification", "value": "1871"}, {"label": "Key Reformer", "value": "Martin Luther (1517)"}, {"label": "Nazi Party Leader", "value": "Adolf Hitler"}, {"label": "Year of Reunification", "value": "1990"}, {"label": "Eurozone Contribution", "value": "Approximately 1/4 of annual GDP"}], "secs": 15.9}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "The history of Germany spans millennia, tracing its origins from early ape presence in Paleolithic times to modern economic power. Early concepts of the region were established by Julius Caesar, who referred to the area east of the Rhine as Germania. The development of German culture and language was marked by significant shifts, including the rise of Standard German during the Protestant Reformation.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The concept of Germany as a distinct region dates back to Julius Caesar's reference to Germania, the area east of the Rhine. After Germanic tribes prevented Roman annexation at the Battle of the Teutoburg Forest, the Franks conquered other West Germanic tribes. Following the division of the Frankish Empire in 843, East Francia became the Kingdom of Germany, and Otto I established the Holy Roman Empire in 962. During this period, German port cities formed the Hanseatic League along the Baltic and North Seas, while the region experienced periods of conflict, including the Northern Crusades and the devastating Thirty Years' War (1618-48)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Protestant Reformation began after Martin Luther challenged the Catholic Church in 1517. The subsequent Peace of Westphalia granted autonomy to regional estates like Austria, Prussia, Bavaria, and Saxony. Following the dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire in 1806, the German revolutions of 1848\u20131849 failed, but the Industrial Revolution modernized the economy. This period saw the rise of Prussia's power and the flourishing of arts and science. The unification of Germany was achieved under Otto von Bismarck with the formation of the German Empire in 1871."}, {"heading": "", "body": "By 1900, Germany was a dominant European power until its defeat in World War I, which forced it to pay reparations and lose territory. The subsequent German revolution (1918\u20131919) established the Weimar Republic. This period ended when Adolf Hitler, leader of the Nazi Party, seized power in 1933, leading to a totalitarian regime that implemented antisemitic policies. After World War II, Germany was divided into NATO-aligned West Germany and Warsaw Pact-aligned East Germany until the Berlin Wall opened and the two were reunited in 1990."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Evidence of early human life in Germany includes traces of modern humans up to 47,500 years ago found in the Ilsenh\u00f6hle cave. The Neolithic period saw farmers migrating from western Anatolia between 10,000-8,000 years ago. Linguistically, the German language evolved through Old High German (6th century), Middle High German (1050\u20131350), and Early New High German, which was significantly shaped by Martin Luther's Bible translation in the 16th century."}], "facts": [{"label": "Early Concept of Germany", "value": "Julius Caesar referred to the area east of the Rhine as Germania."}, {"label": "Holy Roman Empire Founder", "value": "Otto I became the first Holy Roman Emperor in 962."}, {"label": "German Unification Year", "value": "The German Empire was formed in 1871 under Otto von Bismarck."}, {"label": "Nazi Regime Start", "value": "Adolf Hitler established a totalitarian regime in Germany in 1933."}, {"label": "End of Cold War Division", "value": "East and West Germany were reunited in 1990 after the Berlin Wall opened."}, {"label": "Oldest Figurative Art (Germany)", "value": "The 40,000-year-old Venus of Hohle Fels."}], "secs": 16.7}, "mistral": {"lede": "German history spans over two millennia, from prehistoric settlements to modern-day Europe's most populous nation. This timeline explores key events, figures, and cultural developments that have shaped Germany.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The earliest human presence in Germany dates back over a million years ago with the discovery of Homo heidelbergensis. The first distinct German culture emerged during the Iron Age, around 500 BC, with the arrival of Celtic tribes. Germanic tribes later dominated the region, eventually forming the basis for modern German identity."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Frankish Empire's division in 843 led to the establishment of East Francia, which became the Kingdom of Germany in 911. Otto I was crowned Holy Roman Emperor in 962, marking the beginning of the Holy Roman Empire. This period saw significant cultural developments, including the rise of the Hanseatic League and the Northern Crusades."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Protestant Reformation, sparked by Martin Luther's Ninety-Five Theses in 1517, divided Germany religiously. The Thirty Years' War (1618-48) devastated the Holy Roman Empire, but ultimately led to the Peace of Westphalia, which established modern sovereignty and diplomacy."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Prussia's rise in power culminated in the unification of Germany under Otto von Bismarck in 1871. Germany's expansionist policies led to its involvement in World War I, resulting in defeat and significant territorial losses. The interwar period saw the rise of Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party, leading to World War II and the Holocaust."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Divided during the Cold War, Germany was reunited in 1990 following the fall of communism. Today, it is a major economic powerhouse and a key player in European politics."}], "facts": [{"label": "Earliest human presence", "value": "Over 1 million years ago"}, {"label": "First distinct German culture", "value": "Iron Age, around 500 BC"}, {"label": "Holy Roman Empire established", "value": "962 AD"}, {"label": "Protestant Reformation began", "value": "1517"}, {"label": "Germany unified", "value": "1871"}, {"label": "World War I ended", "value": "November 1918"}, {"label": "World War II ended", "value": "May 1945"}, {"label": "Germany reunited", "value": "October 1990"}], "secs": 20.3}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "[\"The concept of Germany as a distinct region dates back to Julius Caesar's time, who referred to it as Germania. Throughout history, Germany has experienced significant transformations, from the Holy Roman Empire to modern times.\", \"Germany's history is marked by key events such as the Protestant Reformation, the Thirty Years' War, and two World Wars, which have shaped its political, social, and cultural landscape.\"]", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The concept of Germany as a distinct region can be traced back to Julius Caesar's time. Germanic tribes' victory in the Battle of the Teutoburg Forest prevented Roman annexation, although parts of the Rhine were under Roman control. Following the fall of the Western Roman Empire, the Franks conquered other West Germanic tribes, and after the division of the Frankish Empire in 843, East Francia became the Kingdom of Germany. The Holy Roman Empire was established in 962 with Otto I as its first emperor."}, {"heading": "", "body": "During the High Middle Ages, the Hanseatic League dominated German port cities along the Baltic and North Seas. The Investiture Controversy saw German emperors resist Church authority. Martin Luther led the Protestant Reformation in 1517, dividing Germany into Protestant and Catholic states. The Thirty Years' War (1618-48) brought significant destruction to the Holy Roman Empire, leading to the Peace of Westphalia where regional dukes gained autonomy."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Napoleonic Wars led to the dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire in 1806 and the establishment of the German Confederation. The Industrial Revolution modernized Germany's economy, leading to urban growth and the socialist movement. Prussia unified Germany into the German Empire in 1871. World War I resulted in Germany's defeat and loss of territories. The Weimar Republic was established after the war but fell under Nazi rule in 1933, leading to World War II and the Holocaust. Post-war division led to East and West Germany until reunification in 1990."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Germany's prehistory includes evidence of early human presence dating back over a million years. Significant finds include the oldest complete set of hunting weapons from Sch\u00f6ningen, wooden spears from Bilzingsleben, and the oldest uncontested figurative art in Europe, the L\u00f6wenmensch figurine. The Neolithic period saw the Linear Pottery culture and the use of wheels and wagons around 3400 BC."}], "facts": [{"label": "First Mention of Germania", "value": "Julius Caesar"}, {"label": "Battle of the Teutoburg Forest", "value": "Prevented Roman annexation"}, {"label": "Establishment of Holy Roman Empire", "value": "962 AD by Otto I"}, {"label": "Peace of Westphalia", "value": "1648, granted autonomy to regional dukes"}, {"label": "Dissolution of Holy Roman Empire", "value": "1806"}, {"label": "German Unification", "value": "1871 under Otto von Bismarck"}, {"label": "World War II Holocaust Victims", "value": "Estimated 160,000 to 180,000 German Jews killed"}, {"label": "Reunification of Germany", "value": "1990"}], "secs": 58.7}}}, {"subject": "boiler room", "slug": "boiler-room", "lens": "collection", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Boiler Room is a platform for live music recordings, featuring sets from various global locations including Tokyo, London, Melbourne, and Tulum. It provides a visual and auditory record of electronic music performances through video recordings of DJ sets.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Recorded performances include sets by Fred again.., KETTAMA, salute, Solomun, and Chase & Status in London, Melbourne, Tulum, and Tokyo. Additionally, \u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac \u00a5UK1MAT$U in Tokyo is featured."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Recordings of Boiler Room sets are available as video recordings on YouTube, with durations ranging from approximately 3444 to 7231 seconds. These performances have been varied across different cities worldwide."}], "facts": [{"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Fred again.. , KETTAMA, KETTAMA, salute, salute, Solomun, and Chase & Status"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "Tokyo, London, Melbourne, Tulum"}], "secs": 20.3}, "ornith": {"lede": "Boiler Room is a platform known for broadcasting live DJ sets and performances from venues worldwide, with extensive recordings hosted on YouTube under the 'Boiler Room' channel. These sessions capture electronic music artists performing in intimate or iconic settings, creating an archive of contemporary club culture.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The platform has hosted a diverse range of artists across global locations. Notable recorded sessions include \u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac \u00a5UK1MAT$U performing at Boiler Room: Tokyo (3596s), Solomun at Boiler Room: Tulum (7231s), and Fred again.. at Boiler Room: London (4305s). Other documented performances feature KETTAMA in London (3444s), Chase & Status in London (3585s), and salute in Melbourne (4606s)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "All identified recordings are available as video streams on YouTube under the 'Boiler Room' channel. The durations of these broadcasts range from approximately 34 minutes to over two hours, with Solomun's Tulum set being the longest at 7231 seconds and KETTAMA's London set among the shorter entries at 3444 seconds."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The programming emphasizes live DJ performances in specific geographic locations, with London appearing as a frequent host city. The character of the output is defined by long-form, unedited broadcasts that capture full sets from artists spanning electronic music subgenres."}], "facts": [{"label": "Platform", "value": "Boiler Room"}, {"label": "Primary Distribution", "value": "YouTube (Boiler Room channel)"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Live DJ sets and performances"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "\u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac \u00a5UK1MAT$U, Solomun, Fred again.., KETTAMA, Chase & Status, salute"}, {"label": "Host Cities", "value": "London, Tokyo, Tulum, Melbourne"}], "secs": 8.4}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "This collection documents performances at the Boiler Room, a highly influential live-music event series. The material is crucial for archiving electronic music and DJ culture, providing documented sets from various global locations. It emphasizes the raw energy of live performance captured in video format.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The sources document several high-profile Boiler Room performances across different cities and years. These include Fred again.. at London, KETTAMA also recorded in London, salute in Melbourne, Solomun in Tulum, and Chase & Status in London. Other documented live sets include King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard's 'Experimental Rave Show' at The Regency Ballroom in San Francisco on November 6, 2024, and moe.'s performance at the King Pine Room in Carabasset Valley, ME, on March 22, 2002."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The recorded output is primarily available as video streams from YouTube (e.g., Fred again..: London; KETTAMA: London; salute: Melbourne; Solomun: Tulum; Chase & Status: London). Additionally, there are specific live recordings of bands such as King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard's set at The Regency Ballroom, which is available via Bandcamp and archive.org, and moe.'s multi-disc set from the King Pine Room."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The material showcases a diverse range of genres and performance styles. Boiler Room features electronic music sets by artists like Fred again.. and Solomun across multiple international locations (London, Melbourne, Tulum). In contrast, other documented shows include complex rock performances (King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard's 'Experimental Rave Show') and multi-set acoustic/rock acts (moe.'s performance)."}], "facts": [{"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Fred again.., KETTAMA, salute, Solomun, Chase & Status, King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard, moe."}, {"label": "Locations Documented", "value": "London, Melbourne, Tulum, San Francisco (The Regency Ballroom), Carabasset Valley, ME (King Pine Room)."}, {"label": "Primary Format", "value": "Video recordings (YouTube) and audio/video bootlegs (Bandcamp/archive.org)."}, {"label": "Key Event Series", "value": "Boiler Room."}, {"label": "Example Date/Event", "value": "King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard, November 6, 2024."}], "secs": 9.8}, "mistral": {"lede": "Boiler Room is a renowned online platform dedicated to live music broadcasting, focusing primarily on electronic dance music. It's significant for live recordings enthusiasts as it offers high-quality performances from various global locations.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Boiler Room has hosted numerous notable artists and events. Some of the standout performances include King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard's 'Experimental Rave Show' at The Regency Ballroom, San Francisco on 2024-11-06 (https://archive.org/details/kglw2024-11-06), Bonobos Convergence Live at Alexandra Palace on 2014-11-27 (https://archive.org/details/bonobo2014-11-27), and moe. Live at King Pine Room on 2002-03-22 (https://archive.org/details/moe.2002-03-22). Additionally, Boiler Room has featured live sets from artists like \u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac \u00a5UK1MAT$U in Tokyo, Fred again.. and KETTAMA in London, salute in Melbourne, Solomun in Tulum, and Chase & Status in London."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Boiler Room offers a wide range of recordings from its events. Many performances are available on the Boiler Room YouTube channel (https://www.youtube.com/user/boilerroomtv), with durations ranging from approximately 35 minutes to over 7 hours. Some performances, like King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard's, are also available as bootlegs on platforms like Bandcamp (https://bootleggizzard.bandcamp.com/)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Boiler Room is known for its focus on electronic dance music, featuring a diverse range of sub-genres and artists. Its events often take place in unique locations, such as rooftops, warehouses, and even natural environments like Tulum. The platform also supports underground and emerging artists alongside established ones."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founded", "value": "2010"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "London, UK (with global events)"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Online live music broadcasting platform, focusing on electronic dance music"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard, Bonobo, moe., \u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac, Fred again.., KETTAMA, salute, Solomun, Chase & Status, among many others"}, {"label": "Recordings Available", "value": "Boiler Room YouTube channel (https://www.youtube.com/user/boilerroomtv), Bandcamp bootlegs (https://bootleggizzard.bandcamp.com/) and other platforms"}], "secs": 13.6}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Boiler Room is a platform that showcases live music performances from around the world, offering viewers and listeners access to diverse genres and artists.', 'It matters for live recordings as it provides high-quality streams and archives of concerts, making them accessible to a global audience.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Boiler Room has featured a variety of notable performances. King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard performed an 'Experimental Rave Show' on November 6, 2024, at The Regency Ballroom in San Francisco, CA. Other highlights include \u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac \u00a5UK1MAT$U in Tokyo, Fred again.. and KETTAMA in London, salute in Melbourne, Solomun in Tulum, and Chase & Status also in London."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Boiler Room's recordings are available on various platforms. King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard's performance can be found on Bandcamp at https://bootleggizzard.bandcamp.com/album/live-in-san-francisco-24. Other Boiler Room sessions, such as those by \u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac \u00a5UK1MAT$U and Solomun, are available on YouTube."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Boiler Room's programming spans a wide range of genres and artists, reflecting its global reach. It includes electronic music, indie rock, and other experimental forms, often featuring emerging and established acts in intimate settings or unique locations around the world."}], "facts": [{"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "King Gizzard & The Lizard Wizard, \u00a5\u00d8U$UK\u20ac \u00a5UK1MAT$U, Fred again.., KETTAMA, salute, Solomun, Chase & Status"}, {"label": "Locations", "value": "The Regency Ballroom (San Francisco, CA), Alexandra Palace (London), King Pine Room (Sugarloaf Mountain, ME), Tokyo, London, Melbourne, Tulum"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Live music performances streamed and recorded for online viewing/listening"}, {"label": "Where Recordings Live", "value": "YouTube, Bandcamp"}], "secs": 27.3}}}, {"subject": "cercle", "slug": "cercle", "lens": "collection", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Cercle is a French music company that produces recorded performances of artists performing in unique locations. These recordings are often available as high-quality video and audio captures of sets played by electronic and other musical artists.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Recorded performances for Cercle include Disclosure at Plitvice Lakes National Park in Croatia, Tame Impala at Parque de Quetzalc\u00f3atl in Mexico, Monolink at Cercle Odyssey in Paris, France, ZHU at Hakuba Iwatake in Nagano, Japan, Folamour at Cath\u00e9drale Saint-Pierre in Geneva, Switzerland (for Cercle & W Hotels), and HVOB at Copa del Sol in Careyes, Mexico."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Cercle's programming focuses on pairing electronic music artists with visually stunning or culturally significant locations, such as national parks, cathedrals, and mountain peaks in countries including Croatia, Mexico, Japan, Switzerland, and France."}], "facts": [{"label": "Company Type", "value": "French music company"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Disclosure, Tame Impala, ZHU, Monolink, Monolink, Folamour, HVOB"}, {"label": "Where Recordings Live", "value": "YouTube"}], "secs": 36.8}, "ornith": {"lede": "Cercle is a French music company that produces immersive electronic music performances filmed at iconic global locations. The project pairs artists with striking natural and architectural backdrops, creating visually driven live recordings rather than traditional concert documentation.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Cercle has produced performances by Disclosure at Plitvice Lakes National Park in Croatia, Tame Impala at Parque de Quetzalc\u00f3atl in Mexico, Monolink at Cercle Odyssey in Paris, France, ZHU at Hakuba Iwatake in Nagano, Japan, Folamour at Cath\u00e9drale Saint-Pierre in Geneva, Switzerland (in partnership with W Hotels), and HVOB at Copa del Sol in Careyes, Mexico. These performances are distributed through the official Cercle YouTube channel."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Cercle recordings exist primarily as video content on YouTube under the 'Cercle' channel. The project also operates a sub-brand called 'Cercle Odyssey,' which produced a Paris performance by Monolink. Additionally, Cercle has collaborated with W Hotels for select events."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Cercle project emphasizes location-driven performances, featuring electronic artists at natural landmarks (Plitvice Lakes, Hakuba Iwatake), architectural sites (Cath\u00e9drale Saint-Pierre), and resort venues (Parque de Quetzalc\u00f3atl, Copa del Sol). The format prioritizes visual spectacle alongside musical performance."}], "facts": [{"label": "Type", "value": "French music company"}, {"label": "Primary Platform", "value": "YouTube (Cercle channel)"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Disclosure, Tame Impala, Monolink, ZHU, Folamour, HVOB"}, {"label": "Locations Featured", "value": "Plitvice Lakes (Croatia), Careyes (Mexico), Paris (France), Nagano (Japan), Geneva (Switzerland)"}, {"label": "Sub-brand", "value": "Cercle Odyssey"}, {"label": "Partnerships", "value": "W Hotels"}], "secs": 9.7}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "This collection documents the use of 'Cercle' as a brand or event series name for hosting diverse musical acts across various international locations. It is particularly valuable for documenting electronic and ambient music performances, featuring artists like Godspeed You! Black Emperor, A Silver Mt Zion, Tame Impala, and Monolink. The material provides specific setlists and recordings from unique venues globally.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Godspeed You! Black Emperor performed at CAT (Cercle des Arts Traditionnels) in Bordeaux, France on January 25, 2002. A Silver Mt Zion played at Le Cercle in Qu\u00e9bec, QC on September 24, 2010. Other notable performances include Tame Impala at Parque de Quetzalc\u00f3atl in Mexico; Monolink at Cercle Odyssey in Paris, France; ZHU at Hakuba Iwatake in Nagano, Japan; and Folamour at Cath\u00e9drale Saint-Pierre in Geneva, Switzerland. HVOB performed live at Copa del Sol in Careyes, Mexico."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Recordings are available from multiple sources: a comprehensive set of two lossless audio recordings of Godspeed You! Black Emperor's 2002 performance (Source 1 and Source 2). Other material exists as YouTube videos documenting live sets at various locations, including Plitvice Lakes National Park in Croatia, and the general availability of specific recorded performances from other artists."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The programming is characterized by diverse international electronic music events. The featured acts include ambient rock (Godspeed You! Black Emperor), experimental folk (A Silver Mt Zion), psychedelic pop (Tame Impala), and house/disco genres (Monolink, Folamour). The event series utilizes various venues, including national parks, cultural centers, and historical buildings."}], "facts": [{"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Godspeed You! Black Emperor, A Silver Mt Zion, Tame Impala, Monolink, ZHU, Folamour, HVOB"}, {"label": "Locations/Venues", "value": "CAT (Cercle des Arts Traditionnels) Bordeaux, FR; Le Cercle Qu\u00e9bec, QC; Parque de Quetzalc\u00f3atl Mexico; Cercle Odyssey Paris, France; Plitvice Lakes National Park Croatia; Cath\u00e9drale Saint-Pierre Geneva, Switzerland"}, {"label": "Recording Formats", "value": "FLAC (lossless audio), WAV, YouTube video streams"}, {"label": "Notable Dates", "value": "January 25, 2002; September 24, 2010"}], "secs": 11.4}, "mistral": {"lede": "Cercle Brugge KSV is primarily known as a Belgian football club based in Bruges. However, it has also hosted notable musical performances and events under the name 'Cercle'.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Godspeed You! Black Emperor performed at Cercle des Arts Traditionnels (CAT) in Bordeaux, France on 2002-01-25. The performance was recorded and is available on Archive.org with two different lossless audio recordings.\n\nA Silver Mt Zion performed at Le Cercle in Qu\u00e9bec, Canada on 2010-09-24. The performance was recorded by 'emmanuel' and is also available on Archive.org.\n\nCercle has hosted unique musical events in various locations around the world, such as Disclosure performing at Plitvice Lakes National Park in Croatia, Tame Impala at Parque de Quetzalc\u00f3atl in Mexico, Monolink live at Cercle Odyssey in Paris, France, ZHU at Hakuba Iwatake in Nagano, Japan, Folamour at Cath\u00e9drale Saint-Pierre in Geneva, Switzerland, and HVOB live at Copa del Sol in Careyes, Mexico."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The performances by Godspeed You! Black Emperor and A Silver Mt Zion have been preserved as audio recordings on Archive.org. The unique musical events hosted by Cercle are available as videos on YouTube."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Cercle's programming seems to focus on electronic music, with artists such as Disclosure, Tame Impala, Monolink, ZHU, Folamour, and HVOB performing at unique locations around the world. The performances by Godspeed You! Black Emperor and A Silver Mt Zion are from their respective tours."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founded", "value": "1899"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "Bruges, Belgium (football club); various locations worldwide (musical events)"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Football club; musical performances and events"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Godspeed You! Black Emperor, A Silver Mt Zion, Disclosure, Tame Impala, Monolink, ZHU, Folamour, HVOB"}, {"label": "Where Recordings Live", "value": "Archive.org (audio recordings), YouTube (video recordings)"}], "secs": 15.7}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Cercle is a network of venues and events that have hosted notable live music performances. It matters for live recordings as it provides a platform for diverse artists to perform, with several concerts captured on audio and video.', 'The archive includes recordings from various Cercle locations around the world, offering insights into different musical styles and performances.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Godspeed You! Black Emperor performed at CAT (Cercle des Arts Traditionnels) in Bordeaux on January 25, 2002. The setlist included tracks like 'Gathering Storm' and 'World Police & Friendly Fire.' A Silver Mt Zion played at Le Cercle in Qu\u00e9bec on September 24, 2010, with songs such as 'I Built Myself a Metal Bird' and 'There Is a Light.'"}, {"heading": "", "body": "Multiple recordings of Godspeed You! Black Emperor's performance at CAT in Bordeaux are available on Archive.org, sourced from different tapers. A Silver Mt Zion's set at Le Cercle in Qu\u00e9bec is also archived there. Various other performances by artists like Disclosure, Tame Impala, Monolink, ZHU, Folamour, and HVOB can be found on YouTube, each captured for the Cercle series."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Cercle events feature a wide range of musical genres and artists, from experimental post-rock to electronic dance music. The venues are typically cultural or historical sites, providing unique settings for live performances. The programming reflects a commitment to showcasing diverse talent and creating memorable experiences for audiences."}], "facts": [{"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Godspeed You! Black Emperor, A Silver Mt Zion, Disclosure, Tame Impala, Monolink, ZHU, Folamour, HVOB"}, {"label": "Locations", "value": "CAT (Cercle des Arts Traditionnels) in Bordeaux, Le Cercle in Qu\u00e9bec, Plitvice Lakes National Park in Croatia, Parque de Quetzalc\u00f3atl in Mexico, Cercle Odyssey in Paris, Hakuba Iwatake in Nagano, Cath\u00e9drale Saint-Pierre in Geneva, Copa del Sol in Careyes"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Live music performances at various venues around the world"}, {"label": "Where Recordings Live", "value": "Archive.org and YouTube"}], "secs": 39.9}}}, {"subject": "great woods", "slug": "great-woods", "lens": "collection", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Great Woods, currently known as the Xfinity Center in Mansfield, Massachusetts, is an outdoor amphitheatre that has hosted a wide array of live performances since 1986. It serves as a critical site for live recordings due to its high volume of concerts and history of filming various concert films and live albums.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The venue has been the site of many significant recorded performances. The Allman Brothers Band's 1991 performance on September 6 was filmed for the concert video 'Live at Great Woods', released on VHS, LaserDisc, and later DVD. Jimmy Buffett & The Coral Reefer Band, who have performed at the venue 63 times, have several recordings here, including the Live in Mansfield, MA Soundboard album (2003) and portions of 'Buffett Live: Tuesdays, Thursdays, Saturdays' (1999) and 'Encores' (2008). The Who's DVD 'The Who: Live in Boston' was filmed here in September 2002. The Smiths same-day performance on August 5, 1986, thirteen songs from which are featured on the 2017 re-release of the The Queen is Dead LP. The Eagles performed five consecutive nights during their Hell Freezes Over (1994) Tour as part of their second live album. Depeche Mode's 2009 show was recorded for their 'Recording the Universe' project, and Phish's 1994 performance on July 8 included a track for 'A Live One' (1994) and a complete performance of the Gamehendge saga. Rush's June 23, 1997 concert featured two songs, including '2112', on the live album 'Different Stages'.\n\nOther notable recordings include the Allman Brothers Band's September 19, 1998 show featuring Jack Pearson, and Jerry Garcia Band's September 10, 1989 show. Allman Brothers Band's 'Live at Great Woods' (1991) includes tracks such as 'Statesboro Blues', 'End of the Line', 'Blue Sky', and 'Whipping Post'.\n\nAdditionally, various artists like Oasis (two DVDs filmed here), Flyleaf, Korn, and Stone Sour (Family Values 2006 DVD), and Aerosmith (performance in the film Be Cool) were recorded here."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The venue's recorded output is diverse, formats include DVDs, VHS, LaserDisc, 'Soundboard' albums,_and music videos. music videos filmed here include Nickelback's 'Rockstar', the Fray's 'All At Once', and Gin Blossoms' 'As Long as It Matters'. Music videos are part of uma own separate category of recorded output from full concert films. \u0447\u0442\u043e\u0442 a variety of\u0442\u0432\u0440\u043a recordings exist across various platforms, including YouTube and Archive.org, appearing as soundboard (SBD) sound-sources up to 1080p remasters of concert footage.    "}, {"heading": "", "body": "The venue is an outdoor amphitheatre with a capacity of 12,000 (expanded to 19,900 after 2000). Its season typically runs from mid-May until late September. It mainly hosts concerts, and occasionally graduation ceremonies for Mansfield High School. Originally proposed as a performing arts center in Brookline, the plan was changed to an amphitheater to meet a regional need for outdoor venues in New England. It has hosted artists such as Santana, R.E.M., The Cure, Pearl Jam, and Spice Girls."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The venue has operated under several names: Great Woods Center for the Performing Arts (1986-1999), Tweeter Center for the Performing Arts (1999-2008), Comcast Center (2008-2013), and Xfinity Center (2014-present)."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founded", "value": "June 13, 1986"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "Mansfield, Massachusetts"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Allman Brothers Band, Phish, Rush, The Smiths, The Who, Jimmy Buffett, Depeche Mode, and Aerosmith"}, {"label": "Where recordings live", "value": "DVDs, VHS, LaserDisc, Soundboard albums, YouTube, and Archive.org"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Outdoor amphitheatre"}], "secs": 69.1}, "ornith": {"lede": "Great Woods, now known as the Xfinity Center in Mansfield, Massachusetts, is an outdoor amphitheatre that opened in June 1986 and has hosted nearly 80 concerts per season at its peak. It matters for live recordings because it served as a filming location for multiple commercial releases (Oasis, The Who, Aerosmith) and a recording site for official live albums by Depeche Mode, the Eagles, and Jimmy Buffett. The venue also sits inside a robust fan-trading ecosystem, with soundboard recordings of Phish, the Jerry Garcia Band, and the Allman Brothers Band circulating on YouTube and archive.org.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Jimmy Buffett & The Coral Reefer Band hold the venue record with 63 shows; portions of Buffett Live: Tuesdays, Thursdays, Saturdays (1999), Encores (2008), and the Live in Mansfield, MA Soundboard album (2003) were captured there. Phish performed at Great Woods 22 times between 1992 and 2024; a track from A Live One (1994) was recorded on July 8, 1994, the same night they played their complete Gamehendge saga (a full recording of that show is available on YouTube). The Allman Brothers Band filmed Live at Great Woods Amphitheater on September 6, 1991 for Japanese TV, released on VHS/LaserDisc in 1992 and DVD in 1998/2014/2021. Aerosmith played the venue 24 times; their live performance was filmed there for the movie Be Cool. Depeche Mode performed here five times, with the last show in 2009 recorded for their Recording the Universe live albums project. The Who filmed The Who: Live in Boston at Great Woods in September 2002. Oasis filmed two DVDs here (2001 and 2005). Pearl Jam played 11 times; on July 11, 2003 they delivered their longest show up to that point."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Commercial releases captured at Great Woods include the Allman Brothers Band's Live at Great Woods (1992 VHS/LaserDisc, 1998/2014/2021 DVD), Depeche Mode's Recording the Universe project (2009 show), the Eagles' second live album from five consecutive nights on their Hell Freezes Over tour (1994), Jimmy Buffett's Live in Mansfield, MA Soundboard (2003) plus portions of two other Buffett DVDs, Oasis's two concert DVDs (2001 and 2005), The Who: Live in Boston (2002), the Family Values (2006) DVD featuring Flyleaf, Korn, and Stone Sour, Aerosmith's Be Cool performance, Gin Blossoms' \"As Long as It Matters\" video (WBCN River Rave, June 8, 1996), Nickelback's \"Rockstar\" video, The Smiths' 2017 re-release of The Queen is Dead featuring thirteen songs from August 5, 1986, Rush's Different Stages album with two songs including a full \"2112\" from June 23, 1997, and the Jerry Garcia Band's September 10, 1989 soundboard release. Fan recordings of Phish (July 8/9, 1994) and the Allman Brothers Band (September 19, 1998) are also available on YouTube."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Great Woods is an outdoor amphitheatre in Mansfield, Massachusetts, within 40 miles of Providence, Boston, Worcester, and Cape Cod. It opened June 13, 1986 with Yo-Yo Ma and the Pittsburgh Symphony Orchestra, was expanded after 2000 to a capacity of 19,900 (7,000 reserved, 7,000 lawn, 5,900 general admission), and runs a summer season from mid-May through late September. It was named Top Grossing Amphitheater by Billboard in 2010 and averages about 36 events per season today after regional competition from the Xfinity Theatre (Hartford) and Leader Bank Pavilion (Boston). Artists with deep ties to the venue include Aerosmith (24 shows), Phish (22 shows between 1992\u20132024), Pearl Jam (11 shows), Depeche Mode (5 shows), Jimmy Buffett (63 shows), R.E.M. (at least 7 shows including a three-night stand in 1989), and Santana (regular performer since 1986). The venue also hosted the Spice Girls on July 8, 1998 as part of their Spiceworld Tour."}], "facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "Mansfield, Massachusetts"}, {"label": "Opened", "value": "June 13, 1986"}, {"label": "Capacity", "value": "19,900 (7,000 reserved / 7,000 lawn / 5,900 general admission)"}, {"label": "Season", "value": "Mid-May through late September"}, {"label": "Most-played-at artist", "value": "Jimmy Buffett (63 shows)"}, {"label": "Notable commercial recordings", "value": "Allman Brothers Band Live at Great Woods; Depeche Mode Recording the Universe; Eagles second live album; Oasis two DVDs; The Who: Live in Boston"}, {"label": "Fan recordings available on", "value": "YouTube and archive.org (Phish, Jerry Garcia Band, Allman Brothers Band)"}, {"label": "Billboard recognition", "value": "Top Grossing Amphitheater (2010)"}], "secs": 21.5}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Great Woods, originally the Great Woods Center for the Performing Arts, is a large outdoor amphitheater in Mansfield, Massachusetts. It has served as a major regional venue for concerts and live performances since opening in 1986, making it a significant location for capturing diverse musical acts.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The venue hosted numerous notable performances, including Aerosmith (24 times), Depeche Mode (five times; the last show in 2009 was recorded for their live albums project Recording the Universe), Eagles (performing five consecutive nights on their Hell Freezes Over Tour), and Oasis (filming two DVDs here, one in 2001, and one in 2005). The Allman Brothers Band performed multiple times, including a set filmed on September 6, 1991, and subsequent sets featuring Jack Pearson on September 19, 1998. Other acts include Pearl Jam (11 times), Phish (22 times between 1992 and 2024), Rush (featuring two songs from a June 23, 1997 concert), The Who (filming the DVD The Who: Live in Boston in September 2002), and Jerry Garcia Band (performing on September 10, 1989)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The venue has been used for filming multiple live albums and concert DVDs. Specific recordings include the Allman Brothers Band's 'Live at Great Woods' (recorded September 6, 1991), which was released on VHS and LaserDisc in 1992, with later versions available on DVD. Other recorded material includes Phish performing a track from A Live One (1994) and completing their Gamehendge saga; the Allman Brothers Band's sets featuring Jack Pearson were filmed for online viewing; and The Who: Live in Boston was filmed here."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The venue is primarily an outdoor amphitheater that operates during the summer months, typically from mid-May until late September. It has hosted a wide variety of genres, ranging from rock (Aerosmith, Pearl Jam) to classic acts (Eagles, James Taylor), and jazz/jam bands (Grateful Dead, Phish). The venue's history includes being proposed as a performing arts center before becoming an amphitheater."}], "facts": [{"label": "Original Name", "value": "Great Woods Center for the Performing Arts (opened June 13, 1986)"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "Mansfield, Massachusetts"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Aerosmith, Allman Brothers Band, Phish, Depeche Mode, Eagles, Pearl Jam, Grateful Dead, The Who"}, {"label": "Recording Availability Examples", "value": "Allman Brothers Band - Live at Great Woods (1992/DVD); Phish 7/8/1994; Jerry Garcia Band 09/10/89"}], "secs": 14.6}, "mistral": {"lede": "Great Woods, originally known as the Great Woods Center for the Performing Arts, is an outdoor amphitheater located in Mansfield, Massachusetts. Opened in 1986 with a capacity of 12,000, it was expanded after 2000 to accommodate 19,900 attendees. The venue hosts primarily concerts from mid-May until late September and has been named Top Grossing Amphitheater by Billboard.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Great Woods has hosted numerous notable performances, including Aerosmith (24 times), Avenged Sevenfold (3 times), Depeche Mode (5 times), Eagles (5 consecutive nights), Flyleaf, Gin Blossoms, James Taylor (4 consecutive dates), Jimmy Buffett & The Coral Reefer Band (63 times), Korn, Nickelback, Oasis (2 DVDs), Pearl Jam (11 times), Phish (22 times), R.E.M. (at least 7 times), Rush, Santana (regular performer), Spice Girls, Stone Sour, The Cure (4 times), The Fray, The Smiths (13 songs featured on LP re-release), and The Who."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Several performances at Great Woods have been recorded and released. Notable examples include 'Live at Great Woods' by the Allman Brothers Band (1992), Grateful Dead's performances from 1973 and 1977, Phish's performance on July 8, 1994, Jerry Garcia Band's performances in 1989 and 1990, and The Allman Brothers Band featuring Jack Pearson's performance on September 19, 1998."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Great Woods primarily hosts concerts, with a focus on rock music. It has also hosted graduation ceremonies such as that of Mansfield High School. The venue's programming includes both established and up-and-coming artists, with notable performances by legendary bands like The Who and Rush, as well as contemporary acts like Phish and Pearl Jam."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founded", "value": "1986"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "Mansfield, Massachusetts"}, {"label": "Format", "value": "Outdoor amphitheater with a capacity of 19,900 (expanded after 2000)"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Aerosmith, Avenged Sevenfold, Depeche Mode, Eagles, Flyleaf, Gin Blossoms, James Taylor, Jimmy Buffett & The Coral Reefer Band, Korn, Nickelback, Oasis, Pearl Jam, Phish, R.E.M., Rush, Santana, Spice Girls, Stone Sour, The Cure, The Fray, The Smiths, The Who"}, {"label": "Recordings", "value": "'Live at Great Woods' by Allman Brothers Band (1992), Grateful Dead performances from 1973 and 1977, Phish's performance on July 8, 1994, Jerry Garcia Band performances in 1989 and 1990, The Allman Brothers Band featuring Jack Pearson's performance on September 19, 1998"}], "secs": 19.2}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "Great Woods, now known as the Xfinity Center in Mansfield, Massachusetts, is an outdoor amphitheater that has hosted numerous notable live music performances and recordings over its decades of operation.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Great Woods has seen a wide array of legendary acts. Notably, the Allman Brothers Band recorded their 'Live at Great Woods' DVD here in 1992. Jimmy Buffett has performed there more than any other artist, with 63 shows. Depeche Mode's final show in 2009 was recorded for their live albums project. Phish played 22 times between 1992 and 2024, including a complete Gamehendge saga performance in 1994."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Several recordings exist from Great Woods performances, including the Allman Brothers Band's 'Live at Great Woods' DVD, portions of Jimmy Buffett's 'Buffett Live: Tuesdays, Thursdays, Saturdays' and 'Encores,' Depeche Mode's live albums project, and Phish's 'A Live One.' The Jerry Garcia Band also performed there in 1989, with recordings available online."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Great Woods primarily hosts concerts during the summer months. It has a diverse lineup of artists ranging from rock legends to contemporary acts. The venue's capacity and outdoor setting make it suitable for both large-scale performances and intimate shows, attracting a wide audience."}], "facts": [{"label": "Location", "value": "Mansfield, Massachusetts"}, {"label": "Capacity", "value": "19,900 (7,000 reserved seats, 7,000 lawn seats, and 5,900 general admission seats)"}, {"label": "Season", "value": "Mid May until late September"}, {"label": "Notable Artists", "value": "Aerosmith, Allman Brothers Band, Avenged Sevenfold, Depeche Mode, Eagles, Jimmy Buffett, Pearl Jam, Phish, R.E.M., Rush, Santana, Spice Girls, The Cure, The Fray, The Smiths, The Who"}, {"label": "Recordings", "value": "'Live at Great Woods' by Allman Brothers Band, portions of Jimmy Buffett's albums, Depeche Mode live albums project, Phish's 'A Live One,' Jerry Garcia Band recordings"}, {"label": "Current Name", "value": "Xfinity Center"}], "secs": 41.0}}}, {"subject": "kanye west", "slug": "kanye-west", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Kanye West is a producer and rapper from Chicago who expanded the vocabulary of hip-hop and steered the genre into the pop mainstream. His signature as a producer is characterized by meticulous, adventurous arrangements and an open-minded approach to musical tastes.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "West's production style is grounded in high-pitched, speeded-up vocal samples from a wide range of sources including Michael Jackson, Shirley Bassey, The Doors, and Daft Punk. This trademark predilection for speeding up old soul loops and affixing them to 'Escalade-worthy' beats creates a textured production sound. He has also worked with string arranger Miri Ben-Ari to bring more musicality and instrumentation to hip-hop, aiming to create songs that make the rest of the listener feel good."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West's breakthrough as a producer came with Jay-Z's 2001 album The Blueprint, where he produced several tracks. As a performer, his debut album The College Dropout (2004) was noted for its inventive production and social consciousness, selling 443,000 copies in its first week. He also produced notable tracks such as Alicia Keys' 'You Don't Know My Name', Jay-Z's 'H To The Izzo', and Twista's 'Slow Jamz'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Inspired by the Wu-Tang Clan's RZA, afterlife West has a fruitful working relationship with Jay-Z, producing most of The Blueprint 3. He has also collaborated with artists across genres, including Janet Jackson, Mariah Carey, and Madonna. Signed to Roc-A Fella,on which he was the 'go to guy' for hip hop and R&B, He is described as a member of the 'pop innovators' of his generation."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West is considered canonical because he bridged urban and suburban, black and white, pop and underground, and 'bling and brains' into a crossover formula that topped album charts. He moved hip-hop away from the 'gangsta rap' rut of the 1990s, providing an alternative to both the street thug fantasy and the 'backpack rapper' label."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Late 1990s - 2009"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, R&B, Pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "The Blueprint (Jay-Z), The College Dropout (Kanye West), 'H To The Izzo' (Jay-Z), 'You Don't Know uma same as 'You Don't Know My Name' (Alicia Keys)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Roc-A Fella"}, {"label": "get rid of the 'signature method/gear' label if it doesn't fit, but let's use: Signature Method", "value": "Speeding up old soul loops and high-pitched vocal samples"}], "secs": 54.2}, "ornith": {"lede": "Kanye West is a Chicago-born producer and rapper who redefined hip-hop production by layering sped-up soul samples over heavy beats, bringing orchestral instrumentation into the genre. He first established himself as an in-demand hitmaker for Jay-Z's The Blueprint before launching his own landmark solo career with The College Dropout (2004). His work bridged urban and suburban audiences, steering hip-hop firmly toward pop mainstream success.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "West's production style is grounded in meticulous, adventurous arrangements and wide-ranging musical tastes. Inspired by Wu-Tang Clan's RZA, he built his signature sound on a bedrock of high-pitched, speeded-up vocal samples drawn from sources ranging from Michael Jackson to Shirley Bassey, The Doors to Daft Punk. He affixed these loops to heavy 'Escalade-worthy' beats and utilized an off-kilter, out-of-tune flow. West explicitly brought pop instrumentation into hip-hop, citing that 'Pop music will have a lot of instrumentation and I brought that to hip-hop.' His textured productions also featured string arrangements, such as those by Miri Ben-Ari."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West's breakthrough came producing several tracks for Jay-Z's groundbreaking 2001 album, The Blueprint. He subsequently produced Alicia Keys' 'You Don't Know My Name', Jay-Z's 'H To The Izzo', and Twista's #1 hit 'Slow Jamz'. His own debut album, The College Dropout (2004), sold 443,000 copies in its maiden week on Roc-A-Fella Records. It announced him as a star with impressive reviews despite its excessive length of 75 minutes. He was also working on future projects including Late Registration and Graduation at the time of his early coverage."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West's fruitful working relationship with Jay-Z continues to this day, with West producing most of The Blueprint 3. He worked with string arranger Miri Ben-Ari on his debut. His production wizardry expanded to include the 'cream of pop and R&B royalty,' including Janet Jackson, Mariah Carey, and Madonna. Damon Dash, his former label boss, noted in 2005 that West combines the superficialness urban demographics need with conscious rhymes for backpack rappers."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West is considered one of the smartest and slickest operators of his generation, with a cross-racial appeal and career path to rival that of President Obama. His genius lies in bridging urban and suburban, black and white, pop and underground, bling and brains into a uniquely appealing crossover formula that tops albums charts and sells in the millions. Thanks largely to this open-minded approach, West has expanded the vocabulary of hip-hop and helped steer the genre firmly into the pop mainstream, serving as one of the most prolific and creative pop innovators of his generation."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Active from late 1990s (Demo Tape c. Sept '97) through 2009"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, R&B, Pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Jay-Z's The Blueprint (2001), Alicia Keys' 'You Don't Know My Name', Twista's 'Slow Jamz'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Roc-A-Fella, Jeanius Level Musik"}, {"label": "Signature Method", "value": "Speeded-up soul vocal samples layered over heavy beats with pop instrumentation"}, {"label": "Key Collaborator", "value": "Jay-Z (produced The Blueprint and The Blueprint 3)"}], "secs": 15.0}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Kanye West (Ye) is a producer and rapper known for his innovative production wizardry and ability to bridge disparate musical genres. His signature sound involves meticulous arrangements, often utilizing sped-up vocal samples from diverse sources\u2014ranging from classical artists like Shirley Bassey to pop figures like Michael Jackson. He has been instrumental in expanding the vocabulary of hip-hop into the mainstream pop landscape.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "West's production style is characterized by adventurous arrangements and a predilection for speeding up old soul loops. He builds his sound on a bedrock of high-pitched, speeded-up vocal samples drawn from a vast range of sources, including The Doors and Daft Punk. His work has been noted for bringing more musicality to hip-hop, incorporating elements like string arrangements. This open-minded approach allows him to bridge urban and suburban, black and white, pop and underground, creating a uniquely appealing crossover formula."}, {"heading": "", "body": "His debut album, *The College Dropout* (2004), was noted for its blend of inventive production and sold 443,000 copies in its first week. The album is described as being brimming with his textured productions and represented a conscious effort to make people feel good while addressing struggles. Furthermore, West's early work included producing tracks for Jay-Z's groundbreaking 2001 album, *The Blueprint*, which marked the beginning of a fruitful working relationship."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West has collaborated with numerous artists across genres, including Janet Jackson, Mariah Carey, and Madonna. He also worked on tracks for Jay-Z's *The Blueprint* (2001) and later produced most of the Brooklyn heavyweight's subsequent release, *The Blueprint 3*. His influences include the Wu-Tang Clan's RZA, and he has been noted to have received credits producing songs for artists like Alicia Keys ('You Don't Know My Name') and Twista's former #1 hit 'Slow Jamz'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West is considered canonical for his ability to bridge the gap between underground hip-hop and pop culture, helping steer the genre firmly into the mainstream. He has been praised for combining superficiality needed by the urban demographic with conscious rhymes appealing to a broader audience. His career trajectory demonstrates an unparalleled track record as a producer, performer, and collaborator in his generation."}], "facts": [{"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Speeding up old soul loops; utilizing high-pitched, speeded-up vocal samples from diverse sources (e.g., Shirley Bassey, Michael Jackson)."}, {"label": "Key Albums/Productions", "value": "*The College Dropout* (2004); *The Blueprint* (2001) for Jay-Z."}, {"label": "Genre Mastery", "value": "Bridging hip-hop, R&B, and pop into a crossover formula; expanding the vocabulary of hip-hop."}, {"label": "Notable Collaborators", "value": "Jay-Z, Alicia Keys, Mariah Carey, Madonna, Janet Jackson."}], "secs": 15.1}, "mistral": {"lede": "Kanye West is a canonical music producer and rapper known for his innovative production techniques, blending soul loops with hip-hop beats, and his influential albums that span genres.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Kanye West's signature sound is characterized by speeding up old soul loops and affixing them to Escalade-worthy beats. He is known for his meticulous, adventurous arrangements and wide-ranging musical tastes, drawing from various sources like Michael Jackson, Shirley Bassey, The Doors, and Daft Punk."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West's debut album 'The College Dropout' (2004) announced his arrival as a star, selling over 443,000 copies in its maiden week. It was praised for its blend of inventive production and off-kilter flow. His later albums like 'Late Registration' (2005) and 'Graduation' (2007) further cemented his status as a canonical producer."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West has worked with a wide range of artists, including Jay-Z, Alicia Keys, Twista, Janet Jackson, Mariah Carey, and Madonna. His collaboration with Jay-Z on 'The Blueprint' (2001) was a breakthrough for West's production career."}, {"heading": "", "body": "West's influence on hip-hop and pop music is undeniable. He has expanded the vocabulary of hip-hop, helping steer it firmly into the pop mainstream. His open-minded approach to music-making has made him a bridge between urban and suburban, black and white, pop and underground."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "2001-present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, R&B, Pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "'The College Dropout' (2004), 'Late Registration' (2005), 'Graduation' (2007)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Roc-A-Fella Records, GOOD Music"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Speeding up old soul loops, wide-ranging musical tastes"}], "secs": 16.7}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Kanye West is a pioneering producer and rapper known for his innovative production techniques that blend soul samples with contemporary beats.', 'His debut album, The College Dropout, showcased his ability to create textured, socially conscious tracks that resonated with both urban and suburban audiences.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Kanye West's signature sound is characterized by high-pitched, sped-up vocal samples from a wide range of sources, including Michael Jackson, Shirley Bassey, The Doors, and Daft Punk. He meticulously arranges these elements to create unique and adventurous musical landscapes that bridge urban and suburban, black and white, pop and underground genres."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The College Dropout (2004) by Kanye West is a landmark record that sold 443,000 copies in its first week. It showcased his innovative production techniques and socially conscious lyrics, blending hip-hop with elements of pop music to create a crossover formula that resonated widely."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Kanye West has worked extensively with Jay-Z, producing tracks for his groundbreaking albums including The Blueprint (2001) and The Blueprint 3. He also collaborated with other notable artists such as Alicia Keys, Janet Jackson, Mariah Carey, and Madonna, expanding the vocabulary of hip-hop and steering it into the pop mainstream."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Kanye West's ability to bridge different musical genres and demographics through his innovative production techniques and socially conscious lyrics has made him a pivotal figure in contemporary music. His work with Jay-Z and other artists, as well as his own albums like The College Dropout, have significantly influenced the evolution of hip-hop and its integration into mainstream pop culture."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "2000s-present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, R&B, Pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "The College Dropout (2004), The Blueprint 3 (2010)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Roc-A-Fella Records, GOOD Music"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "High-pitched, sped-up vocal samples; meticulous arrangement techniques"}], "secs": 41.6}}}, {"subject": "nile rodgers", "slug": "nile-rodgers", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Nile Rodgers is an American guitarist and producer known for his 'chucking' guitar style and as a co-founder of the disco group Chic. He has written, produced, and performed on records that have sold over 750 million albums and 100 million singles worldwide.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers' signature sound is defined by his 'chucking' guitar style and an inventive lead guitar approach. As part of the Chic production formula, he and bassist Bernard Edwards developed a blend of upbeat stylized string charts combined with Edwards' original bass technique and Rodgers' own guitar work. This fusion of jazz, soul, and funk grooves with European-influenced melodies and lyrics created a distinctive sound that became a a standard for club, pop, and R&B."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Key records include Chic's self-titled debut (1977), which featured hits like 'Dance, Dance, Dance (Yowsah, Yowsah, Yowsah)' and 'Everybody Dance'. The 1978 album C'est Chic included the multi-million seller 'Le Freak', which reached No. 1 on the US charts. The 1979 album Risqu\u00e9 featured 'Good Times', a number one single on pop and soul charts that became one of the most-sampled songs of all time, serving as the basis for Grandmaster Flash's 'Adventures on the Wheels of Steel' and The Sugarhill Gang's 'Rapper's Delight'. Additionally, Rodgers produced David Bowie's Let's Dance (1983), Madonna's Like a Virgin (1984) and Diana Ross's album Diana (1980), which yielded hits like 'Upside Down' and 'I'm Coming Out'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers' most significant early collaborator was bassist Bernard Edwards, with whom he co-founded the Big Apple Band (later Chic) and produced for artists such as Sister Sledge ('We Are Family', 'He's the Greatest Dancer') and Diana Ross. He also worked with a variety of high-profile artists across different eras, including Daft Punk (Random Access Memories), Beyonc\u00e9 (Renaissance), and Duran Duran ('The Reflex', 'Notorious')."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers is an inductee of the Songwriters Hall of Fame and the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (via the Award for Musical Excellence). He has received a Grammy Lifetime Achievement/Special Merit Award. His influence is widely recognized, with 'Good Times' influencing sounds of others like Queen's 'Another One Bites the Dust' and Blondie's 'Rapture'. Rolling Stone placed him 7th on their list of the 250 greatest guitarists of all time, calling him a 'true innovator'."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1970s to present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Disco, Funk, R&B, Pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Like a Virgin (Madonna), Let's Dance (David Bowie), Diana (Diana Ross), Random Access Memories (Daft Punk)"}, {"label": "label", "value": "Atlantic Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "'Chucking' guitar style"}], "secs": 267.4}, "ornith": {"lede": "Nile Rodgers is an American guitarist and record producer whose career spans over five decades, defined by the creation of Chic's foundational disco sound and a subsequent era as one of pop music's most sought-after hitmakers. As co-founder of Chic alongside Bernard Edwards, he developed a signature guitar style\u2014often called 'chucking'\u2014that fused jazz, soul, and funk into a seamless groove that bridged rock and dance music. His production work in the 1980s helped define the sound of artists like David Bowie, Madonna, and Diana Ross, while his later collaborations with Daft Punk and Beyonc\u00e9 have kept him at the forefront of contemporary music.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers' production signature is built on a distinctive guitar technique known as 'chucking,' which involves percussive, muted strumming that creates a rhythmic backbone for the track. This style, developed alongside bassist Bernard Edwards and drummer Tony Thompson, fused jazz, soul, and funk grooves with European melodic influences to create Chic's immersive sound. The production formula typically mixed up-beat, stylized string charts with Edwards' incisive bass lines and Rodgers' inventive lead guitar work. Rolling Stone described Rodgers as a 'true innovator who never slows down,' placing him 7th on their list of the 250 greatest guitarists of all time for his massive influence on the genre."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Chic's 1979 album 'Risqu\u00e9' featured 'Good Times,' a track that became one of the most-sampled songs in history and served as the basis for The Sugarhill Gang's 'Rapper's Delight' and Queen's 'Another One Bites the Dust.' In the 1980s, Rodgers produced Diana Ross's album 'Diana' (1980), which sold over ten million copies internationally and yielded the No. 1 hit 'Upside Down.' He co-produced David Bowie's 'Let's Dance' (1983) and was largely responsible for Madonna's early success with 'Like a Virgin' (1984). More recently, his work on Daft Punk's 'Random Access Memories' (2013) won three Grammy Awards in 2014, and his production on Beyonc\u00e9's 'Renaissance' earned two Grammys in 2023."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers' primary creative partnership was with bassist Bernard Edwards; together they formed Chic and produced records for numerous artists before the band's initial breakup. Their production company, Chic Organization, worked with acts including Sister Sledge ('We Are Family'), Sheila and B. Devotion ('Spacer'), and Carly Simon. After Chic's dissolution in 1983, Rodgers expanded his collaborations to include David Bowie, INXS, Duran Duran, Kylie Minogue, the B-52s, Jeff Beck, Avicii, Kygo, Daft Punk, Mick Jagger, Coldplay, Bryan Ferry, Christina Aguilera, Lady Gaga, George Michael, and Beyonc\u00e9. He also co-founded the We Are Family Foundation following the September 11 attacks."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers is recognized as one of the most sought-after record producers of the eighties and a Songwriters Hall of Fame inductee. He was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame via the Award for Musical Excellence and received the Grammy Lifetime Achievement/Special Merit Award. His work with Chic has been preserved in the Library of Congress, with 'We Are Family' (2018) and 'Le Freak' (2018) selected for preservation in the National Recording Registry and Library of Congress respectively. As of 2025, Rodgers and Chic continue to perform as Nile Rodgers & Chic, maintaining his active presence in music history."}], "facts": [{"label": "Born", "value": "September 19, 1952, New York City"}, {"label": "Primary Genres", "value": "Disco, Funk, Pop, Rock"}, {"label": "Key Collaborator", "value": "Bernard Edwards (Chic co-founder)"}, {"label": "Signature Technique", "value": "Chucking guitar style"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "David Bowie's 'Let's Dance', Madonna's 'Like a Virgin', Daft Punk's 'Random Access Memories'"}, {"label": "Grammy Awards", "value": "3 in 2014 (Daft Punk), 2 in 2023 (Beyonc\u00e9)"}, {"label": "Hall of Fame Inductions", "value": "Rock and Roll Hall of Fame, Songwriters Hall of Fame"}, {"label": "Records Sold", "value": "More than 750 million albums worldwide"}], "secs": 20.0}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Nile Rodgers is an American guitarist and co-founder of Chic, known for establishing a signature sound that fused jazz, soul, and funk grooves with European influences. His production formula often mixed up-beat stylized string charts with the distinctive bass lines of Bernard Edwards and his own inventive lead guitar style.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers' sound is characterized by a 'chucking guitar style,' which has been recognized as highly influential. His signature production formula involves mixing up-beat stylized string charts with the original bass technique of Bernard Edwards and his own inventive lead guitar work. The group Chic developed a fusion of jazz, soul, and funk grooves with melodies and lyrics that had a European influence."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Chic's self-titled debut (1977) featured hits like \"Dance, Dance, Dance\" and \"Everybody Dance.\" The 1978 album C'est Chic included the multi-million-seller \"Le Freak,\" which was a major success. Later, the group released the Risqu\u00e9 album in 1979, featuring \"Good Times,\" a song that became one of the most-sampled songs of all time. Beyond Chic, Rodgers produced successful tracks for Sister Sledge's We Are Family (1979), Diana Ross's Diana album (1980), and David Bowie's Let's Dance (1983)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers formed Chic with bassist Bernard Edwards, a partnership that defined the disco era. They worked extensively with artists such as Sister Sledge, Diana Ross, Madonna, INXS, and David Bowie. Other collaborators include Tony Thompson (drummer), Norma Jean Wright, Luci Martin, and Alfa Anderson. He has also produced work for major artists including Beyonc\u00e9, Lady Gaga, and Coldplay."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers is recognized as a true innovator in music, with Rolling Stone calling him 'massively influential.' His career spans multiple genres, from early jazz/funk bands to disco, R&B, and pop. He has received numerous accolades, including three Grammy Awards for his work on Daft Punk's Random Access Memories (2014) and two in 2023 for Beyonc\u00e9\u2019s Renaissance album."}], "facts": [{"label": "Co-founder/Primary Role", "value": "Chic; Guitarist, Producer"}, {"label": "Signature Style", "value": "Chucking guitar style; Fusion of jazz, soul, and funk grooves with European influence."}, {"label": "Key Early Hits (Chic)", "value": "\"Le Freak\" (1978), \"I Want Your Love,\" \"Good Times\" (1979)."}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Sister Sledge's We Are Family; Diana Ross's Diana album; David Bowie's Let's Dance; Madonna's Like a Virgin."}, {"label": "Awards/Recognition", "value": "Three Grammy Awards (Daft Punk, 2014); Two Grammys (Beyonc\u00e9, 2023)."}, {"label": "Early Band Formation", "value": "Formed The Big Apple Band in 1972 with Bernard Edwards."}, {"label": "Influential Technique", "value": "Mixing up-beat stylized string charts with inventive lead guitar and bass lines."}], "secs": 15.5}, "mistral": {"lede": "Nile Rodgers is an American guitarist and co-founder of Chic, a disco band that released several hits in the late 70s. He has written, produced, and performed on records that have sold over 750 million albums and 100 million singles worldwide.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers is known for his distinctive chucking guitar style. His signature sound is characterized by a fusion of jazz, soul, funk grooves with European influences in melodies and lyrics."}, {"heading": "", "body": "'Le Freak' (1978) was Chic's biggest hit, selling over seven million singles worldwide. 'Good Times' (1979), another Chic track, became one of the most-sampled songs in music history."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers co-founded Chic with bassist Bernard Edwards and drummer Tony Thompson. They worked extensively together, producing hits for Sister Sledge ('We Are Family', 'He's the Greatest Dancer'), Diana Ross ('Upside Down', 'I'm Coming Out'), and others."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers is a Songwriters Hall of Fame inductee, Rock and Roll Hall of Fame inductee via the Award for Musical Excellence, and Grammy Lifetime Achievement/Special Merit Award recipient. Rolling Stone ranked him 7th on their list of the 250 greatest guitarists of all time."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Late 1970s - Present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Disco, Funk, Soul, Pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Chic's 'Le Freak', Sister Sledge's 'We Are Family', Diana Ross' 'Upside Down'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Atlantic Records, Warner Bros. Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Chucking guitar style, fusion of jazz, soul, funk grooves with European influences"}], "secs": 20.1}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Nile Rodgers is an American guitarist renowned for co-founding Chic, a band that revolutionized disco music in the late 1970s.', 'His signature as a producer is marked by innovative guitar work and a unique blend of jazz, soul, funk, and European influences.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers' production style is characterized by upbeat stylized string charts, Bernard Edwards' distinctive bass lines, and Rodgers' inventive lead guitar. His work with Chic combined jazz, soul, funk grooves, and European-influenced melodies and lyrics to create a seamless and immersive sound."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Chic's self-titled debut (1977) featured hits like 'Dance, Dance, Dance' and 'Everybody Dance'. The 1978 album C'est Chic included the multi-million-selling hit 'Le Freak', which became Atlantic Records' only triple platinum single at the time. 'Good Times' from Risqu\u00e9 (1979) reached No. 1 on both pop and soul charts and is one of the most-sampled songs of all time."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers and Edwards produced numerous influential records for other artists, including Sister Sledge's 'We Are Family' (1979), Diana Ross's 'Upside Down' and 'I'm Coming Out', David Bowie's 'Let's Dance' (1983), Madonna's 'Like a Virgin' (1984), and many others. His work with Chic laid the groundwork for his later success as a producer."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rodgers is considered canonical due to his groundbreaking work with Chic, which defined disco music in the late 1970s and early 1980s. His innovative production techniques and collaborations have influenced countless artists across genres, making him a true innovator in popular music."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Late 20th century to present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Disco, funk, soul, jazz, new wave"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Chic albums (1977-1982), Sister Sledge's 'We Are Family' (1979), Diana Ross's 'Diana' (1980)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Atlantic Records, Warner Music"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Upbeat stylized string charts, Bernard Edwards' bass technique, Nile Rodgers' lead guitar"}, {"label": "Awards", "value": "Grammy Awards (2014, 2023), Songwriters Hall of Fame inductee, Rock and Roll Hall of Fame via Award for Musical Excellence"}], "secs": 59.1}}}, {"subject": "rick rubin", "slug": "rick-rubin", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Rick Rubin is a record producer and co-founder of Def Jam Records. He is known for his signature 'spare, sparse sound' and a Zen-like approach to production where he acts as an editor and coach rather than spending months in the studio.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rubin's early productions for Run-DMC, LL Cool J, and the Beastie Boys set a template for a sparse sound that helped build the hip hop genre. He believes that great songs need little enhancing and maintains a Zen-like approach to production, seeing himself as an editor or coach who issues notes on work in progress rather than spending long hours in the studio with artists. This method often leads to artists to produce career-best work that sounds like it was made by 'real flesh-and-blood human beings'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin has worked across a variety of genres, spanning from the sacred to the profane. Notable records include Tom Petty's Wildflowers (1994), which Rubin considers one of his favorites and uses as a test for new sound systems; LL Cool J's debut LP Radio, where he was credited as 'Reduced by Rick Rubin'; and productions for artists such as Slayer, Justin Timberlake, Neil Diamond, Shakira, and the Dixie Chicks."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin co-founded Def Jam Records with Russell Simmons in 1984. His lineage includes early hip hop pioneers like Run-DMC, LL Cool J and the Beastie Boys, and later collaborations with Public Enemy, Johnny Cash, Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan, and Slayer. He also served as co-chairman of Columbia Records in the US in 2007."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin is described as an iconoclast who has 'tamed rap' and brought it to a mass white audience. His influence is 'stronger than ever' and the 'crush grooves' he produced through Def Jam and American Recordings have cast a long, dark shadow over contemporary popular music for nearly two decades."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Active since 1984 (founding of Def Jam)"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip hop, Heavy Metal, and various popular music genres"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Tom Petty's Wildflowers, LL Cool J's Radio, productions for Johnny Cash, Slayer, and the Beastie Boys"}, {"label": "label", "value": "Def Jam Records (Co-founder), American Recordings, Columbia Records (Co-chairman)"}, {"label": "Signature Method", "value": "Acting as an editor/coach with a 'spare, sparse sound' and a "}], "secs": 77.3}, "ornith": {"lede": "Rick Rubin (born Frederick Jay Rubin in 1963) is a record producer and co-founder of Def Jam Recordings who revolutionized hip hop production with a spare, sparse aesthetic before expanding into rock, metal, country, and pop. Operating from Malibu, he views himself as an editor or coach rather than a traditional producer, favoring minimal enhancement to capture the 'real flesh-and-blood' quality of performances.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rubin's production philosophy centers on the belief that great songs require little enhancing. He is described as a 'Zen-like' iconoclast who habitually leads artists to career-best work by leaving them in the hands of an engineer and issuing notes on the work in progress, rather than spending months in the studio. His early productions for LL Cool J, Beastie Boys, and Run-DMC set the template for hip hop's 'spare, sparse sound.' He is known for his meticulous attention to environment and purity\u2014evidenced by his preference for specific spring water and a home designed like a temple\u2014and he views himself less as a producer and more as an editor/coach."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin's career spans nearly two decades of influential productions across genres. In hip hop, his early work with LL Cool J (whose debut LP 'Radio' carried the credit 'Reduced by Rick Rubin'), Beastie Boys, and Run-DMC defined the genre's sonic template. He later brought rap to a mass audience through Def Jam. His range is highlighted by collaborations spanning from Public Enemy and Slayer to Johnny Cash, Nusrat Fate Tom Petty (whose album 'Wildflowers' he cites as one of his favorites), Neil Diamond, and the Dixie Chicks. The text notes that going from a Chili Peppers session to Neil Diamond forces him to think about music in new ways daily."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin co-founded Def Jam Recordings in 1984 at age 21 with Russell Simmons, whom he met at a party for the hip hop TV show 'Graffiti Rock.' He cites Tom Mottley as an early influence and lists D'Angelo's 'Voodoo,' Radiohead's 'The Bends,' Nick Cave's 'No More Shall We Part,' Neil Young's 'Ragged Glory,' and Tom Petty's 'Full Moon Fever' among his favorite records of the last 20 years. He has worked with a diverse lineage including AC/DC, Bob Dylan, Bruce Springsteen, Barbra Streisand, Beyonc\u00e9, Red Hot Chili Peppers, and James Brown (referenced via The Blues Brothers). His approach is described as 'needle-phobic' in a metaphorical sense, crafting sounds that cause 'needles to drop, skip and jump all over the world.'"}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin is canonical because he set the template for hip hop production with his sparse sound and later became one of music's last great iconoclasts, bridging sacred and profane genres. In 2007, Time magazine named him among their 100 Most Influential People in the World. He was appointed co-chairman of Columbia Records in the US that same year, serving as a figurehead for a label with heritage artists like AC/DC and Bob Dylan alongside contemporary heavyweights like Beyonc\u00e9. His reported six-figure fee and ability to attract top talent across metal, rap, country, and pop underscores his unique position as a producer who sees himself as an editor rather than a technician."}], "facts": [{"label": "Born", "value": "10 March 1963 in Lido Beach, New York"}, {"label": "Founded", "value": "Def Jam Recordings (1984) with Russell Simmons"}, {"label": "Role at Columbia", "value": "Co-chairman of Columbia Records in the US (appointed 2007)"}, {"label": "Signature Hip Hop Credit", "value": "LL Cool J's 'Radio' (credited as 'Reduced by Rick Rubin')"}, {"label": "Production Philosophy", "value": "Views himself as an editor/coach; believes great songs need little enhancing"}, {"label": "Recognition", "value": "Named one of Time's 100 Most Influential People (2007)"}, {"label": "Residence", "value": "Malibu, California"}], "secs": 17.3}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Rick Rubin is known for his minimalist approach, viewing himself less as a producer and more as an editor or coach. His signature style emphasizes the need for great songs to require little enhancing, leading to records that sound like they were made by real flesh-and-blood human beings. He has overseen artists spanning genres from hip hop to heavy metal, maintaining a reputation for guiding talent toward their career best work.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rubin's approach is described as Zen-like; he does not spend months in the studio with his charges but instead leaves them in the hands of an engineer, issuing notes on the work in progress. This method has been credited with producing records that sound authentic and natural. His early productions for hip hop artists like LL Cool J, Beastie Boys, and Run-DMC set a template for a spare, sparse sound within the genre. He is noted for his ability to guide diverse acts\u2014from Slayer to Justin Timberlake, or from Public Enemy to Johnny Cash\u2014by forcing him to think about music in new ways each day."}, {"heading": "", "body": "His notable releases include LL Cool J's debut LP *Radio* (which carried the credit 'Reduced by Rick Rubin'), and work with artists such as Beastie Boys, Run-DMC, Public Enemy, Johnny Cash, Slayer, and Justin Timberlake. He has also worked with contemporary heavyweights like Beyonc\u00e9 and heritage artists including AC/DC, Bob Dylan, and Bruce Springsteen."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin founded Def Jam Records in 1984 out of his college dormitory room with Russell Simmons. His career spans collaborations with a vast range of acts, including the Beastie Boys, Run-DMC, Red Hot Chili Peppers, and Johnny Cash. He has been associated with Columbia Records as co-chairman, a label home to diverse artists like Bob Dylan and Beyonc\u00e9."}, {"heading": "", "body": "He is viewed as both a throwback to another era and one of music's last great iconoclasts. His ability to work across wildly different genres\u2014from the sacred to the profane\u2014is central to his canonical status, demonstrating an influence that has cast a long shadow over contemporary popular music for decades."}], "facts": [{"label": "Signature Method", "value": "Zen-like approach; acts as editor/coach rather than hands-on producer."}, {"label": "Early Genre Template", "value": "Spare, sparse sound in hip hop (LL Cool J's debut LP *Radio*)."}, {"label": "Founding Label", "value": "Def Jam Records (founded 1984 with Russell Simmons)."}, {"label": "Genre Range", "value": "Spans from hip hop and punk to heavy metal, folk, and pop."}, {"label": "Notable Collaborations", "value": "Beastie Boys, Run-DMC, Johnny Cash, Public Enemy, Slayer, Justin Timberlake."}], "secs": 13.3}, "mistral": {"lede": "Rick Rubin, born Frederick Jay Rubin in 1963, is an American record producer known for his minimalist approach and signature sound, which he developed while founding Def Jam Records. He has produced landmark albums across various genres, including hip hop, rock, and country.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rick Rubin is renowned for his minimalist production style, often referred to as 'reduced by Rick Rubin.' He believes in letting the artist's raw talent shine through, with little enhancement. His productions are characterized by a spare, sparse sound, emphasizing organic instruments and authentic performances."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rick Rubin has produced numerous landmark records across genres:\n- Beastie Boys' 'Licensed to Ill' (1986) \u2013 the first album certified platinum by the RIAA for a hip hop artist.\n- Red Hot Chili Peppers' 'Blood Sugar Sex Magik' (1991) \u2013 a critical and commercial success that sold over 15 million copies worldwide.\n- Johnny Cash's 'American' series (2000\u20132010) \u2013 a critically acclaimed trilogy that revitalized Cash's career."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rick Rubin has collaborated with numerous influential artists, including:\n- LL Cool J\n- Run-DMC\n- Slayer\n- Metallica\n- Tom Petty\n- Dixie Chicks\n- Neil Diamond\nHis influence can be traced through the careers of many prominent producers and musicians who have worked with him."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rick Rubin is considered canonical in music production due to his pioneering role in bringing hip hop to mainstream audiences, his influential minimalist approach, and his enduring impact on various genres. His work continues to inspire producers and musicians today."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Active since the mid-1980s"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip hop, rock, country, metal, alternative"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Beastie Boys' 'Licensed to Ill', Red Hot Chili Peppers' 'Blood Sugar Sex Magik', Johnny Cash's 'American' series"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Def Jam Recordings, American Recordings"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Minimalist production style, emphasis on organic instruments and authentic performances"}], "secs": 18.3}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Rick Rubin is a renowned music producer known for his minimalist approach and ability to bring out the raw essence in artists across various genres.', 'His signature sound is characterized by stripped-down production, emphasizing natural performances over elaborate studio techniques.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Rubin's approach to production is marked by minimalism and a focus on the purity of the performance. He often leaves artists in the hands of an engineer, providing guidance through notes rather than extensive studio time. This method ensures that the music retains its authentic, unpolished quality."}, {"heading": "", "body": "LL Cool J's debut LP 'Radio' (1985) is a landmark record, credited with setting the template for hip-hop production under Rubin's guidance. The album's sparse sound and focus on raw talent exemplify his signature style. Another notable release is Johnny Cash's 'American Recordings' series (1994-2003), which brought new life to the country legend's music through Rubin's innovative approach."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin has worked with a diverse array of artists, including LL Cool J, Beastie Boys, Run-D.M.C., Slayer, Johnny Cash, Neil Diamond, and the Dixie Chicks. His collaborations span genres from hip-hop to heavy metal, country, and pop, showcasing his versatility and ability to bring out unique qualities in each artist."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rubin's influence on music production is profound, particularly in the development of hip-hop. His minimalist approach has become a hallmark of modern production, emphasizing raw talent over studio embellishment. His work with Johnny Cash and other artists also demonstrates his ability to revitalize established genres and performers."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1980s-present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, heavy metal, country, pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "LL Cool J's 'Radio', Johnny Cash's 'American Recordings'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Def Jam Records, American Recordings, Columbia Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Minimalist production, emphasis on natural performances"}], "secs": 43.9}}}, {"subject": "j dilla", "slug": "j-dilla", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "J Dilla (James Dewitt Yancey) was an American record producer, rapper, and composer from Detroit, Michigan. He is widely considered one of the most influential producers in hip-hop and popular music, known for a signature sound characterized by melodic loops, breakbeats, and vocal samples.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dilla's production is marked by a 'drunk' style of drum programming that eschews quantization, resulting in a loose feel. He frequently utilized melodic loops\u2014often in odd-numbered lengths such as five, seven, or eleven bars\u2014rather than the standard four-bar hip-hop loop. Within these phrases, he would often loop different parts of the beat in threes, fives, and sevens. His sound is described as having rich, unique drum sounds, warm, muzzy instrumentation, and endlessly inventive melodies."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla's body of work includes his final album, Donuts, released three days before his death. He produced the Pharcyde's Labcabincalifornia (1995), and contributed heavily to Common's breakthrough album Like Water for Chocolate (2000). With A Tribe Called Quest, he worked on Beats, Rhymes and Life (1996) and The Love Movement (1998), often as part of the production collective The Ummah. He also recorded Fantastic, Vol. 1 (1997) with his group Slum Village."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla was a founding member of the Soulquarians musical collective and the production collective The Ummah, which included Q-Tip and Ali Shaheed Muhammad of A Tribe Called Quest, and later Raphael Saadiq. He collaborated with Madlib as the duo Jaylib (releasing Champion Sound) and worked extensively with artists such as Erykah Badu, Talib Kweli, Common, and Janet Jackson."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla is regarded as a modern genius whose work raised the artistic level of hip-hop production in Detroit. His influence extends to modern music and other producers and drummers due to his unique approach to drum programming and his compositional depth. His music has been described as having parallels with impressionistic composers like Erik Satie, Ravel, and Debussy."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Mid-1990s to 2006"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, instrumental hip-hop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Donuts, Like Water for Chocolate (Common), Labcabincalifornia (The Pharcyde)"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Akai MPC music workstation; non-quantized 'drunk' drum programming"}, {"label": "Key Collaborators", "value": "The Ummah, Soulquarians, Madlib (as Jaylib)"}], "secs": 65.2}, "ornith": {"lede": "J Dilla (James Dewitt Yancey) was an American record producer, rapper, and composer who emerged from Detroit's underground hip-hop scene in the mid-1990s as a founding member of Slum Village. He is widely considered one of the most influential producers in hip-hop history, known for raising the artistic level of production through his 'drunk' drum style and complex melodic loops.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dilla's approach to drum programming was characterized by a loose, off-kilter rhythm often described as 'drunk,' which eschewed the use of quantization. According to The Guardian, his affinity for crafting lengthy, melodic loops peppered with breakbeats and vocal samples took instrumental hip-hop into new, more musically complex realms. His sound featured rich, unique drum sounds and warm, muzzy instrumentation. Composer Miguel Atwood-Ferguson noted that Dilla loved five-bar loops and utilized odd time signatures (sevens and elevens), creating a depth of expression comparable to impressionistic composers like Satie, Ravel, and Debussy."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla's production discography includes several seminal works. He produced the Pharcyde album Labcabincalifornia (1995) and handled seven tracks on it. In 1996, A Tribe Called Quest received a Grammy nomination for their Dilla-produced album Beats, Rhymes and Life. His work with Common on Like Water for Chocolate (2000) was critically acclaimed; he produced the majority of the album alongside Soulquarians collaborators D'Angelo, Questlove, and James Poyser. His final album, Donuts, was released three days before his death in 2006."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla was a founding member of the Soulquarians, a musical collective active during the late 1990s and early 2000s that included Ahmir 'Questlove' Thompson, D'Angelo, and James Poyser. He collaborated with Madlib as the duo Jaylib, releasing the album Champion Sound. Under the production umbrella 'The Ummah,' he worked with Q-Tip and Ali Shaheed Muhammad on projects for A Tribe Called Quest, De La Soul, and Busta Rhymes. Bilal credited Dilla with showing him a unique approach to drum programming."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Despite a short mainstream career cut short by his death at age 32 from TTP and lupus, Dilla is widely considered one of the most influential producers in hip-hop and popular music. His work influenced a huge amount of modern music through its exploration and lack of vanity. Composer Miguel Atwood-Ferguson described him as a 'modern genius' whose music captured the spirit of his time. The classical world has also saluted him, with orchestral tributes like Suite for Ma Dukes highlighting the profound humanity and depth of heart in his compositions."}], "facts": [{"label": "Born", "value": "February 7, 1974"}, {"label": "Died", "value": "February 10, 2006 (age 32)"}, {"label": "Origin", "value": "Detroit, Michigan"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, instrumental hip-hop"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Payday Records, MCA Records, BBE (Beat Generation series)"}, {"label": "Key Groups", "value": "Slum Village, Soulquarians, Jaylib"}, {"label": "Signature Method", "value": "Off-kilter drum programming ('drunk' style), no quantization, melodic loops"}, {"label": "Notable Production", "value": "Common - Like Water for Chocolate (2000)"}], "secs": 16.9}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "J Dilla (James Dewitt Yancey) was an American record producer, rapper, and composer who emerged from the mid-1990s underground hip-hop scene in Detroit. His signature sound is characterized by rich, utterly unique drum sounds, warm, muzzy instrumentation, and endlessly inventive melodies, often utilizing lengthy, melodic loops peppered with breakbeats and vocal samples. He was widely considered one of the most influential producers in hip-hop and popular music.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dilla's approach to drum programming is notably loose, or 'drunk,' eschewing the use of quantization. His music features rich, utterly unique drum sounds and warm, muzzy instrumentation. He was known for crafting lengthy, melodic loops that blended breakbeats and vocal samples, taking instrumental hip-hop into more musically complex realms. Furthermore, his work has been noted for its depth and honesty in how beats meld together."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla produced key tracks for The Pharcyde's album *Labcabincalifornia*, including 'Bullshit,' 'Runnin',' and 'Splattitorium.' He was a founding member of the Soulquarians collective, working with artists like Common on his critically acclaimed breakthrough album, *Like Water for Chocolate*. Other notable productions include work for Janet Jackson (*Got 'Til It's Gone*), Busta Rhymes ('Still Shining'), and A Tribe Called Quest (with The Ummah). His final album was *Donuts*, released three days before his death."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla is widely considered one of the most influential producers in hip-hop. Critics have praised him for being a 'modern genius' who captured and represented the spirit of a particular time, focusing on exploration through music rather than vanity. His work has influenced subsequent artists; Loyle Carner noted his debt to Dilla's less-is-more work with A Tribe Called Quest."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era/Genre", "value": "Mid-1990s underground hip-hop scene in Detroit, Michigan"}, {"label": "Signature Method", "value": "Loose or 'drunk' drum programming that eschews quantization."}, {"label": "Key Groups/Collectives", "value": "Slum Village (founding member), Soulquarians (founding member), The Ummah."}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "The Pharcyde - *Labcabincalifornia*; Common - *Like Water for Chocolate*"}, {"label": "Signature Sound Elements", "value": "Lengthy, melodic loops peppered with breakbeats and vocal samples; warm, muzzy instrumentation."}, {"label": "Key Collaborators", "value": "A Tribe Called Quest (with The Ummah), De La Soul, Common, Erykah Badu."}], "secs": 14.3}, "mistral": {"lede": "J Dilla, born James Dewitt Yancey, was an American record producer, rapper, and composer from Detroit, Michigan. He emerged in the mid-1990s underground hip-hop scene as a member of Slum Village and later collaborated with artists like A Tribe Called Quest, De La Soul, Busta Rhymes, and Janet Jackson. Dilla's signature sound featured lengthy, melodic loops peppered with breakbeats and vocal samples, raising the artistic level of instrumental hip-hop.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "J Dilla is renowned for his unique approach to drum programming, often described as 'loose' or 'drunk', eschewing quantization. His productions are characterized by lengthy, melodic loops, breakbeats, and vocal samples, raising the artistic level of instrumental hip-hop. He was also known for his use of the Akai MPC sampler."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla's production work includes Slum Village's 'Fantastic, Vol. 1' (1997), Janet Jackson's 'Got 'Til It's Gone' (1997), A Tribe Called Quest's 'The Love Movement' (1998), and Common's 'Like Water for Chocolate' (2000). His final album, 'Donuts', released three days before his death in 2006, is considered a masterpiece of hip-hop production."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dilla collaborated with numerous artists throughout his career, including Q-Tip, Madlib, Erykah Badu, Common, and Bilal. He was a founding member of the Soulquarians collective alongside Questlove, D'Angelo, and James Poyser. His influence can be heard in the work of producers like Flying Lotus, Madlib, and Thundercat."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Despite his short mainstream career, J Dilla is widely considered one of the most influential producers in hip-hop and popular music. His innovative approach to sampling, drum programming, and composition has left an indelible mark on modern production techniques."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Mid-1990s - Early 2000s"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, Instrumental hip-hop, Jazz-rap"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Slum Village's 'Fantastic, Vol. 1', Janet Jackson's 'Got 'Til It's Gone', A Tribe Called Quest's 'The Love Movement', Common's 'Like Water for Chocolate'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "BBE Records, MCA Records, Payday Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Akai MPC sampler, loose/quantization-free drum programming"}], "secs": 24.4}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['J Dilla, also known as Jay Dee or simply Dilla, was an influential American hip-hop producer and rapper from Detroit, Michigan.', 'His signature as a producer is characterized by intricate drum programming, melodic loops, and a unique approach to sampling that elevated the complexity of instrumental hip-hop.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "J Dilla's production style was marked by his loose, or 'drunk', drum programming that eschewed quantization. He crafted lengthy, melodic loops peppered with breakbeats and vocal samples, which took instrumental hip-hop into new realms of musical complexity."}, {"heading": "", "body": ["J Dilla produced the Pharcyde's album Labcabincalifornia in 1995, contributing to tracks like 'Bullshit', 'Runnin'', and 'Splattitorium'. This work showcased his unique production style and established him as a rising star.", "His debut solo album, Welcome 2 Detroit, released in 2001, began BBE's 'Beat Generation' series. It featured tracks like 'Fuck the Police', demonstrating Dilla's growth as both a producer and rapper.", "J Dilla's final album, Donuts, released posthumously in 2006, included collaborations with artists such as Bilal and Common, further cementing his legacy."]}, {"heading": "", "body": "J Dilla was a founding member of Slum Village and the Soulquarians. He collaborated extensively with Q-Tip on projects like A Tribe Called Quest's Beats, Rhymes and Life and the Ummah collective. His work with artists such as Common, Erykah Badu, and Bilal also significantly influenced their careers."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Despite his short mainstream career, J Dilla is widely considered one of the most influential producers in hip-hop and popular music. His innovative approach to drum programming and melodic loops raised the artistic level of hip-hop production in Detroit and beyond."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Mid-1990s to early 2000s"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, instrumental hip-hop, trip hop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Labcabincalifornia (The Pharcyde), Welcome 2 Detroit, Donuts"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "BBE, MCA Records, Payday Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Loose drum programming, Akai MPC, melodic loops, sampling"}], "secs": 59.2}}}, {"subject": "quincy jones", "slug": "quincy-jones", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Quincy Jones is an American record producer, composer, and former jazz arranger and conductor. He is recognized as a production genius who has worked across genres from swing, bebop, and soul to pop and rock, spanning seven decades of his career.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Jones's craft is rooted in his early training as a jazz arranger and conductor. He studied under Nadia Boulanger in Paris to refine his musicality, which encouraged him to embrace his roots. His production style evolved from arranging for horns and guitars up to composing complex film scores and producing high-impact pop hits. His work with Michael Jackson transformed the same artist from a bubble-gum singer into a global musical phenomenon."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones produced three of Michael Jackson's most successful albums: Off the Wall (1979), Thriller (1982), and Bad (1987). Thriller is noted as the highest selling album of all time. He also produced and conducted the charity song \"We Are the World\" (1985) and produced Lesley Gore's pop hit records in the early 1960s, including \"It's My Party\"."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones's lineage is defined by his sequence of backing legends such as Billie Holiday, Ella Fitzgerald, Miles Davis, Charlie Parker, and Duke Ellington. He served as an arranging for Dizzy Gillespie and Ray Charles\u2014producing the latter's first album for Atlantic Records\u2014and acted as musical director for Frank Sinatra for three years in the mid-1960s. He also produced Chaka Khan and Donna Summer."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones is considered canonical due to his versatility across multiple disciplines: record production, film scoring (composing scores for 35 movies including The Color Purple and In the Heat of the Night), and conducting. He has received 28 Grammy Awards, a Primetime Emmy Award, and aTony Award, c\u00f9ng v\u1edbi nominations for seven Academy Awards. Time magazine named him one of the most influential jazz musicians of the 20th century."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1950s\u20132024"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Jazz (Bebop, Swing), Pop, Soul, Rock"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Off the Wall, Thriller, Bad, We Are the World"}, {"label": "Notable Film Scores", "value": "The Color Purple, In the Heat of the Night, In Cold Blood, Roots"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Jazz arranging and conducting"}], "secs": 58.0}, "ornith": {"lede": "Quincy Jones (1933\u20132024) was an American record producer, composer, and arranger whose seven-decade career bridged jazz, pop, film scoring, and television. He is best known as the production genius behind Michael Jackson's most successful albums\u2014Off the Wall (1979), Thriller (1982), and Bad (1987)\u2014and for producing the global charity single 'We Are the World' (1985).", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Jones came to prominence in the 1950s as a jazz arranger and conductor before transitioning into pop production. He studied under Nadia Boulanger in Paris, who advised him to 'deal with your roots,' grounding his sophisticated arrangements in jazz and R&B traditions. As a producer, he served as the creative architect behind Michael Jackson's metamorphosis from teen idol to global phenomenon, beginning with Off the Wall (1979). His work spanned swing through soul, bebop through hip-hop, reflecting a deep versatility across American popular music."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones produced three of Michael Jackson's most successful albums: Off the Wall (1979), which began Jackson's transformation; Thriller (1982), which became the highest-selling album of all time; and Bad (1987), whose first-week sales exceeded the rest of the Top 30 combined. In 1985, he produced and conducted 'We Are the World,' raising funds for famine victims in Ethiopia. He also produced pop hits for Lesley Gore in the early 1960s, including 'It's My Party.'"}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones backed jazz legends including Billie Holiday, Ella Fitzgerald, Duke Ellington, Count Basie (whom he called 'daddy'), Thelonius Monk, Miles Davis, and Charlie Parker. He served as musical director for Frank Sinatra in the mid-1960s and arranged for Dizzy Gillespie and Ray Charles, producing Charles's first album for Atlantic Records. His collaborations extended to Chaka Khan and Donna Summer."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones received 28 Grammy Awards, a Primetime Emmy Award, and a Tony Award, with nominations for seven Academy Awards and four Golden Globe Awards. He composed film scores for The Pawnbroker (1965), In the Heat of the Night (1967), In Cold Blood (1967), The Italian Job (1969), The Wiz (1978), and The Color Purple (1985). He won the Emmy for Roots (1977) and a Tony for producing the 2016 revival of The Color Purple. Named one of the most influential jazz musicians of the 20th century by Time, he received posthumous honors including the Academy Honorary Award in 2025."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1950s\u20132024 (seven-decade career)"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Jazz, Pop, R&B, Soul, Film Scores"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Michael Jackson's Off the Wall, Thriller, Bad; 'We Are the World'; Lesley Gore hits"}, {"label": "Awards", "value": "28 Grammy Awards, Primetime Emmy, Tony Award, Academy Honorary Award (posthumous)"}, {"label": "Signature Role", "value": "Producer and arranger for Michael Jackson; jazz arranger/conductor"}, {"label": "Film Scores", "value": "The Pawnbroker, In the Heat of the Night, In Cold Blood, The Color Purple"}, {"label": "Education", "value": "Seattle University; Berklee College of Music; studied under Nadia Boulanger in Paris"}], "secs": 16.4}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Quincy Jones is an American record producer, composer, and arranger whose career spanned seven decades, reflecting the history of American popular music. His signature style blends jazz arrangements with pop sensibilities, making him a production genius capable of guiding artists from swing through soul to hip-hop. He is best known for his work with Michael Jackson, but also for composing numerous film scores and producing major charity records.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Jones's early career established him as a jazz arranger and conductor, playing instruments like the trumpet while backing legends such as Billie Holiday, Miles Davis, Count Basie, and Duke Ellington. His compositional scope expanded greatly after studying with Nadia Boulanger in Paris, who advised him to 'deal with your roots.' He has successfully transitioned his sound across genres, from bebop and swing to soul, pop hits (like those for Lesley Gore), and even hip-hop, demonstrating versatility as a producer."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones produced three of Michael Jackson's most successful albums: *Off the Wall* (1979), *Thriller* (1982), and *Bad* (1987). He was also involved in producing the charity song \"We Are the World\" in 1985. Furthermore, he composed numerous film scores for major works including *The Pawnbroker* (1965), *In the Heat of the Night* and *In Cold Blood* (both 1967), *The Italian Job* (1969), and *The Wiz* (1978)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "His career included working with foundational jazz artists such as Count Basie, Billie Holiday, Duke Ellington, Miles Davis, and Charlie Parker. He was a musical director for Frank Sinatra in the mid-1960s. Later collaborations included producing Chaka Khan and Donna Summer, and most notably, serving as the production genius behind Michael Jackson's transformation into a global superstar."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones's career is viewed as reflecting the history of American popular music since the war. His ability to adapt his craft across diverse genres\u2014from jazz arrangements to pop hits and film scores\u2014and his involvement in major cultural moments, such as producing *Thriller* and directing the charity single \"We Are the World,\" solidify his status as a canonical figure in modern music production."}], "facts": [{"label": "Early Career Focus", "value": "Jazz arranger and conductor (1950s)"}, {"label": "Key Early Influences/Mentors", "value": "Count Basie, Duke Ellington, Billie Holiday, Miles Davis, Nadia Boulanger"}, {"label": "Signature Production Successes", "value": "Michael Jackson's *Off the Wall* (1979), *Thriller* (1982), and *Bad* (1987)"}, {"label": "Genre Versatility", "value": "Jazz, Pop, Soul, Hip-hop, Film Scoring"}, {"label": "Notable Collaborations", "value": "Frank Sinatra, Lesley Gore, Michael Jackson, Chaka Khan, Donna Summer"}], "secs": 14.7}, "mistral": {"lede": "Quincy Jones is an American record producer, composer, and musician who has had a significant impact on popular music since the 1950s. He has won numerous awards, including 28 Grammy Awards, for his work with artists such as Michael Jackson, Frank Sinatra, and Count Basie.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Quincy Jones is known for his innovative arrangements and productions that blend various musical styles. He has a signature sound that combines elements of jazz, soul, funk, and pop. His work with Michael Jackson on albums like 'Off the Wall', 'Thriller', and 'Bad' showcases his ability to create timeless, iconic tracks."}, {"heading": "", "body": "'Thriller' by Michael Jackson (1982) is considered one of Quincy Jones's most influential productions. It remains the best-selling album of all time, with over 66 million copies sold worldwide. Other notable works include 'We Are the World', a charity single produced by Jones in 1985, which raised funds for victims of famine in Ethiopia."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Quincy Jones has collaborated with numerous legendary artists throughout his career. Some key collaborators include Michael Jackson, Frank Sinatra, Count Basie, and Miles Davis. His influence can be seen in the work of many contemporary producers and musicians."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Quincy Jones is widely regarded as one of the most influential figures in popular music history. His innovative approach to production and arranging has left an indelible mark on various genres, from jazz and soul to pop and hip-hop. He continues to be celebrated for his contributions to music and culture."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1950s - present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Jazz, soul, funk, pop, hip-hop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "'Off the Wall', 'Thriller', 'Bad' by Michael Jackson; 'We Are the World'; scores for films like 'The Pawnbroker', 'In the Heat of the Night', and 'The Color Purple'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Various labels, including Motown, Columbia, and Quincy Jones Productions"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Innovative arrangements, blending of musical styles; worked with various instruments and technologies throughout his career"}], "secs": 20.8}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Quincy Jones was an American record producer, composer, and arranger known for his versatility across genres and his groundbreaking work with Michael Jackson.', 'His signature as a producer is marked by innovative arrangements, blending jazz, R&B, pop, and hip-hop to create enduring hits.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Quincy Jones was renowned for his ability to blend various genres, creating a unique sound that often featured intricate jazz arrangements and R&B rhythms. His work with Michael Jackson exemplifies this, as he transformed Jackson's pop image into a more sophisticated musical presence through complex compositions and orchestral elements."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Three of the most successful albums by Michael Jackson\u2014Off the Wall (1979), Thriller (1982), and Bad (1987)\u2014were produced by Jones. These records not only defined Jackson's career but also had a profound impact on popular music, setting new standards for production quality and artistic vision."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Jones worked with numerous legendary artists including Billie Holiday, Miles Davis, Frank Sinatra, Count Basie, and Ray Charles. His collaborations spanned decades and genres, from jazz to hip-hop, establishing him as a key figure in the evolution of American popular music."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Quincy Jones' contributions to music are vast and varied, earning him 28 Grammy Awards and numerous other accolades. His ability to innovate within different genres and his pivotal role in shaping Michael Jackson's career solidify his status as a canonical figure in the history of popular music."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1950s-2024"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Jazz, R&B, Pop, Hip-Hop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Off the Wall (1979), Thriller (1982), Bad (1987)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Various labels including Epic, Motown"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Innovative jazz arrangements, orchestral elements in pop music"}, {"label": "Awards", "value": "28 Grammy Awards, Primetime Emmy Award, Tony Award"}, {"label": "Collaborators", "value": "Michael Jackson, Billie Holiday, Miles Davis, Frank Sinatra, Count Basie, Ray Charles"}], "secs": 53.3}}}, {"subject": "pharrell williams", "slug": "pharrell-williams", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Pharrell Williams is a highly influential producer and musician who arguably invented the sound of contemporary pop and R&B. As one half of the production duo The Neptunes, he has shaped the same era's music for some of the world's biggest stars.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell's production style is characterized by hyperactive, compressed, insatiable layers of instrumentation and beats. The Neptunes' signature sound is further defined by clipped bounce beats and a dazzling melodic style. In his work with N*E*R*D, he employs subtly experimental rhythms, digital funk, and sunkissed synth-soul, sebagainya own solo work often features a whispered falsetto tone."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Neptunes' output is produced for artists such as Britney Spears, Beyonc\u00e9, and Justin Timberlake. Notable tracks include Nelly's 'Hot in Here', which was covered by Coldplay, and 'Drop It Like It's Hot'. Other significant works include the lapped-up success of 'Get Lucky', 'Blurred Lines', and 'Happy'. His work with N*E*R*D's first album, In Search of..., features tracks like 'Provider' and 'Bobby James'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell is the main man in the peerless production duo The Neptunes, consisting of himself and Chad Hugo. He is also one third of the band N*E*R*D (No-one Ever Really Dies), which includes Hugo and rapper Shay Haley. He has produced for a wide array of artists including Jay-Z, Kelis, Kelis, Usher, Snoop Dogg, Justin Timberlake, Kanye West, Nelly, Shakira, and Britney Spears. He has also collaborated with with Daft Punk on 'Get Lucky' and 'Lose Yourself To Dance'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell is described as a virtually unique figure in modern culture, almost incalculably influential upon the world of music. His impact is documented by the 2003 survey showing The Neptunes produced nearly 20% of tracks played on British radio and 43% of songs played on American radio."}, {"heading": "", "body": "// a bit more on his other ventures such as the Billionaire's Boys Club clothing company, Ice Cream sneakers, and perfume G I R L/Comme Des Gar\u00e7on. He also composed the McDonald's 'I'm Lovin' It' jingle."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Early 2000s to present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Pop, R&B, Hip-Hop, Digital Funk, Synth-Soul"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Get Lucky, Happy, Hot in Here, Drop It Like It's Hot"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Star Trak"}, {"label": "// a bit more on his label same as the ", "value": ""}], "secs": 60.5}, "ornith": {"lede": "Pharrell Williams is a Virginia Beach-born producer who, alongside Chad Hugo as The Neptunes, defined the sonic identity of early-2000s pop and R&B with hyperactive, compressed instrumentation and signature clipped bounce beats. He is credited with inventing the sound of contemporary pop, producing nearly 43% of songs played on American radio at his peak.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The Neptunes' production style is characterized by 'signature clipped bounce beats and dazzling melodic style,' creating a sound described as 'hyperactive, compressed, insatiable layers of instrumentation.' This approach defined the music of Jay-Z, Beyonc\u00e9, Kelis, Usher, Snoop Dogg, and Justin Timberlake. Pharrell's production aesthetic is so distinct that he can play a selection of songs given to other artists\u2014such as 'Hot In Herre,' 'Drop It Like It's Hot,' and 'Hollaback Girl'\u2014and they remain 'unmistakably Pharrell.'"}, {"heading": "", "body": "Key productions include the 2003 debut solo single 'Frontin',' which launched his standalone career, and the 2003 LP *The Neptunes Present... Clones*, featuring rap elites like Busta Rhymes, N.O.R.E., Clipse, Ludacris, and Nelly. His production dominance is highlighted by a 2003 survey finding that The Neptunes produced close to 20% of tracks on British radio and 43% in America. Later landmark singles include 'Get Lucky,' 'Blurred Lines,' and the soundtrack work for *Despicable Me*."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell met Chad Hugo at age 12 at a summer camp near Virginia Beach, forming the production duo The Neptunes. He is also one-third of N*E*R*D (No-one Ever Really Dies), alongside Hugo and rapper Shay Haley; their album *In Search of...* (2001) featured 'subtly experimental rhythms and sunkissed synth-soul.' His collaborators span Jay-Z, Beyonc\u00e9, Justin Timberlake, Snoop Dogg, Kelis, Usher, Britney Spears, Kanye West, Nelly, Shakira, and Daft Punk. He also co-wrote McDonald's 'I'm Lovin' It' jingle."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell is described as a 'virtually unique figure in modern culture' who arguably 'invented the sound of contemporary pop and R&B.' His influence extended beyond music into fashion through Billionaire's Boys Club and collaborations with brands like Adidas, Timberland, and Comme Des Gar\u00e7ons. He founded Star Trak record label and maintained a presence as both a producer and solo artist, proving himself as a 'standalone solo artist' rather than just a featured collaborator."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Active since late 1990s; peak production dominance early 2000s"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Pop, R&B, Hip-Hop, Funk, Rock (via N*E*R*D)"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "'Frontin'', 'Drop It Like It's Hot', 'Hot In Herre', 'Hollaback Girl', 'Get Lucky', 'Blurred Lines'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Star Trak Entertainment, The Neptunes"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Clipped bounce beats, hyperactive compressed instrumentation, layered production"}, {"label": "Key Collaborators", "value": "Chad Hugo (The Neptunes), Shay Haley (N*E*R*D), Jay-Z, Beyonc\u00e9, Justin Timberlake, Snoop Dogg"}], "secs": 16.3}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Pharrell Williams is a highly influential figure credited with inventing the sound of contemporary pop and R&B. He operates as both a solo artist and a core member of the production unit The Neptunes, known for his signature ability to craft hyperactive, compressed layers of instrumentation and beats.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell's sound is defined by its 'clipped bounce beats' and dazzling melodic style. As a producer with The Neptunes (alongside Chad Hugo), he created hits characterized by hyperactive, compressed layers of instrumentation and beats that define modern pop and R&B. His solo work showcases a blend of genres, ranging from louche, lazily stoned funk ('The Man') to gospel-pop ('Happy'), while also incorporating elements like the Isaac Hayes/Blaxploitation groove ('Help Me')."}, {"heading": "", "body": "While his career includes multiple successful singles and albums (e.g., 'Get Lucky,' 'Blurred Lines,' 'Happy'), his early success was built through The Neptunes, which produced hits for major artists including Britney Spears, Beyonc\u00e9, Justin Timberlake, Jay-Z, Kelis, and Snoop Dogg. His 2003 debut solo single, 'Frontin',' marked an early milestone, followed by the N*E*R*D album *In Search of...* (2001)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell is most famous for his partnership with Chad Hugo in The Neptunes. He has collaborated extensively with global superstars such as Beyonc\u00e9, Justin Timberlake, Jay-Z, Kelis, and Snoop Dogg. He also fronts the group N*E*R*D (No-one Ever Really Dies) alongside Chad Hugo and Shay Haley. Furthermore, he is noted for his fashion lines and collaborations, including a sneaker design with renowned Japanese artist Nigo."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell Williams is considered canonical due to his immense influence on the music industry; sources state that The Neptunes had produced close to 20% of tracks played on UK radio in 2003, and up to 43% in America. He has demonstrated an ability to evolve from a celebrated producer role into a standalone solo artist, proving himself as 'Pharrell Williams' rather than merely 'feat Pharrell.' His career is marked by both commercial success (e.g., the McDonald's 'I'm Lovin' It' jingle) and artistic depth, such as his work with N*E*R*D."}], "facts": [{"label": "Signature Sound", "value": "Hyperactive, compressed layers of instrumentation and beats; clipped bounce beats."}, {"label": "Primary Production Unit", "value": "The Neptunes (with Chad Hugo)."}, {"label": "Key Collaborators/Groups", "value": "N*E*R*D (No-one Ever Really Dies); Beyonc\u00e9, Justin Timberlake, Jay-Z."}, {"label": "Notable Early Success", "value": "The Neptunes produced hits for artists like Britney Spears and Snoop Dogg; 2003 survey found The Neptunes produced nearly 20% of UK radio tracks."}, {"label": "Fashion/Design Focus", "value": "Designed sneakers with Nigo (Ice Cream); known for his distinctive style, including green New Era caps and Ray-Ban aviator shades."}], "secs": 15.3}, "mistral": {"lede": "Pharrell Williams is a prolific music producer and artist known for his distinctive sound and collaborations with prominent musicians. He co-founded The Neptunes production duo with Chad Hugo, creating hits for artists like Britney Spears, Jay-Z, and Justin Timberlake.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell's signature sound is characterized by compressed, hyperactive beats and layered instrumentation. He often incorporates elements of funk, soul, R&B, and hip-hop into his productions."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Some of Pharrell's most notable works include 'Frontin'' (2003), a solo debut that showcased his unique style, and 'Happy' (2014), which became an international phenomenon. His collaborations with Daft Punk on 'Get Lucky' (2013) and 'Lose Yourself to Dance' (2013) also garnered critical acclaim."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell has collaborated extensively with artists such as Jay-Z, Beyonc\u00e9, Kelis, Usher, Snoop Dogg, and Justin Timberlake. He is also a member of the band N.E.R.D."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell's influence on contemporary pop and R&B is undeniable. His production work has shaped the sound of numerous artists, and his solo projects have earned him critical acclaim and commercial success."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Late 1990s - Present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "R&B, Hip-Hop, Pop, Funk, Soul"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "'Frontin'' (2003), 'Get Lucky' (2013), 'Happy' (2014)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Star Trak, Interscope Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Compressed beats, layered instrumentation, collaborations with Chad Hugo as The Neptunes"}], "secs": 18.7}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Pharrell Williams is a pivotal figure in contemporary pop and R&B production, known for his hyperactive, compressed beats and melodic layers that have defined the sound of numerous top artists.', \"His signature as a producer is evident in hits like 'Blurred Lines' and 'Happy,' which showcase his ability to blend funk, hip-hop, and electronic elements into universally appealing tracks.\"]", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell Williams is renowned for his innovative production style that combines intricate layers of instrumentation with compressed beats. His work often features a blend of funk, hip-hop, and electronic elements, creating a sound that is both danceable and melodic. This signature approach has been influential in shaping the contemporary pop and R&B landscape."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell's production credits include 'Blurred Lines' by Robin Thicke featuring T.I. & Pharrell (2013), which became a global hit; 'Happy' from the Despicable Me soundtrack (2014), which topped charts worldwide; and 'Get Lucky' with Daft Punk (2013), another massive success that exemplifies his ability to create crossover hits."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell's most notable collaborations are with Chad Hugo, forming The Neptunes, a production duo responsible for numerous hit tracks. He has also worked extensively with artists like Justin Timberlake, Beyonc\u00e9, and Jay-Z. His solo work includes the album 'G I R L' (2013), which showcases his growth as an artist beyond production."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Pharrell Williams is considered canonical in music due to his immense influence on contemporary pop and R&B. His production style has been adopted by numerous artists, and he has produced hits that have defined eras of popular music. Additionally, his work with The Neptunes has had a significant impact on the industry, contributing to the success of many top artists."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Contemporary pop and R&B"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Funk, hip-hop, electronic music"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "'Blurred Lines', 'Happy', 'Get Lucky'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Star Trak, Atlantic Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Layered instrumentation with compressed beats"}, {"label": "Collaborators", "value": "Chad Hugo (The Neptunes), Justin Timberlake, Beyonc\u00e9, Jay-Z"}, {"label": "Solo Album", "value": "'G I R L' (2013)"}, {"label": "Film Soundtracks", "value": "Despicable Me series"}], "secs": 54.4}}}, {"subject": "timbaland", "slug": "timbaland", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Timbaland is an American record producer, rapper, singer and songwriter known for his distinctive production work and \"stuttering\" rhythmic style. He is credited with influencing pop trends, from urban-edged R&B to the incorporating of avant-garde sounds into No. 1 hits.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's trademark sound is rooted in hip-hop with fast-paced nature and clear drum breaks, which he applied to R&B to blur the distinction between the two genres. He is acclaimed for a \"stuttering\" rhythmic style and an electronic production style. His work often incorporates avant-garde sounds and samples from the sub-continent, as seen in tracks like \"Indian Flute.\""}, {"heading": "", "body": "His mainstream breakthrough occurred in 1996 with Ginuwine's debut album Ginuwine...the Bachelor (featuring the hit \"Pony\") and Aaliyah's second album One in a Million (featuring \"If Your Girl Only Knew\"). In 1997, he fully produced Missy Elliott's debut Supa Dupa Fly. He also produced Justin Timberlake's 'Cry Me a River' (2002) and the majority of Timberlake's subsequent releases, including FutureSex/LoveSounds (2006) and The 20/20 Experience (2013)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland began a long-term collaboration with rapper Magoo, forming the duo Timbaland & Magoo. He was part of the production ensemble S.B.I. (Surrounded By Idiots), which included his cousin Pharrell Williams. He also worked extensively with Missy Elliott and DeVante Swing, who renamed him 'Timbaland' after the Timberland boot.\u0e04\u0e19\u0e17\u0e35\u0e48\u0e04\u0e19 a long-term collaboration with rapper Magoo. His production credits expanded to include artists such as Jay-Z, Beyonc\u00e9, Beyonc\u00e9, Madonna, Rihanna, and Michael Jackson."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Entertainment Weekly in 2007 stated that nearly every current pop trend can be traced back to him. He is described as the world's pre-eminent rap music producer and the most in-demand music producer in the world by 2006."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1990s to present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "R&B, Hip-Hop, Pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "One in a Million (Aaliyah), Supa Dupa Fly (Missy Elliott), FutureSex/LoveSounds (Justin Timberlake)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Beat Club, Mosley Music Group"}, {"label": "Signature Method", "value": "\"Stuttering\" rhythmic style and avant-garde sounds"}], "secs": 87.0}, "ornith": {"lede": "Timbaland (born Timothy Zachery Mosley) is an American record producer and rapper widely acclaimed for his distinctive production work and 'stuttering' rhythmic style. He is credited with blurring the distinction between hip-hop and R&B production by applying a fast-paced, clear-drum-break hip-hop sound to R&B artists. His influence on pop trends was such that Entertainment Weekly stated in 2007 that 'just about every current pop trend can be traced back to him.'", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's trademark sound is rooted in hip-hop, characterized by a fast-paced nature and clear drum breaks. He took this hip-hop foundation and applied it to R&B, effectively blurring the distinction between the two genres' production styles. His work is noted for incorporating 'avant-garde sounds into No. 1 hits.' In his later work with Missy Elliott (specifically *Under Construction*), he attempted to rebuild hip-hop production from the ground up by covering other people's songs and re-contextualizing sonic motifs, though critics noted this sometimes resulted in a formulaic approach."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's first major production credit was Ginuwine's *Ginuwine... the Bachelor* (1996). His mainstream breakthrough came in 1996 with the majority of Aaliyah's *One in a Million*, featuring hits like 'If Your Girl Only Knew' and 'Pony.' In 1997, he fully produced Missy Elliott's debut *Supa Dupa Fly*, continuing his electronic production style. He scored a major hit in 1999 with Jay-Z and UGK's 'Big Pimpin''. His second album, *Shock Value* (2007), featured the #1 single 'Give It To Me' and the top-3 hit 'The Way I Are.' He produced Justin Timberlake's 'Cry Me a River' (2002) and subsequently the majority of Timberlake's releases, including *FutureSex/LoveSounds* (2006)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland began his career in the Swing Mob ensemble alongside DeVante Swing, Missy Elliott, and Magoo. He was a member of the production group S.B.I. (Surrounded By Idiots) with Pharrell Williams. His long-term partner is rapper Melvin 'Magoo' Barcliff, with whom he formed the duo Timbaland & Magoo in 1989; they released three albums on Blackground Records. He founded Beat Club in 2001 and Mosley Music Group in 2006, signing artists such as Nelly Furtado, OneRepublic, Keri Hilson, Bubba Sparxxx, and Chris Cornell."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland is widely acclaimed for his distinctive production work. His influence on pop trends was highlighted by Entertainment Weekly in 2007, which noted that trends ranging from 'sultry, urban-edged R&B songstresses' to the incorporation of avant-garde sounds into No. 1 hits could be traced back to him. He has won four Grammy Awards from 22 nominations. Along with Swizz Beatz, he co-created the webcast series Verzuz in 2020 and launched Beat Club, a mentorship service for upcoming producers."}], "facts": [{"label": "Born", "value": "March 10, 1972 (Norfolk, Virginia)"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, R&B"}, {"label": "Labels Founded", "value": "Beat Club (2001), Mosley Music Group (2006)"}, {"label": "Grammy Awards", "value": "4 wins from 22 nominations"}, {"label": "Key Collaborators", "value": "Missy Elliott, Magoo, Aaliyah, Ginuwine, Justin Timberlake"}, {"label": "Signature Style", "value": "Stuttering rhythmic style; avant-garde sounds in pop hits"}], "secs": 18.5}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Timbaland (Timothy Zachery Mosley) is an American record producer, rapper, singer, and songwriter widely acclaimed for his distinctive production work and 'stuttering' rhythmic style. His sound has been instrumental in blurring the distinction between hip-hop and R&B production, influencing pop trends from sultry urban-edged R&B to avant-garde sounds.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland is known for his distinctive 'stuttering' rhythmic style. His trademark electronic production style was evident early on and continued throughout his career, making him a key figure in blurring the lines between hip-hop and R&B production. He has been noted for incorporating avant-garde sounds into mainstream hits."}, {"heading": "", "body": "His commercial breakthrough came with producing Aaliyah's album *One in a Million* (1996) and Ginuwine's debut album *Ginuwine... the Bachelor* (1996), which included major hits like \"If Your Girl Only Knew\" by Aaliyah and \"Pony\" by Ginuwine. Later, he produced Justin Timberlake's subsequent releases, including *FutureSex/LoveSounds* (2006) and *The 20/20 Experience* (2013). His work on Missy Elliott's album *Supa Dupa Fly* (1997) further established his electronic production sound in the hip-hop genre."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland has collaborated extensively with major artists including Justin Timberlake, Missy Elliott, Jay-Z, Beyonc\u00e9, Madonna, Rihanna, and Kanye West. He formed a long-term partnership with rapper Magoo as the duo Timbaland & Magoo. Professionally, he also worked alongside fellow producer Swizz Beatz to co-create the webcast series Verzuz."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland is considered canonical due to his profound influence on pop culture and music production trends. Entertainment Weekly stated that 'just about every current pop trend can be traced back to him.' His ability to take a hip-hop sound and apply it successfully to R&B, while maintaining an innovative electronic edge, cemented his status as a highly influential producer."}], "facts": [{"label": "Signature Style", "value": "Distinctive 'stuttering' rhythmic style; blending hip-hop and R&B production."}, {"label": "Early Breakthrough Albums", "value": "*Ginuwine... the Bachelor* (1996) and *One in a Million* (1996)."}, {"label": "Key Collaborations", "value": "Missy Elliott, Justin Timberlake, Jay-Z, Beyonc\u00e9, Madonna, Rihanna."}, {"label": "Duo Partnership", "value": "Timbaland & Magoo (formed 1989); released *Welcome to Our World* (1997)."}, {"label": "Labels Founded", "value": "Beat Club (2001) and Mosley Music Group (2006)."}, {"label": "Notable Hits Produced", "value": "\"Pony\" by Ginuwine; \"If Your Girl Only Knew\" by Aaliyah; \"Cry Me a River\" by Justin Timberlake."}], "secs": 16.7}, "mistral": {"lede": "Timbaland, born Timothy Zachery Mosley, is an American record producer, rapper, singer, and songwriter from Norfolk, Virginia. He is widely acclaimed for his distinctive production work and 'stuttering' rhythmic style, with Entertainment Weekly stating that 'just about every current pop trend can be traced back to him'.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's signature sound is characterized by his distinctive 'stuttering' rhythmic style, fast-paced hip-hop beats applied to R&B, and innovative use of electronic sounds. He often incorporates avant-garde sounds into pop hits."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's first major production credit was on Ginuwine's debut album 'Ginuwine... the Bachelor' (1996), which included the hit single 'Pony'. He also produced Aaliyah's second album 'One in a Million' (1996) and Missy Elliott's debut album 'Supa Dupa Fly' (1997). His production work on Justin Timberlake's albums, including 'Justified' (2002), 'FutureSex/LoveSounds' (2006), and 'The 20/20 Experience' (2013), has been highly influential in pop music."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's long-term collaborators include Missy Elliott, with whom he formed the production duo The Neptunes, and rapper Magoo. He has also worked extensively with Justin Timberlake, Aaliyah, Ginuwine, and Jay-Z."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's innovative approach to music production has had a significant impact on contemporary pop, R&B, and hip-hop. His distinctive sound has influenced numerous producers and artists, making him a canonical figure in modern music."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1990s - present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "R&B, hip-hop, pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Ginuwine's 'Ginuwine... the Bachelor' (1996), Aaliyah's 'One in a Million' (1996), Missy Elliott's 'Supa Dupa Fly' (1997), Justin Timberlake's 'Justified' (2002) and 'FutureSex/LoveSounds' (2006)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Blackground Records, Beat Club, Mosley Music Group"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "'Stuttering' rhythmic style, innovative use of electronic sounds, fast-paced hip-hop beats applied to R&B"}], "secs": 25.4}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "[\"Timbaland is an American record producer known for his distinctive production work and 'stuttering' rhythmic style.\", 'His signature sound blends hip-hop with R&B, influencing numerous pop trends.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's trademark sound is characterized by a fast-paced nature and clear drum breaks, often incorporating avant-garde sounds. He blends hip-hop with R&B, creating a unique rhythmic style that has influenced many pop trends."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland produced Aaliyah's album One in a Million (1996) and Missy Elliott's Supa Dupa Fly (1997), which were pivotal in establishing his signature sound. His production of Justin Timberlake's FutureSex/LoveSounds (2006) and The 20/20 Experience (2013) further cemented his influence, with hits like 'Cry Me a River', 'The Way I Are', and 'Apologize'."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland has worked extensively with artists such as Missy Elliott, Justin Timberlake, Aaliyah, and Jay-Z. His early collaborations include those with his childhood friend Missy Elliott and long-term partner Magoo Barcliff. He is part of the S.B.I. production ensemble, which also includes his cousin Pharrell Williams."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Timbaland's innovative approach to blending hip-hop with R&B has significantly influenced contemporary pop music. His ability to incorporate avant-garde sounds into mainstream hits and his prolific production work for numerous high-profile artists have solidified his status as a canonical figure in the industry."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1990s\u2013present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "R&B, hip-hop, pop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "Aaliyah's One in a Million (1996), Missy Elliott's Supa Dupa Fly (1997), Justin Timberlake's FutureSex/LoveSounds (2006)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Beat Club, Mosley Music Group"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "'Stuttering' rhythmic style, use of avant-garde sounds, electronic production techniques"}], "secs": 59.4}}}, {"subject": "dr dre", "slug": "dr-dre", "lens": "craft", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Dr. Dre is an American rapper, record producer, and record executive who co-founded Death Row Records and founded Aftermath Entertainment. He is recognized as a key figure in the crafting and popularization of West Coast G-funk, a subgenre of hip-hop characterized by slow, heavy production and a synthesizer foundation.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre's production style evolved from the generic recordings of his early career with World Class Wreckin' Cru to the development of G-funk. In his 1999 album 2001, he expanded on this sound, utilizing sparse beats and reduced use of samples compared to his debut solo effort. His recording process involves starting with a drum machine in a room full of trusted musicians who are carry beepers and work when he wants them to; Dre programs the beat and then asks musicians to play along, isolating and refining the sound based on what he likes. On 2001, he utilized collaborators like Scott Storch on keyboards, Mike Elizondo on bass guitar, and himself on the drum machine."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre's career is landmark records include The Chronic (1992), which was one of the best-selling American music artists of 1993 and featured the hit single \"Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang\". N.W.A's Straight Outta Compton (1989) gave rise to the popularity of gangsta rap. Snoop Dogg's debut album Doggystyle (1993), produced by Dre, produced several hits and mentored producers like Warren G and Daz Dillinger. The album 2001 (1999) was certified 6x Platinum in the US and expanded on his G-funk sound with a cinematic approach to sequencing, treating the album as a movie with build-ups and aggressive moments."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dre's lineage includes early collaborations with DJ Yella in the World Class Wreckin' Cru and N.W.A, and later with Mel-Man as a co-producer on 2001. He mentored producers Warren G and Daz Dillinger. He signed and extensively contributed to releases by Eminem and 50 Cent through Aftermath Entertainment, and has since signed other artists such as Kendrick Lamar, Anderson .Paak, the Game, Busta Rhymes, Eve, and Rakim. He is described as a mentor to Eminem, and some of his lyrics on 2001 were penned by ghostwriters including Jay-Z and Eminem."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre is considered canonical due to his influence on the West Coast hip-hop scene and the same as the influential producers Phil Spector, Quincy Jones, and George Clinton. He has won seven Grammy Awards, including Producer of the Year, Non-Classical. Rolling Stone ranked him number 56 on the uma list of 100 Greatest Artists of All Time."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "Mid-1980s to present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Hip-hop, Gangsta rap, G-funk"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "The Chronic, 2001, Doggystyle, Straight Outta Compton"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Death Row Records, Aftermath Entertainment, Ruthless Records"}, {"label": "Signature Method", "value": "Drum machine programming followed by musician refinement"}], "secs": 82.0}, "ornith": {"lede": "Dr. Dre (Andre Young) is an American record producer and executive who defined West Coast G-funk in the early 1990s and later served as a primary architect of mainstream hip-hop's commercial expansion in the 2000s. He founded Death Row Records and Aftermath Entertainment, launching the careers of Snoop Dogg, Eminem, and 50 Cent while winning seven Grammy Awards, including Producer of the Year.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre is credited as a key figure in crafting West Coast G-funk, characterized by synthesizer foundations and slow, heavy production. His recording process involves programming beats on a drum machine surrounded by trusted musicians; he isolates players to refine their sounds. On his second album, 2001 (1999), Dre expanded on his debut sound with new, sparse beats and reduced sampling, utilizing collaborators like Scott Storch for piano and orchestration while maintaining the core 'drum machine + live band' approach."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The Chronic (1992) was Death Row's first major project, making Dre one of the best-selling artists of 1993 and featuring the hit 'Nuthin' but a 'G Thang.' N.W.A's Straight Outta Compton (1989) popularized explicit lyrics detailing street violence. 2001 (1999) debuted at number two on the Billboard 200 with 516,000 first-week sales and was certified 6x Platinum; Dre described it as a 'movie' with varied situations rather than club or radio play. Compton (2015) served as the soundtrack to the N.W.A biographical film."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dre mentored producers including his stepbrother Warren G and Snoop Dogg's cousin Daz Dillinger. He signed Eminem in 1998 and 50 Cent in 2002, extensively contributing to their releases. Aftermath Entertainment has since signed the Game, Kendrick Lamar, Anderson .Paak, Silk Sonic, Busta Rhymes, Eve, and Rakim. His early career involved the World Class Wreckin' Cru (1984) and N.W.A (formed 1987 with Eazy-E, Ice Cube, and Arabian Prince)."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Rolling Stone ranked Dre number 56 on the list of 100 Greatest Artists of All Time. He won seven Grammy Awards, including Producer of the Year, Non-Classical. N.W.A's Straight Outta Compton is often credited for the rise in popularity of gangsta rap. Dre is described as a 'Phil Spector' and 'Quincy Jones' figure for his role in shaping the future of hip-hop through production and artist development."}], "facts": [{"label": "Born", "value": "February 18, 1965"}, {"label": "Labels Founded", "value": "Death Row Records, Aftermath Entertainment"}, {"label": "Key Genres", "value": "G-funk, Gangsta Rap"}, {"label": "Grammy Awards", "value": "7 (including Producer of the Year)"}, {"label": "Notable Artists Launched", "value": "Snoop Dogg, Eminem, 50 Cent"}, {"label": "Signature Album", "value": "The Chronic (1992)"}], "secs": 29.0}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Dr. Dre is an American rapper, record producer, and executive known for shaping the sound of West Coast hip-hop. His signature production style involves foundational beats, often characterized by a synthesizer foundation and slow, heavy rhythm (G-funk). He has been credited with mentoring and promoting numerous artists who have defined modern rap.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dre was key in the popularization of West Coast G-funk during the early 1990s, a subgenre characterized by a synthesizer foundation and slow, heavy production. His recording process often begins with him programming a beat on a drum machine, followed by asking trusted musicians to play along, which allows him to isolate players and refine sounds. On later albums, his production expanded on earlier work by incorporating new, sparse beats and reducing the prominent use of samples."}, {"heading": "", "body": "N.W.A's debut album, Straight Outta Compton (1989), was instrumental in popularizing gangsta rap within the West Coast hip-hop scene. Dre\u2019s solo debut, The Chronic (1992), established him as a best-selling artist and featured hits like \"Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang.\" Following his departure from Death Row Records, he released Dr. Dre Presents: The Aftermath (1996) and the album 2001 (1999). Later, Compton (2015) served as the soundtrack to the N.W.A biographical film Straight Outta Compton."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dre formed N.W.A in 1987 with Eazy-E, Ice Cube, and Arabian Prince. He produced Snoop Dogg's debut album, Doggystyle (1993), and mentored producers like his stepbrother Warren G and Snoop Dogg's cousin Daz Dillinger. After establishing Aftermath Entertainment, he signed and extensively contributed to releases by artists including Eminem (since 1998) and 50 Cent (2002). He is described as a mentor figure who has shaped the future of gangsta rap."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dre's career trajectory\u2014from founding N.W.A to pioneering G-funk, establishing his own label Aftermath Entertainment, and promoting subsequent generations of artists like Eminem\u2014solidifies his canonical status. He is recognized as a foundational figure who not only created a genre but also built the infrastructure (Aftermath) that continues to define modern rap."}], "facts": [{"label": "Early Career", "value": "Began in World Class Wreckin' Cru (1984) and N.W.A (1987-1991)."}, {"label": "Signature Genre/Sound", "value": "West Coast G-funk, characterized by a synthesizer foundation and slow, heavy production."}, {"label": "Key Albums", "value": "The Chronic (1992), Doggystyle (1993), 2001 (1999), Compton (2015)."}, {"label": "Labels/Companies", "value": "Founder and CEO of Aftermath Entertainment; co-founder of Death Row Records."}, {"label": "Notable Collaborations", "value": "Snoop Dogg, Eminem, Ice Cube, 50 Cent, Kendrick Lamar."}], "secs": 18.6}, "mistral": {"lede": "Dr. Dre, born Andre Romell Young in 1965, is an American rapper, record producer, and CEO of Aftermath Entertainment and Beats Electronics. He began his career with World Class Wreckin' Cru in the mid-80s before co-founding N.W.A in 1987, popularizing gangsta rap with their debut album 'Straight Outta Compton'. Dre's signature G-funk sound debuted on his solo album 'The Chronic', and he has since produced for artists like Snoop Dogg, Eminem, and Kendrick Lamar.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre is known for his signature G-funk sound, characterized by a synthesizer foundation and slow, heavy production. He expanded on this style with his second album '2001', incorporating sparse beats and reduced use of samples. His production process involves programming a beat, then asking musicians to play along, refining the sound based on what he likes."}, {"heading": "", "body": "'Straight Outta Compton' (N.W.A, 1989) was one of the most successful albums in West Coast hip-hop and is credited for popularizing gangsta rap. Dre's solo debut 'The Chronic' (1992) made him one of the best-selling American music artists of 1993, with its lead single 'Nuthin\u2019 but a \u2018G\u2019 Thang\u2019 peaking at number two on the Billboard Hot 100."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dre co-founded N.W.A with Eazy-E and Ice Cube, producing their debut album 'Straight Outta Compton'. He later produced Snoop Dogg's debut album 'Doggystyle' (1993) and mentored producers like Warren G ('Regulate... G Funk Era', 1994) and Daz Dillinger ('Dogg Food', 1995). After leaving Death Row Records, Dre founded Aftermath Entertainment, signing artists such as Eminem, 50 Cent, Kendrick Lamar, and Anderson .Paak."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre is considered canonical in hip-hop production due to his influence on the G-funk subgenre, his work with N.W.A, and his contributions to the careers of numerous successful artists. He has won seven Grammy Awards, including Producer of the Year, Non-Classical."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1980s - present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "G-funk, gangsta rap, hip-hop"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "'Straight Outta Compton' (N.W.A), 'The Chronic', 'Doggystyle' (Snoop Dogg), '2001'"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Death Row Records, Aftermath Entertainment"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Akai MPC60 sampler, Roland TR-808 and TR-909 drum machines, Pro Tools"}], "secs": 30.5}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Dr. Dre is an influential American rapper and producer known for his pioneering work in West Coast G-funk and gangsta rap.', 'His signature sound blends heavy synthesizers, slow tempos, and gritty lyrics detailing urban life.']", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre's production is characterized by its use of synthesizers, deep basslines, and a slow, heavy tempo that defines West Coast G-funk. His beats often feature sparse instrumentation and reduced sampling compared to his earlier work, allowing for more focus on the rhythm and melody."}, {"heading": "", "body": "N.W.A's debut album 'Straight Outta Compton' (1989) is pivotal, popularizing gangsta rap. Dr. Dre's solo debut, 'The Chronic' (1992), solidified his status with hits like 'Nuthin' but a 'G' Thang.' His second studio album, '2001' (1999), further expanded his sound and featured collaborations with Eminem, Snoop Dogg, and others."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre has mentored and produced numerous artists including Snoop Dogg, Warren G, Eminem, 50 Cent, Kendrick Lamar, and Silk Sonic. His influence extends through his label Aftermath Entertainment, which continues to produce critically acclaimed music."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Dr. Dre's innovative production techniques and raw, unfiltered lyrics have shaped the landscape of hip-hop. Albums like 'The Chronic' and '2001,' along with his work with N.W.A, have cemented his legacy as a foundational figure in gangsta rap and G-funk."}], "facts": [{"label": "Era", "value": "1980s\u2013Present"}, {"label": "Genres", "value": "Gangsta Rap, West Coast G-Funk"}, {"label": "Notable Productions", "value": "'The Chronic' (1992), '2001' (1999), 'Compton' (2015)"}, {"label": "Labels", "value": "Death Row Records, Aftermath Entertainment"}, {"label": "Signature Method/Gear", "value": "Synthesizers, drum machines, sparse beats"}], "secs": 67.6}}}, {"subject": "lavenue", "slug": "lavenue", "lens": "encyclopedia", "models": {"gemma": {"lede": "Lavenue refers to the cookery school founded by Felix Lavenue, a pseudonym for Englishman Charles Shepperd. The institution was located at 67 Mortimer Street.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The Lavenue cookery school was established by Charles Shepperd, who used the name Felix Lavenue. Shepperd died in 1864, after which the school was run by his daughter, Agnes Mary Lavenue, who died a few months after selling the business in January 1883. In January 1883, the school was acquired by Agnes Bertha Marshall and her husband Alfred William Marshall. Following the purchase, the Marshalls renamed the institution as the Marshall's School of Cookery."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Shortly after the acquisition in 1883, the school was moved from its original location at 67 Mortimer Street to 30 Mortimer St because the building at 67 Mortimer Street was scheduled for demolition."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founder", "value": "Felix Lavenue (Charles Shepperd)"}, {"label": "Original Location", "value": "67 Mortimer Street"}, {"label": "Date of Acquisition by the Marshalls", "value": "January 1883"}, {"label": "label own's daughter", "value": "Agnes Mary Lavenue"}, {"label": "New Name after Acquisition", "value": "Marshall's School of Cookery"}], "secs": 42.8}, "ornith": {"lede": "Lavenue was a cookery school located at 67 Mortimer Street in Marylebone, London. It was founded by Felix Lavenue, the pseudonym of Englishman Charles Shepperd, who died in 1864.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "After Felix Lavenue's death in 1864, the school was run by his daughter, Agnes Mary Lavenue. The Marylebone rate books show that 67 Mortimer Street was scheduled for demolition in 1883. A few months after selling the school, Agnes Mary Lavenue died in Tunbridge Wells."}, {"heading": "", "body": "In January 1883, Agnes and Alfred Marshall acquired the Lavenue cookery school. Although the original records of the transaction have not survived, later evidence suggests that the couple purchased the school together. This was unusual at the time, as women had only very recently earned the legal right to purchase property through the Married Women's Property Act 1882."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Soon after the purchase, the Marshalls moved the school down the road to 30 Mortimer Street and renamed it Marshall's School of Cookery. Agnes's half-brother John was employed as the school's manager, and her half-sister Ada worked for a time as the housekeeper."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founder", "value": "Felix Lavenue (pseudonym of Charles Shepperd)"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "67 Mortimer Street, Marylebone"}, {"label": "Year sold", "value": "1883"}, {"label": "Buyers", "value": "Agnes and Alfred Marshall"}, {"label": "New name", "value": "Marshall's School of Cookery"}], "secs": 21.4}, "gemma-small": {"lede": "Lavenue was the name of a cookery school that Agnes Marshall and her husband acquired in 1883. The original school, which was situated at 67 Mortimer Street, was founded by Felix Lavenue, who used this name as a pseudonym for Englishman Charles Shepperd. In 1883, the couple purchased the school from his daughter, Agnes Mary Lavenue.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The Marshall's School of Cookery, which operated at 67 Mortimer Street and later moved to 30 Mortimer St., was a major culinary institution. It taught a mixture of high-end English and French cuisine and became one of only two major cookery schools in the city, alongside The National Training School Of Cookery. Agnes Marshall's husband, Alfred, also operated other businesses through the school, including an agency that supplied domestic staff, and a food shop selling flavorings, spices, and syrups."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Agnes Bertha Marshall acquired the Lavenue cookery school in January 1883. The original founder was Felix Lavenue, who used this name as a pseudonym for Charles Shepperd, and he died in 1864. In 1883, the school was being run by his daughter, Agnes Mary Lavenue. Following the purchase, the Marshalls moved the school down the road to 30 Mortimer St. The couple purchased the school together, which was notable as women had only recently gained the legal right to purchase property through the Married Women's Property Act 1882."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Marshall published her first book, *The Book of Ices*, in 1885. This publication contained 177 different ice cream and dessert recipes and was self-published through the cookery school. The book also promoted Marshall's inventions, including the Marshall's Patent Freezer. Other innovations included an \"ice-breaking machine,\" an \"ice cave\" (an insulated box for storing ice cream), and several kitchen appliances and food ingredients."}], "facts": [{"label": "Original Founder", "value": "Felix Lavenue (pseudonym of Charles Shepperd)"}, {"label": "Acquisition Date", "value": "January 1883"}, {"label": "Initial Location", "value": "67 Mortimer Street"}, {"label": "New Location", "value": "30 Mortimer St."}], "secs": 16.5}, "mistral": {"lede": "Lavenue is a historic cookery school founded by Felix Lavenue, later acquired and renamed by Agnes Marshall, a prominent English culinary entrepreneur known for her work on ice cream.", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "The Lavenue cookery school was founded by Charles Shepperd under the pseudonym Felix Lavenue. Agnes Marshall and her husband Alfred acquired the school in 1883, renaming it the Marshall's School of Cookery. It taught a mixture of high-end English and French cuisine and became one of two major cookery schools in London."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Agnes Bertha Marshall (born Agnes Beere Smith) was an English culinary entrepreneur, inventor, and celebrity chef. She popularised ice cream in England and is often regarded as the inventor of the modern ice cream cone. Marshall wrote four cookbooks, including two devoted to ice cream and other desserts."}], "facts": [{"label": "Founder", "value": "Charles Shepperd (as Felix Lavenue)"}, {"label": "Acquirers", "value": "Agnes Marshall and Alfred William Marshall"}, {"label": "Year acquired", "value": "1883"}, {"label": "New name", "value": "Marshall's School of Cookery"}], "secs": 15.6}, "qwen-coder": {"lede": "['Lavenue was a cookery school in London that played a significant role in the culinary education and career of Agnes Marshall, an influential English chef.', \"Marshall renamed Lavenue to the Marshall's School of Cookery after acquiring it in 1883, transforming it into one of the leading culinary schools in London.\"]", "sections": [{"heading": "", "body": "Lavenue was originally a cookery school founded by Felix Lavenue (the pseudonym of Charles Shepperd) in 1864. It was renamed Marshall's School of Cookery after Agnes Marshall and her husband Alfred acquired it in 1883. The school became renowned for teaching high-end English and French cuisine."}, {"heading": "", "body": "The original Lavenue cookery school, located at 67 Mortimer Street, was managed by Agnes Mary Lavenue until its sale in 1883. After the purchase, the Marshalls moved the school to 30 Mortimer Street and expanded its offerings. The school's curriculum included specialized classes taught by experts from various culinary backgrounds."}, {"heading": "", "body": "Under Agnes Marshall's leadership, Marshall's School of Cookery grew rapidly in popularity and became one of the most prestigious cookery schools in London. It played a crucial role in Marshall's career development and contributed to her reputation as an influential culinary entrepreneur."}], "facts": [{"label": "Original Name", "value": "Lavenue"}, {"label": "New Name", "value": "Marshall's School of Cookery"}, {"label": "Year Acquired by Marshall", "value": "1883"}, {"label": "Location", "value": "67 Mortimer Street, then moved to 30 Mortimer Street, London"}, {"label": "Founder of Lavenue", "value": "Charles Shepperd (under the pseudonym Felix Lavenue)"}, {"label": "Number of Students", "value": "Up to 40 students per class, with nearly 2,000 students overall"}, {"label": "First Book Published by Marshall", "value": "The Book of Ices (1885)"}, {"label": "Notable Invention", "value": "Marshall's Patent Freezer"}], "secs": 50.6}}}]};
